---
id: org.dxos.plugin.bramble
name: BramblePlugin
version: 0.1.0
---

Bramble — a property-graph plugin where every bullet is a first-class ECHO `Node`, structural relations are typed `Edge`s, and the user
navigates the graph through zoom + panes + supertag-conditional rendering today, with a first-class **Lenses** surface planned (see `CONCEPTS.md` §8.2). Honours the Ackoffian mess of human thought (see `CONCEPTS.md` for the substrate stance, the DSRP-on-triples data model, and the naming rationale). Designed to losslessly absorb Tana Paste imports.

**Conceptual scaffolding** — Ackoff, Snowden, DSRP, naming rationale — lives in `CONCEPTS.md` (sibling file, not bound by `R-Spec-Entry-Conventions`). Cite sections from there rather than paraphrasing.

## Rules

```mdl
rule R-Edges-First-Class:
  desc: |
    Structural parent/child links between Nodes are modelled as
    first-class ECHO `Relation` entities (the `Edge` schema in
    `types/Edge.ts`) — NOT as opaque entries in
    `Node.children: Ref<Node>[]`. The relation carries `source`,
    `target`, `order`, and reserves room for per-occurrence
    `expanded` and a future `kind` taxonomy.

    Why: a tree where `Node.children` is the only parent/child
    representation forces single-parent-per-Node and offers
    nowhere to attach per-edge data (ordering across multiple
    parents, per-occurrence collapse, provenance, edge labels for
    'structural' vs 'embed' vs 'linked'). Materialising the edge as
    an entity unlocks a property-graph model on top of ECHO without
    touching `@dxos/echo` (per rule R-No-Echo-Changes).

    How to apply: new structural-children writers MUST go through
    `createEdge(db, parent, child)` from
    `components/Node/child-edges.ts`. Readers MUST use
    `useStructuralChildren(parent)` (or its sync sibling
    `getStructuralChildren`) which merges `Node.children` (legacy,
    still authoritative for outline bullets that haven't been
    ported) with Edges fanning out of the parent. Direct reads
    of `node.children` are deprecated and survive only until each
    call-site is migrated. New domain relationships beyond
    parent/child (e.g. WorksAt, AssignedTo, Wraps) follow the same
    pattern: define a `Type.relation(...)` schema in `types/`,
    register it in `BramblePlugin`, write helpers + a hook.
```

```mdl
rule R-Predecessor-Successor-Terms:
  desc: |
    New `req` / `feat` text MUST use the graph-theory terms
    PREDECESSOR (a Node with a structural edge pointing AT this one)
    and SUCCESSOR (a Node this one has a structural edge pointing
    TO) when talking about Edge relationships. The tree words
    PARENT / CHILD remain valid in two narrow contexts only:
    (a) tree-only legacy spec text written before F-DAG (e.g. the
    F-Page-Header single-parent breadcrumb prose); (b) function and
    variable names already in code (`createEdge`,
    `parentEdgesOf`, `useStructuralChildren`) until those names are
    refactored.

    Why: in a DAG a Node has a SET of predecessors, not a single
    parent. "Parent" misleads readers into assuming
    single-parenthood and obscures the multi-parent UX F-DAG enables
    (`F-DAG.Phase3e.link-via-cmd-tab`,
    `F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-*`). Predecessor/successor keeps
    the spec aligned with the underlying property-graph model and
    with the `Relation.getSource` / `Relation.getTarget` API the
    code already speaks.

    How to apply: when writing a new req or feat desc, pick the
    graph term. When you touch an older parent/child passage for
    an unrelated edit, you MAY rename in passing if the diff stays
    small; otherwise leave the legacy term — don't cross-rename
    in bulk.
```

```mdl
rule R-No-Echo-Changes:
  desc: |
    plugin-blocks is a strict consumer of `@dxos/echo` and its sibling
    packages (`@dxos/types`, `@dxos/schema`, etc.). It MUST NOT depend
    on edits to those packages, schema additions there, or any new
    ECHO API. Everything plugin-blocks needs — including Tana-style
    behaviours such as materializing enum choices as Nodes — must be
    implemented as a layer ABOVE the ECHO surface that ships today.

    Why: keeps the plugin shippable independently of core ECHO
    release cycles, prevents the outliner from accidentally
    co-evolving with the substrate, and forces design choices that
    respect ECHO's stable API as the contract.

    How to apply: when an F-* increment seems to need a schema field,
    annotation, or helper that doesn't exist in `@dxos/echo` today,
    re-shape the feature so it lives inside plugin-blocks (e.g.,
    extra Nodes acting as registries, plugin-private metadata on the
    Node's `content`, lookups keyed by typename+fieldName+value).
    Never reach for a `@dxos/types` schema edit, a new ECHO
    annotation, or a new core helper as the path of least resistance.
```

```mdl
rule R-Spec-Entry-Conventions:
  desc: |
    When authoring or reviewing `feat`, `req`, or `test`
    entries in this spec, apply these four conventions:

    1. POSITIVE LANGUAGE ONLY. Clauses describe what
       happens, not what doesn't. Defensive absence claims
       ("we don't filter X", "no error is thrown",
       "exactly N children with no others permitted") are a
       yellow flag — usually the absence is implied by a
       positive rule earlier in the entry, or by enumeration
       of the positive set. Two specific sub-cases worth
       calling out:

       1a. Don't preclude additional members of a set. Prefer
           "for every X there is a corresponding Y among
           Schema's children" over "Schema's children are
           exactly Y1..Yn and nothing else". The latter
           bakes in an exclusivity the surrounding system
           may legitimately violate.

       1b. List specific items as EXAMPLES, not as exhaustive
           enumerations. "Examples of such fields on Task
           today: Priority, Status, …; the exact set tracks
           whatever the schema currently declares" beats
           "the rendered rows are exactly Priority, Status,
           …" — the former survives schema evolution.

    2. ABSTRACT `#T` / type T form (tests especially). Tests
       use abstract `#T` with concrete typenames like
       `#Task` only as examples — e.g. "a bullet N tagged
       with supertag `#T` (e.g. `#Task`)". This keeps tests
       robust to schema evolution.

    3. REFER-DON'T-RESTATE. When a rule is canonically
       defined in a `req` or sibling `test`, reference it
       ("per F-Supertag.types-shown") instead of
       duplicating. Drift between copies is the failure mode.

    4. DIVORCE FROM SPECIFIC RENDERINGS where possible.
       Data-level invariants ("the supertag node set matches
       the allowed type set, idempotently") beat UI-event
       reproductions ("close and reopen the outliner"). The
       entry's truth lives in the database, not in a
       specific surface's mount lifecycle.

    UX and behaviour assertions share one given/when/then
    shape — every plugin spec in DXOS uses given+then
    required, when optional, prose with bullet lists. There
    is no UX-specific keyword (no `view:` / `see:` /
    screenplay format).

    Why: established 2026-05-13 during a 1-by-1 review of 22
    F-Supertag test entries. Drafts violating these were
    rejected; revisions accepted. The rule covers `feat`
    and `req` entries as well as `test` entries — the
    positive-language principle was applied to a req's
    `then:` bullet during the review (dropping
    F-Supertag.types-shown's "SystemTypeAnnotation is
    INTENTIONALLY NOT a filter" clause), so the scope is
    broader than tests alone.

    How to apply: read each clause critically. If it asserts
    an absence, ask whether a positive enumeration would
    cover the same ground. If it names a specific type
    (Task, Person), check whether `#T` with that as an
    example would work. If it restates a rule already
    declared elsewhere, replace with a reference. If a
    field/member list is hardcoded, ask whether it should
    be "examples; the exact set tracks the current schema".
```

```mdl
rule R-Greenfield-Stance:
  desc: |
    plugin-blocks is early enough that no user data exists in
    production. When a bug or design flaw can be fixed at the
    root for newly-created (greenfield) spaces, fix it there
    and accept that existing test-space artifacts may be
    inconsistent. Do NOT invest in migration code,
    backwards-compatibility shims, or one-off normalisation
    passes whose only purpose is to repair test-space data
    written by an earlier implementation.

    Why: established 2026-05-13. The concurrent-mount race in
    `findOrCreateSchemaBlock` / `findOrCreateLibraryBlock`
    produced duplicate Schema and Library system Nodes in the
    user's test space. The right fix is to make those helpers
    race-safe so a greenfield instance never produces dupes;
    the existing dupes in the user's space are accepted as
    test-data noise rather than worked around. Generalising
    that decision: pre-launch, root-cause fixes are cheap and
    correct; defensive backwards-compat is dead weight that
    will outlive its purpose.

    How to apply: when you discover state in the user's test
    space that disagrees with current code's invariants, do
    NOT add cleanup code unless the same bug could recur on a
    fresh space. Instead, identify and fix the underlying
    write path so new spaces are correct, and flag the stale
    test-space state to the user as a separate concern (they
    can wipe & re-seed if it bothers them). This rule is
    plugin-blocks-specific and expires when the plugin ships
    to real users — at that point, real data migration becomes
    a first-class concern.
```

```mdl
rule R-Step-Is-Tagged-Node:
  desc: |
    A "Step" (per substrate-principles; see CONCEPTS.md §12.3) is
    a Bramble.Node carrying the `#Step` supertag. NOT a separate
    ECHO type; NOT a sub-struct on `Node.state` (which is
    reserved for transient UI state like `expanded` and
    `checked`). The Step supertag's instance Schema starts empty
    and grows as demand pulls fields in.

    Why: CONCEPTS.md §12.3. Bramble has one kind of thing
    (the Node), and F-Supertag is the established
    typing-as-X mechanism (see R-Edges-First-Class for the
    structural-relations counterpart). Putting semantic typing
    into a different slot — a `state.step` sub-struct, or a
    parallel non-Node ECHO type — violates "one kind of thing,
    recursively composed" (substrate-principles, Key terms).

    How to apply: new substrate-vocabulary primitives that name
    a "kind of work-unit" or "kind of executor" attach to Nodes
    via supertag. Spec entries that propose `Node.state.<X>`
    for typing-like data, or that propose a parallel non-Node
    ECHO type for a Step-shaped concept, MUST cite §12.3 and
    justify the deviation.
```

```mdl
rule R-Run-Is-Tagged-Node-Linked-Via-Edge:
  desc: |
    A "Run" (per substrate-principles; see CONCEPTS.md §12.4) is
    a Bramble.Node carrying the `#Run` supertag. Its relationship
    to its Step is an Edge of `kind: 'is-run-of'`. Its
    relationship to its parent Run (if any) is an Edge of
    `kind: 'parent-run'`.

    Why: CONCEPTS.md §12.4. Edges give bidirectional traversal
    cheaply (list-runs-of-a-step vs. step-of-a-run). The Run's
    supertag-instance Schema starts empty; what fields it gains
    emerges from iteration on concrete scenarios.

    How to apply: do not add `Run.of` or `Run.parent` as Refs on
    the supertag instance — use the edges. Do not add an event-
    sourcing layer outside what ECHO provides natively
    (Principle #15). When implementing UX that creates these
    edges, see F-Edge-Creation-Gesture (the new-edge gesture is
    a downstream concern and is intentionally not yet
    user-exposed in iteration 2b).
```

```mdl
rule R-Substrate-Types-Are-Plugin-Bramble-Local:
  desc: |
    Supertag classes introduced under CONCEPTS.md §12 — `#Step`,
    `#Run`, and the determinism-gradient kinds `#Person`,
    `#LLM`, `#Classifier`, `#Rule` — live in
    `plugin-bramble/src/types/`. They are NOT added to
    `@dxos/echo` core. New `Edge.kind` values
    (`'is-run-of'`, `'parent-run'`, `'available-executor'`,
    `'executed-by'`) follow the same rule.

    Why: R-No-Echo-Changes — plugin-bramble is a strict
    consumer of the `@dxos/echo` surface. New types and edge
    kinds for substrate vocabulary belong in plugin-bramble's
    own type module, not in core ECHO.

    How to apply: when adding a substrate-vocabulary type or
    edge kind, create or extend a schema under
    `plugin-bramble/src/types/`. If the `Edge.kind` enum lives
    in a core package such that new entries require core-
    package edits, that is a coordination point with
    `@dxos/types` that MUST be raised before implementation —
    not after.
```

```mdl
rule R-DAG-First-Class
  desc: |
    Bramble's data model is a DAG (per F-DAG), not a tree.
    Don't ship tree-shape artifacts: no designated root, no
    sole-parent assumptions, no parent-chain navigation
    primitives baked into the substrate. The structural top
    is an EMERGENT set (Nodes with no incoming `'child'`
    predecessor edges), not a designated entity. Multi-
    parent is first-class (F-DAG.Phase3e); predecessor
    navigation walks the emergent set (PredecessorNav); the
    user lands on a specific Node via explicit nav (today's
    Node per F-Bramble-Nav, supertag pages per F-Supertag,
    F-Zoom, @-mention, etc.).

    Watch for tree-shape leaks during design. The most
    common: a "root" / "container" / "head" field on a
    parent type. When such a field feels needed, the design
    has probably reverted to tree-think; the right question
    is "which Node should the user be focused on?" — answered
    by an explicit nav primitive, not by a designated root
    on the data side.

    How to apply: reject new schema fields that designate a
    structural root. Reject helpers that assume "the parent"
    (singular) of a Node. When reviewing a new feat, ask:
    does this work if the Node has zero parents? Two parents?
    If the feat ASSUMES one, surface the assumption — it's
    probably a tree-shape leak.

    Concrete example: 2026-05-17 session — `Graph.root:
    Ref<Node>` field had been on Bramble.Graph for the
    plugin's whole life (predating F-DAG) and persisted
    through F-One-Graph + F-Bramble-Nav + F-Today before
    Steve caught it as a tree-shape artifact in the
    F-No-Root design conversation. Retired entirely (Graph
    became a marker; the DAG speaks for itself).
```

```mdl
rule R-Consolidation-Scrub:
  desc: |
    PLUGIN.mdl, CONCEPTS.md, `src/**` comments, README, and the
    agent memory file carry only CURRENT canon. Superseded reqs,
    retired vocabulary, and deprecated patterns live in git
    history alone — no `## Superseded` section, no `_archive/`
    directory, no in-scope summaries, no `Note:` entries that
    preserve the retired shape for explanatory purposes. Every
    commit that consolidates, renames, or retires a req /
    pattern / vocabulary item MUST grep PLUGIN.mdl, CONCEPTS.md,
    `src/**`, README, and the agent memory file for the retired
    name and scrub in-scope leftovers in the SAME commit.

    Why: superseded-but-in-scope text is the shadow-source for
    agent paraphrase. When a retired allowlist, an older
    trigger, or a renamed primitive lives in the corpus an
    agent reads as current state, the agent's paraphrase from
    memory blends current and superseded shapes into a
    frankenstein description of "current" behaviour.

    How to apply: when authoring a commit that retires or
    consolidates a name (req id, pattern name, vocabulary
    item), run a final pass before staging:

        git grep -F '<retired-name>' -- '*.mdl' '*.md' src/

    plus the agent memory file. Every match either gets
    removed or gets edited to use the current canonical name.
    Explanatory "this used to be called X" content goes in the
    COMMIT MESSAGE (where it lives in git log alongside the
    rename diff), not in any in-scope file.

    Concrete examples — both on this branch, both the same
    paraphrase-from-shadow failure mode:

    - 2026-05-16 — agent confabulated a "first apply" trigger
      for the predecessor `F-6.Phase3.tag-node.materialize`.
      Real predecessor said "first chip render"; current spec
      `F-Supertag.eager-materialization` (PLUGIN.mdl) says
      "outliner mount + on schemaRegistry change."
    - 2026-05-17 — agent confabulated declared supertag
      classes `[Person, Task, Org, Run, Step]`. No allowlist
      exists in current spec or code: PLUGIN.mdl says "no
      allowlist" and `tag-types.ts`'s `collectTagTypes`
      excludes only Relations + `Bramble.Node` +
      `Bramble.Graph`. The confabulation blended the
      SUPERSEDED hardcoded allowlist `[Task, Person,
      Organization]` (preserved at the time as a `Note:` in
      `## Remaining`) with the in-code Bramble-declared types
      Run / Step.

    Companion artefact: a one-time audit sweep of existing
    leftovers from prior consolidations on this branch
    (`F-V6`, `F-6.Phase3.tag-node.materialize`,
    `F-6.Phase3.tag-node.click`,
    `F-6.Phase3.tag-node.shift-click`,
    `F-6.Phase3.tag-node.schema-parent`,
    `F-6.Phase3.all-echo-types`, the `tag block` → `supertag
    node` vocabulary rename) is queued separately under
    `## Remaining`. This rule governs all FUTURE commits
    regardless of when the audit lands.
```

```mdl
rule R-Bramble-Surfaces-Wrap-Only:
  desc: |
    Bramble's user-facing reference / selection / drag /
    drop / nav surfaces target `Bramble.Node` instances ONLY.
    The @-mention picker, edge endpoints, drop targets,
    predecessor nav, page rendering, and position/order
    operations all consume Bramble.Nodes as their candidate
    set. Payload ECHO objects (Wnfs.File, typed-instance
    Person / Task / …, any object created outside Bramble)
    enter Bramble's world via the F-Supertag wrap pattern —
    a Bramble.Node is created carrying the payload as a
    supertag Ref — and become referenceable through that
    wrapper, never directly.

    Why: payload objects and their wrappers share display
    labels by design (the wrapper's `content` is seeded from
    the payload's label per `F-Supertag.title-sync` and
    `F-PDF-Upload.drop-seeds-content-with-filename`).
    Surfacing both in the same picker produces duplicate
    entries that are indistinguishable to the user. Treating
    the wrapper as the canonical handle everywhere user-
    facing surfaces resolve identity removes the duplication
    structurally — no per-typename suppression rules
    accreting over time.

    How to apply: when building a new picker, list, drag-
    source, drop-target, or reference-resolver, filter to
    `Filter.typename(Bramble.Node.typename)` as the candidate
    type. Do NOT enumerate the schema registry and OR
    together typename filters — that pattern picks up
    promotable payloads alongside wrappers.

    Exception — the `#` supertag-application picker (M-Apply,
    not M-Promote): chooses a TYPE, not an instance, so it
    legitimately enumerates the schema registry via
    `collectTagTypes` (`F-Supertag.types-shown`). M-Apply
    remains the canonical place to discover schemas; every
    other Bramble surface targets Bramble.Nodes.

    Concrete example — 2026-05-17 reported bug: drop a PDF
    named "XXX" into a Bramble bullet (creates a `Wnfs.File`
    payload + a wrapping `Bramble.Node`); open the @-mention
    picker on another bullet and type `@XXX`; the picker
    shows two entries labelled "XXX" — the wrapper and the
    unwrapped payload — indistinguishable to the user. The
    wrapper IS the user's handle to the PDF; the payload
    should not appear. Fix: filter `MentionPicker.tsx` to
    `Bramble.Node` only.
```

```mdl
rule R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet:
  desc: |
    Real `Bramble.Node` instances carry meaningful state on at
    least one axis — non-empty `content`, non-empty `supertags`,
    non-empty outgoing Edges, non-empty `children` (legacy), or
    non-empty `fields`. The renderer projects a pending row — a
    render-state placeholder — at every editable position in the
    Bramble that would otherwise correspond to a Node with zero
    meaningful state.

    Pending rows promote to real Nodes on first meaningful input.
    The promotion paths today (examples; the exact set tracks
    whatever paths the spec declares, per
    R-Spec-Entry-Conventions clause 1b):

    - A typed character routes through the `onPromote` path: a
      new Node carrying the typed content is persisted, and a
      structural `'child'` Edge is created from the parent at
      the pending row's slot per R-Edges-First-Class.
    - An `@`-mention target commit routes through the
      `onAddExisting` path: a structural Edge is created from
      the parent to the picked target at the slot.
    - A `#` supertag apply routes through the corresponding
      supertag promotion path (F-Supertag.types-shown +
      F-Supertag.uniqueness): a new Node is persisted carrying
      the supertag Ref at the slot.

    Real Nodes whose meaningful state collapses to zero across
    every axis are retired by removing their INCOMING Edges;
    the renderer projects a pending row at the vacated slot.
    The Node itself remains in ECHO per substrate-principles
    §10 (`PLUGIN.mdl:3930-3938` — "the substrate is non-
    destructive — Targets get RETIRED, not destructively
    removed; their history stays reconstructable via
    `checkoutVersion`"). Identity is preserved; `F-Versioning`
    pinned references continue to resolve.

    Why: the pending row is the substrate's single locus for
    content entering the Bramble. Every surface that resolves
    Bramble identity — mention picker, queries, drop targets,
    drag sources, predecessor nav — reads one shape: a Node
    carrying meaningful state. Tana's outliner product is built
    on the same invariant; the pattern is well-precedented.

    How to apply: when a new feat / req proposes creating a
    Node, check the meaningful-state predicate at creation
    time. If the proposed Node carries meaningful state at
    creation, the creation is correct and the gesture's effect
    is the pending row's promotion. If the proposed Node would
    carry zero meaningful state at creation, a pending row at
    the appropriate slot is the correct artefact; the real
    Node materialises later when input arrives.

    When a new feat / req proposes deleting a Node, check
    whether the Node would become empty across every
    meaningful axis post-delete. If yes, retire via incoming-
    edge removal (the renderer projects a pending row at the
    vacated slot); the Node remains in ECHO.

    Established 2026-05-17. Design conversation captured in
    CONCEPTS §13; the @-mention substrate refactor
    (R-Bramble-Surfaces-Wrap-Only) landed concurrently.
```

```mdl
rule R-Bramble-Subject-Path:
  desc: |
    Every `Bramble.Node` is addressable as a pane subject via
    a canonical, stable, shareable path. The pane's deck
    subject is the single source of truth for "which Node the
    pane is on" — no per-pane component-local state hides
    pane position from the layout system.

    Role-named entry points get stable aliases that resolve to
    whichever Node currently fills the role:

    - `bramble.today` → today's day-Node (the Bramble.Node
      carrying `#Day` whose `date` is today's local-tz
      `YYYY-MM-DD`). Resolution: `ensureDayNodeForDate(db,
      today())` per F-Today.

    An alias and the corresponding Node's canonical path name
    the SAME node; the subject resolver accepts either form.
    The left-nav button uses the alias (so the link auto-rolls
    daily, per F-Bramble-Nav.today-opens-todays-day-node).
    Gestures that target a specific Node (e.g. F-Zoom) write
    the canonical form (so each Node has a stable, shareable,
    history-navigable subject).

    Why: pane state — which Node a pane is currently
    rendering — lives in the subject path, not in component-
    local React state. Two consequences:

    1. Every pane navigation goes through the same operation
       (`LayoutOperation.Open`). Article reads `subject` and
       renders the corresponding Node; there is no per-pane
       zoom state hidden from the layout system.
    2. Browser navigation, bookmarks, and URL sharing all
       work via the subject path as the source of truth.
       Back / forward across zoom levels falls out for free.

    How to apply: when a new feat proposes a gesture that
    changes which Node the pane shows, the gesture invokes
    `LayoutOperation.Open` with the target's canonical path.
    New role-named entry points introduce a new alias (e.g.
    a future "All Tags" alias for the per-space Schema
    system node); the subject resolver maps the alias to that
    role's current Node.

    The literal path-string format is implementation-level
    (today's implementation reuses the existing
    `getObjectPathFromObject` shape from `@dxos/app-toolkit`,
    sharing the form F-Open-Pane already uses). The rule
    fixes the SEMANTIC — every Node has one canonical
    subject; aliases name roles; the deck subject is the
    truth — and stays out of the way of the literal format.

    Established 2026-05-17. Design conversation captured in
    CONCEPTS §14; the F-Zoom rewrite from local React zoom
    state to subject-path-driven zoom landed concurrently.
```

## Extensions

| Term        | URI                            |
|-------------|--------------------------------|
| `type`      | `org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0`       |
| `feat`      | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`       |
| `test`      | `org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0`       |
