## Types

```mdl
type TextSpan
  fields:
    kind: 'text'
    text: string
    marks?: ('bold' | 'italic' | 'highlight' | 'code')[]
```

```mdl
type RefSpan
  fields:
    kind: 'ref'
    target: Ref<Obj>          # any ECHO object; label resolved at render time
```

```mdl
type StructuredContent
  desc: ordered run of TextSpan | RefSpan; supports inline marks and live-name refs.
  literals: (TextSpan | RefSpan)[]
```

```mdl
type TagDef
  desc: stub for supertag definitions; expanded in a later increment.
  fields:
    name: string
```

```mdl
type Node
  desc: |
    The substrate node. Most fields optional and unused in Increment 1;
    defined now to be a faithful Tana-Paste import target without later migration.
  fields:
    content?: StructuredContent
    children?: Ref<Node>[]
    kind?: 'bullet' | 'heading' | 'todo' | 'image' | 'url' | 'search' | 'view' | 'code'
    supertags?: Ref<TagDef>[]
    fields?: Record<string, Ref<Node>[]>
    state?: { checked?: boolean }
    view?: { kind: 'table' | 'cards' | 'tabs' | 'calendar' }
```

```mdl
type Graph
  desc: |
    Marker on the space — proves the singleton-per-space Bramble
    exists (F-One-Graph) and carries metadata. Bramble's data is a
    DAG, not a tree; the Graph object does NOT designate a
    structural root (F-No-Root). It is NOT directly viewable —
    the user reaches the Bramble through its Nodes, never through
    the Graph object.
  fields:
    name?: string
```

```mdl
type Day
  desc: |
    Supertag schema marking a Node as the bullet that represents a
    specific date. A Node carrying `#Day` is the "today" / "March
    5" / "last Friday" anchor — a noun, not a content field. Day-
    as-noun, consistent with how Wnfs.File represents file-as-noun
    (and pointedly distinct from the older JournalEntry pattern,
    which represents content-tied-to-a-date, not a date itself).

    Invariant: at most one Node per (date, space) carries `#Day`.
    Date-keyed extension of F-Supertag.uniqueness — find-or-create
    semantics at every write path.

    Marked SystemType so the generic per-typename navigator
    listing does not surface a flat list of Day instances.
  fields:
    date: string                   # ISO YYYY-MM-DD, user's local timezone
```

## Components

```mdl
component Article
  desc: |
    Article surface for a Graph. Resolves the outline's root Node
    and mounts the Editor. Falls back to a (loading...) message
    while the root ref is unresolved.
  props:
    role: string
    subject: Graph
    attendableId: string
  layout: |
    ┌─────────────────────────┐
    │ <Editor>           │
    └─────────────────────────┘
```

```mdl
component Editor
  desc: |
    ProseMirror editor mounted over a single Node. Reads Node.content
    on mount, writes Node.content on every doc-changing transaction.
    Plain text in one paragraph; no marks, no inline refs, no nested
    children. External mutations into the editor are not synced
    (one-way: editor -> ECHO).
  props:
    block: Node
    autoFocus?: boolean
    onEnter?: (beforeText, afterText) -> void   # parent splits into a sibling
  emits:
    onEnter(beforeText: string, afterText: string)   # cursor split position
  layout: |
    ┌─────────────────────────┐
    │ contenteditable text…   │
    └─────────────────────────┘
```

```mdl
component Graph
  desc: |
    Renders the visible bullets of a Graph as a flat list of
    Editors. Holds the Enter-creates-sibling logic and a
    contextmenu listener that copies a bullet's DXN to the clipboard.
    The rootNode is the (invisible) container; its children are the
    visible Nodes.
  props:
    rootNode: Node
  state:
    focusId?: string                  # node id to autofocus after Enter
  actions:
    handleEnter(currentNode, beforeText, afterText)
    handleContextMenu(event)          # copies Obj.getDXN(node) to clipboard
  layout: |
    ┌─────────────────────────┐
    │ • bullet 1              │
    │ • bullet 2              │
    │ ...                     │
    └─────────────────────────┘
```

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: Plugin scaffold
  req F-1.1: Graph appears in the navigator's Create menu (gated under isLabs).
  req F-1.2: Creating a Graph persists a Graph + a single empty root Node in ECHO.
  req F-1.3: Opening a Graph renders the Article placeholder without console errors.
  req F-1.4: Build, lint, and tests pass for plugin-blocks and composer-app.
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Editable bullet      # Increment 2 (current)
  desc: |
    Single editable text bullet backed by a Node ECHO object. Out of
    scope: marks (bold/italic/highlight), inline refs (Increment 4),
    hierarchy (Increment 3), and syncing external mutations into the
    editor (deferred).
  req F-2.1: ProseMirror editor renders the root Node's content on mount.
  req F-2.2: Typing updates Node.content reactively; reload restores it.
  req F-2.3: Doc schema is one paragraph holding text*; no marks or ref nodes.
  req F-2.4:
    when: Node.content is empty or undefined
    then: editor mounts with an empty paragraph; cursor placeable; typing creates a single TextSpan.
```

```mdl
feat F-3: Flat sibling bullets    # Increment 3 (current)
  desc: |
    The outline's root Node is invisible; its `children` array holds
    the visible bullets. Enter splits the current bullet at the cursor
    and inserts a new sibling. Each bullet has a stable DXN copyable
    via right-click. Out of scope here: Tab/Shift+Tab nesting (F-3b),
    drag-reorder, folding.
  req F-3.1:
    when: user presses Enter inside a bullet
    then:
      - current Node.content trimmed to the before-cursor text
      - new Node created with the after-cursor text as content
      - new Node ref spliced into rootNode.children at currentIndex + 1
      - cursor lands at start of the new bullet
  req F-3.2: Each rendered bullet has a stable DXN derived from Obj.getDXN(node).
  req F-3.3:
    when: user right-clicks a bullet
    then: bullet's DXN is copied to the clipboard
  req F-3.4: I1/I2 outlines (root Node with content, no children) auto-migrate to root with one child Node on first mount.
```

```mdl
feat F-3b: Nested bullets         # Increment 3b (current)
  desc: |
    Bullets can be re-parented via Tab (indent under previous sibling) and
    Shift+Tab (dedent to grandparent's children). Rendering is recursive:
    each Node's children render indented below it. The moved Node's own
    children come along (indenting moves the whole subtree).
  req F-3b.1:
    when: user presses Tab inside a bullet that has a previous sibling
    then:
      - bullet's ref is removed from its parent's children
      - bullet's ref is appended to the previous sibling's children
      - bullet keeps its own children (subtree moves with it)
      - editor for the moved bullet regains focus after re-mount
  req F-3b.2:
    when: user presses Tab on the first child (no previous sibling)
    then: nothing happens; Tab is consumed (focus does not escape).
  req F-3b.3:
    when: user presses Shift+Tab inside a nested bullet
    then:
      - bullet's ref is removed from its parent's children
      - bullet's ref is inserted into its grandparent's children at parent's index + 1
      - editor for the moved bullet regains focus after re-mount
  req F-3b.4:
    when: user presses Shift+Tab on a top-level bullet (no grandparent)
    then: nothing happens; Shift+Tab is consumed.
```

```mdl
component Node
  desc: |
    Recursive renderer. One Editor for this Node, then a nested column
    of Nodes for its children. Tab/Shift+Tab/Enter handlers live here so
    they can close over the parent + grandparent context.
  props:
    block: Node
    parent: Node                       # who owns node via children[]
    grandparent?: Node                 # who owns parent
    focusId?: string
    setFocusId: (id?: string) -> void
  actions:
    handleEnter(beforeText, afterText)
    handleIndent()                      # Tab — re-parent under prev sibling
    handleDedent()                      # Shift+Tab — re-parent to grandparent
```

```mdl
component TagChip
  desc: |
    Inline chip rendered for each supertag on a node-tagged-with-
    supertag. Displays the supertag's renameable label (sourced
    from the per-space supertag node's content) prefixed by `#`.
    Immediately adjacent: an open-instance control that navigates
    to the typed instance's Composer article surface
    (F-Supertag.open-instance-control).
  props:
    typename: string
    db: Database
    instance: Obj.Any
  actions:
    handleChipClick(event)              # F-Supertag.chip-gestures
    handleOpenInstance(event)           # F-Supertag.open-instance-control
  layout:
    [#Task] [↗]
       |     |
       |     open-instance control
       chip
```

```mdl
feat F-4: Inline live-name refs    # Increment 4 (current)
  desc: |
    Typing @ inside a bullet opens an object picker; selecting a target
    inserts an atomic inline ref node into the editor's doc and persists
    a real Ref<Obj.Unknown> into Node.content. The RefNodeView resolves
    the target by DXN at mount and on each ProseMirror update(), so a
    rename followed by any local edit refreshes the label. External
    rename without a local edit refresh propagates in I4b.
  req F-4.1:
    when: user types @ at the start of a paragraph or after whitespace
    then: |
      a picker popover opens adjacent to the cursor, with its top-left
      corner anchored at the @ position. The popover flips above the line
      when there isn't enough room below; otherwise it opens below. Both
      coordinates are clamped to the viewport so it never goes off-screen.
      The picker shows matching Bramble.Nodes per
      R-Bramble-Surfaces-Wrap-Only — payload ECHO objects (Wnfs.File,
      typed-instance Person / Task / …) are reached through their
      wrapping Bramble.Node, never directly. Each candidate is labelled
      by its resolved display text; empty-labelled candidates (Nodes
      with no content yet, unnamed system Nodes) are hidden. The Node
      currently being edited is excluded from results — a bullet cannot
      reference itself.
  req F-4.2:
    when: user types more letters after @
    then: the picker filters live by case-insensitive substring on the object's label
  req F-4.3:
    when: user clicks an item in the picker
    then:
      - the @-and-query range is replaced by an atomic ref node
      - a real Ref is persisted into Node.content as a RefSpan segment
      - the picker closes
      - a space is inserted after the ref node
  req F-4.4:
    when: an inline ref renders
    then: the visible text is the target's current label (Obj.getLabel)
  req F-4.5:
    when: user presses Escape while picker is open
    then: picker closes; the @ trigger remains as plain text
  req F-4.6:
    given: the @-mention picker is open
    when: the user presses Shift+Enter, Cmd+Shift+Enter, or any other
      Enter chord with shiftKey or metaKey held
    then:
      - the picker swallows the keypress as a no-op
        (preventDefault + stopPropagation; picker stays open; the
        editor's F-Shift-Enter / F-Cmd-Shift-Enter handlers do
        NOT fire — no sibling is created)
      - the rationale matches F-6.Phase1.keyboard-nav: a
        sibling-creation gesture mid-`@`-query would silently insert
        a new bullet while the user's attention is on the picker.
        The user must dismiss the picker (Escape, click outside, or
        commit a target) before sibling-creation gestures take
        effect.
      - Cmd+ArrowUp / Cmd+ArrowDown (F-V2.8 / F-V2.9 collapse-expand)
        are NOT intercepted — they fall through to the editor's
        keymap.
    Note: this req is symmetric with F-6.Phase1.keyboard-nav's
    modifier-swallow clause for the `#` (TagPicker) picker. Both
    pickers share the same `bareKey` gate (no modifiers) for arrow
    nav and commit; modifier-Enter chords are always swallowed while
    the picker is open.
  req F-4.7:
    given: the @-mention picker is open with one or more matching
      candidates (e.g. user typed `@happy` and an existing Node
      labelled `happy` is visible)
    when: the user presses ArrowDown, ArrowUp, or bare Enter
    then:
      - ArrowDown advances the active item by one, wrapping from the
        last entry to the first.
      - ArrowUp moves the active item back by one, wrapping from the
        first entry to the last.
      - the active item renders with a visible highlight (distinct
        from the hover background) so the keyboard cursor is always
        findable.
      - moving the mouse over an entry sets the active item to that
        entry — keyboard and mouse share a single highlight.
      - if the active item is outside the picker's scroll viewport
        after a nav move, the list scrolls so the active item is in
        view.
      - bare Enter commits the active item (same path as a mouse
        click: `onSelect(item)` → caller's `handleSelectTarget` for
        the editor body case, or `onAddExisting(target)` for the
        pending-child case). The editor does NOT split the bullet
        and the pending-child does NOT promote a new Node.
      - when the picker filters down to zero matches, bare Enter is
        a no-op (the picker stays open and the editor still does not
        split / promote). Escape dismisses per the existing F-4.5.
    Note: F-4.7 closes the bug reported 2026-05-14 where typing
    `@happy` in a pending-child and pressing Enter (without clicking
    a picker entry) eventually led to duplicate Nodes being created.
    Root cause: the MentionPicker had no keyboard handler, so Enter
    fell through to the plain `<div contenteditable>`'s native
    behaviour (insert newline), making the picker filter fail; users
    then backspaced back to a state where the pending-child's
    `handleInput` saw text that didn't start with `@` and routed
    through `onPromote`, creating a new Node with the typed label.
    F-4.7 closes this gap by giving the picker keyboard ownership of
    Enter (commit) and Arrow keys (nav), preventing the fall-through
    that triggered the misroute. The fix matches F-6.Phase1.keyboard-
    nav's pattern exactly — window-capture keydown listener with the
    same modifier gate from F-4.6.
```

```mdl
feat F-V1: Bullet markers and indent guide   # Increment V1 (current — landed alongside F-V2)
  desc: |
    Visual fidelity to the Tana outliner. Each rendered Node has a small
    bullet glyph to its left (filled circle by default). When a Node has
    children visible, a thin vertical guide line runs from below its bullet
    through the nested column so the parent/child relationship is visible
    at a glance.
  req F-V1.1: every Editor renders a bullet marker (filled circle) at consistent gutter offset.
  req F-V1.2:
    when: a Node has at least one rendered child
    then: a vertical guide line is drawn from the parent's bullet down through the indented child column.
  req F-V1.3: the gutter width is consistent regardless of bullet state, so text alignment matches across siblings.
```

```mdl
feat F-V2: Collapse / expand parents         # Increment V2 (current)
  desc: |
    A parent Node can be collapsed; its children are hidden until expanded.
    State lives on Node.state.expanded so it persists across reloads and peers.

    Visual vocabulary (terminology used below):
    - "Node Bullet" — the small dark filled dot rendered for every Node.
      When a Node has children AND is collapsed, a shaded ring (halo)
      surrounds the dot. When a Node has children AND is expanded, or has
      no children, the dot is alone — no halo. The halo is the lone signal
      that hidden children exist; there is NO numeric badge.
    - "Expand/Collapse control" — a chevron (▸ closed, ▾ open) set inside a
      bordered, lightly filled circle, rendered to the IMMEDIATE LEFT of the
      Node Bullet for parents only. It is hidden by default and revealed
      ONLY while any part of the Node's row is hovered, or while the
      control itself has focus. Leaves render no control — a same-width
      spacer reserves the slot so columns stay aligned.

    Toggle inputs:
    - Click the Expand/Collapse control to toggle.
    - Mod+ArrowUp (Cmd+Up) collapses the current parent when open;
      Mod+ArrowDown (Cmd+Down) expands it when closed. Both are no-ops in
      any other state and fall through to the editor's default cursor
      behaviour.
    - Hovering the Expand/Collapse control surfaces a tooltip with the
      shortcut.
    - The Node Bullet itself does NOT toggle; clicking it zooms into the
      Node (see F-Zoom).

    Visual size:
    - The chevron glyph inside the Expand/Collapse control matches the
      surrounding body text size — it is not visually small relative to
      the bullet's text.
    - The chevron itself is rendered as a thin stroke (two diagonal lines
      meeting at a point), matching Tana's visual style. Filled
      triangles like ▸ / ▾ are NOT used — they read too heavy against
      the body text.
  req F-V2.1: clicking a Node's bullet marker toggles its expanded state.
  req F-V2.2:
    when: Node has children AND Node.state.expanded === false
    then:
      - the children column is not rendered
      - the Node Bullet renders the dark filled dot WITH a shaded ring (halo) surrounding it
      - no count badge or other annotation is added (the halo alone signals hidden children)
  req F-V2.3:
    when: Node has no children
    then: the Node Bullet renders the dark filled dot alone (no halo); the bullet is non-interactive.
  req F-V2.4:
    when: Node has children AND state.expanded is undefined or true
    then:
      - the Node Bullet renders the dark filled dot alone (no halo) — visually identical to a leaf
      - the indented child column + guide line below the row distinguish the open parent from a leaf
  req F-V2.5: collapsed state is persisted on Node.state.expanded and survives reloads.
  req F-V2.6:
    when: Node has children OR has any supertags (F-6) OR is a leaf (so the
      pending child can be revealed via F-Pending-Child)
    then: an Expand/Collapse control (chevron in a bordered circle) is mounted to the immediate left of the Node Bullet; the control's slot is reserved at all times so layout does not shift on hover.
    Note (revised in F-Pending-Child): the prior version of this req only
    fired when the Node had children. Leaves now also show the chevron
    so they can be expanded to reveal the pending-child placeholder
    (F-Pending-Child) and, when tagged, the field group(s) (F-6 Phase 2).
  req F-V2.6a:
    when: the user's pointer is over any part of THIS Node's row only (other Nodes' rows are unaffected)
    then: this Node's Expand/Collapse control becomes fully visible (opacity 1).
  req F-V2.6b:
    when: the pointer leaves this Node's row AND the control does not have keyboard focus
    then: this Node's control is hidden (opacity 0); the slot remains reserved; other Nodes' controls are unaffected.
  note: hover state is tracked per-Node in React (no Tailwind group-hover) so a hover on one row does not light up controls in adjacent or nested rows.
  req F-V2.6c:
    when: the user clicks the Expand/Collapse control
    then: state.expanded toggles, identical to clicking the Node Bullet.
  req F-V2.7:
    when: a Node does not satisfy F-V2.6 (extremely rare given the
      revised condition — currently no such case)
    then: a same-width empty spacer renders in place of the chevron so leaves align with parents.
  req F-V2.8:
    when: cursor is in a Node whose state.expanded is true (or undefined) and that has children, and the user presses Mod+ArrowUp
    then: the node collapses; state.expanded becomes false.
  req F-V2.9:
    when: cursor is in a Node whose state.expanded is false and that has children, and the user presses Mod+ArrowDown
    then: the node expands; state.expanded becomes true.
  req F-V2.10:
    when: cursor is in a Node that does not match the conditions of F-V2.8 / F-V2.9
    then: Mod+ArrowUp / Mod+ArrowDown fall through to the editor's default cursor behaviour.
  req F-V2.11:
    when: user hovers the chevron
    then: a tooltip displays "Collapse ⌘ ↑" or "Expand ⌘ ↓" matching the available action.
  req F-V2.12:
    given: a new Node is created via the outline UI (initial graph seed,
      Enter / Shift+Enter / Cmd+Shift+Enter sibling, or pending-child
      promote)
    when: it is rendered
    then:
      - the Node renders collapsed: `state.expanded === false` is set
        explicitly at creation time (NOT left undefined), so subsequent
        renders treat the Node as collapsed without relying on the
        "undefined = expanded" default that F-V2.4 establishes for
        legacy Nodes.
      - the pending-child placeholder row (F-Pending-Child.expand) is
        NOT rendered until the user explicitly expands the Node.
      - if the Node carries supertags (F-6), the field group is NOT
        rendered until the user explicitly expands it (the chip itself
        still renders inline per F-6.Phase1.chip).
      - the user expands by clicking the chevron or pressing
        Mod+ArrowDown (F-V2.9), at which point pending-child /
        field group surface normally.
    Note: this applies to user-facing creation paths only — system
    Nodes (library, schema, tag wrappers, query nodes) retain their
    existing default-expanded behaviour so their pre-materialised
    children remain visible on first navigation.
```

```mdl
feat F-Pending-Child: leaf shows pending child when expanded   # Increment Pending-Child (current)
  desc: |
    Tana-style: leaf bullets (no real children) get an Expand/Collapse
    chevron just like parents (per the revised F-V2.6). When the user
    expands a leaf, a "pending child" placeholder row renders below it
    in the same column its real children would occupy. The pending child
    is a faint bullet + an empty editable area; it is visual only and
    has no persisted Node. As soon as the user types into the ghost
    row, a real Node is created and appended to the leaf's children;
    focus transfers to the new Node and any typed characters land
    there.

    Parents (Nodes that already have at least one child) do NOT get
    a pending child row — their expanded view continues to show only
    their real children. (Per user spec: "only leaf shows the ghost
    child at the end".)

    F-6 Phase 2 reuses the same chevron-on-leaves mechanic to expose
    field groups for tagged bullets; the pending row and field groups
    can co-exist when a tagged leaf is expanded.
  req F-Pending-Child.hover:
    given: a Node has no children
    when: the user's pointer hovers anywhere on the Node's row
    then: the Expand/Collapse control becomes visible (chevron in
      collapsed orientation if state.expanded is undefined / false).
  req F-Pending-Child.expand:
    given: a Node has no children
    when: the user clicks the Expand control (chevron) on the row
    then:
      - state.expanded is set to true (persisted on the Node)
      - a pending child row renders in the indented children column
        (same indent and guide-line column as real children would
        use); the pending row consists of a faint bullet (lighter shade
        than a real bullet) and an empty editable area.
      - the pending row's interactive surface — clicking, focus,
        cursor placement, typing — behaves IDENTICALLY to an empty
        real bullet (same baseline alignment per
        F-Pending-Child.cursor-alignment, same click-anywhere-to-focus,
        same key handling). The only visible differences are the
        lighter bullet shade and the lack of persistence until first
        input.
  req F-Pending-Child.type:
    given: a leaf Node is expanded showing the pending child row
    when: the user clicks anywhere on the pending row and types any
      character
    then:
      - a new Node is created with the typed text as its content
      - the new Node's Ref is appended to the leaf's `children`
      - focus moves to the new (now-real) Node; further typing
        continues there as if the user had typed into a normal child
        bullet
      - the pending row visually disappears (the new real child
        replaces it). Subsequent edits to the new child do NOT
        re-create another ghost.
  req F-Pending-Child.empty-collapse:
    given: a leaf Node was expanded (pending row showing) but the user
      did not type anything
    when: the user collapses the Node (chevron click or Cmd+Up)
    then: state.expanded becomes false; no Node was created. No
      cleanup is required because no Node was persisted.
  req F-Pending-Child.cursor-alignment:
    given: a leaf Node is expanded showing the pending child row
    when: the user clicks on the row and the editable area receives focus
    then: the caret renders at the SAME vertical baseline as a real
      bullet's editor (i.e. the pending row's caret sits where a
      typed character's first letter would sit, not above it).
    Note: empty `<div contentEditable>` line-boxes default to a
    different vertical metric than `.ProseMirror`'s `<p>`; the
    pending editable matches by being inline-block with a minimum
    width and the same line-height as the editor's text, so
    `items-baseline` on the row's flex container aligns the caret
    to the bullet baseline.
  req F-Pending-Child.page-root:
    given: a Node N is the pane's page node (rooted via F-Open-Pane's
      shift-click, F-Zoom's regular click, or via the initial graph
      subject)
    when: the pane renders
    then:
      - a pending-child row is ALWAYS mounted at the END of the page
        body — i.e. AFTER any real children when N has children
        (so the user can always extend the list past the last bullet
        without first pressing Enter on it), or as the only thing
        in the body when N has no children (so a freshly-zoomed
        leaf has a place to start typing). Same indented column
        real children occupy. Sits below the H1 PageHeader (and
        below any page-level FieldGroups for tagged page nodes).
      - this page-root pending-child row is INDEPENDENT of
        `N.state.expanded` — it appears even when N was created
        collapsed by F-V2.12.
      - typing into the page-root pending-child promotes a new
        Node and appends it as N's last child (via `createEdge`),
        following the same semantics as the row-level
        F-Pending-Child.type — the new Node is created collapsed
        per F-V2.12.
      - typing `@` opens the MentionPicker so an existing Node can be
        added as a structural child via a fresh ChildEdge, identical
        to the row-level pending-child's F-DAG.Phase3a.add-existing-
        via-picker behaviour.
      - the page-root pending-child does NOT double-render with the
        row-level pending-child of F-Pending-Child.expand: the row-
        level one only renders when N appears AS A ROW (in its
        parent's body), never when N is the page itself.
    Note: this closes the empty-page UX hole reported 2026-05-14
    (zooming into a leaf showed a completely blank pane with no
    affordance for adding content) AND the always-end-of-page
    affordance reported 2026-05-14 (a page with bullets had no
    visible "add another" pending row). Distinct from
    F-Pending-Child.expand, which is about ROW-level rendering
    gated by state.expanded.
  req F-Pending-Child.sibling-slot:
    given: a creation gesture requests a pending row at a
      specific sibling slot — a position in the Bramble
      between two real sibling Nodes under a common parent,
      OR at the start / end of a parent's children list.
      Example creation gestures today (the exact set tracks
      whatever creation gestures the spec declares per
      R-Spec-Entry-Conventions clause 1b): Shift+Enter
      (F-Shift-Enter.1) and Cmd+Shift+Enter
      (F-Cmd-Shift-Enter.1) on a body bullet; Shift+Enter
      in the H1 (F-Page-Header.9) requesting the first-child
      slot of the page node.
    when: the pending row renders at the requested slot per
      R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet
    then:
      - the pending row's render carries the same visual
        properties as the leaf-row-end pending row of
        F-Pending-Child.expand and the end-of-body pending
        row of F-Pending-Child.page-root: same indent
        column, same bullet style, same caret alignment per
        F-Pending-Child.cursor-alignment, same click-
        anywhere-to-focus behaviour;
      - the pending row carries a position hint identifying
        the requested slot (target parent + insertion order);
      - on the `onPromote` path (typed character), the new
        Bramble.Node is persisted with the typed content and
        a structural `'child'` Edge is created from the
        target parent to the new Node at the requested slot
        via `createEdge` per R-Edges-First-Class — the
        edge's `order` lands the new Node between the
        flanking real siblings (or at the start / end of the
        children list as requested);
      - on the `onAddExisting` path (mention target commit),
        a structural `'child'` Edge is created from the
        target parent to the picked target at the requested
        slot via `createEdge` with the same ordering rules;
      - on the `#` supertag apply path, the new Bramble.Node
        carrying the supertag Ref is persisted at the
        requested slot via `createEdge` with the same
        ordering rules.
```

```mdl
feat F-Retire-Empty-Node: a Node whose meaningful state collapses to zero across every axis retires via incoming-edge removal; the renderer projects a pending row at the vacated slot    # Increment Retire-Empty-Node (not started)
  desc: |
    Operationalises R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet's
    erasure-side corollary. When user gestures (backspace
    through the last character, deletion of the last supertag,
    removal of the last outgoing edge, etc.) reduce a real
    Bramble.Node X to zero meaningful state across every axis
    (per the rule's predicate — empty `content` AND empty
    `supertags` AND empty outgoing Edges AND empty `children`
    AND empty `fields`), X's incoming `'child'` Edges are
    removed; the renderer projects a pending row at each
    vacated slot.

    X itself remains in ECHO as a retired orphan per
    substrate-principles §10 (`PLUGIN.mdl:3930-3938`).
    Identity is preserved; `F-Versioning` pinned references
    continue to resolve via `Obj.checkoutVersion`.

    Out of scope for F-Retire-Empty-Node:
    - cleanup / GC of retired orphan Nodes — deferred per
      F-PDF-Upload's non-cascading delete model;
    - explicit revival of a retired Node — no UX gesture
      today; orphans are reachable via direct id lookup or
      cross-pane traversal of pinned references.
  req F-Retire-Empty-Node.1:
    given: a real `Bramble.Node` X with meaningful state on
      at least one axis (per
      R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet's predicate),
      referenced in the Bramble via one or more incoming
      `'child'` Edges from parents
    when: a user gesture reduces X to zero meaningful state
      across every axis (e.g. backspacing through the last
      character of X's content while X has empty supertags,
      empty children, no outgoing edges, empty fields)
    then:
      - every incoming `'child'` Edge to X is removed via
        the Edge-deletion path R-Edges-First-Class governs;
      - the renderer projects a pending row at each vacated
        slot (one pending row per removed Edge) per
        R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet;
      - X remains in ECHO with full history;
        `Obj.version(X)` and prior versions are retrievable
        via `Obj.checkoutVersion` per F-Versioning's
        substrate alignment;
      - focus moves to the pending row that replaces X's
        most recent rendered position.
```

```mdl
feat F-Shift-Enter: pending row at the sibling slot below         # Increment Shift-Enter (current)
  desc: |
    Shift+Enter renders a pending row at the sibling slot
    immediately after the editing Node per
    R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet. The pending row takes
    focus; the editing Node's content is preserved. First
    meaningful input into the pending row promotes it to a real
    Bramble.Node at the slot. Distinct from Enter which splits
    the editing Node's content at the cursor into two real
    Nodes.
  req F-Shift-Enter.1:
    given: I am editing a real `Bramble.Node` X
    when: I press Shift+Enter
    then:
      - a pending row Y renders at the sibling slot immediately
        after X — same indent as X, same parent in the Bramble,
        same render slot any real sibling Node would occupy; Y
        exists in the render layer per
        R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet;
      - focus moves to Y; the caret renders at Y's editable
        area per `F-Pending-Child.cursor-alignment`;
      - on first meaningful input into Y, Y promotes to a real
        Bramble.Node at its sibling slot via the pending-row
        promotion paths (per R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet).
```

```mdl
feat F-Cmd-Shift-Enter: pending row at the sibling slot above   # Increment Cmd-Shift-Enter (current)
  desc: |
    Cmd+Shift+Enter (Mod+Shift+Enter) renders a pending row at
    the sibling slot immediately before the editing Node per
    R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet. Mirrors F-Shift-Enter for
    the "above" direction. The pending row takes focus; first
    meaningful input promotes it to a real Bramble.Node at the
    slot.
  req F-Cmd-Shift-Enter.1:
    given: I am editing a real `Bramble.Node` X
    when: I press Cmd+Shift+Enter (Mod+Shift+Enter)
    then:
      - a pending row Y renders at the sibling slot immediately
        before X — same indent as X, same parent in the Bramble,
        same render slot any real sibling Node would occupy; Y
        exists in the render layer per
        R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet;
      - focus moves to Y; the caret renders at Y's editable
        area per `F-Pending-Child.cursor-alignment`;
      - on first meaningful input into Y, Y promotes to a real
        Bramble.Node at its sibling slot via the pending-row
        promotion paths (per R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet).
```

```mdl
feat F-Zoom: zoom into a Node by changing the pane subject    # Increment Zoom (current)
  desc: |
    Clicking a `Bramble.Node`'s bullet navigates the pane to
    that Node by changing the pane subject to the Node's
    canonical path per R-Bramble-Subject-Path. The pane's
    Article re-renders with the zoomed Node as its pageNode.
    Zoom state lives in the pane subject — no per-pane React
    state hides the current zoom from the layout system.

    Consequences that fall out for free:

    - Browser back / forward navigate across zoom levels (each
      zoom is a subject change pushed to history).
    - Bookmarking and URL-sharing land on the exact zoomed
      view.
    - Clicking a different navigation target (e.g. Today in
      the left nav, per `F-Bramble-Nav.today-resets-zoom`)
      changes the subject; the pane's prior zoom is naturally
      discarded because "the subject is the zoom."
    - F-Open-Pane (shift-click) and F-Zoom (regular click)
      become symmetric — both write subject paths; F-Zoom
      writes to the current pane, F-Open-Pane writes to a new
      pane.

    Backlink counts continue to reflect every incoming ref
    across the Bramble (not just the zoomed subtree) so badges
    remain accurate at every zoom level.

  req F-Zoom.click-changes-subject:
    given: a pane displays Bramble.Node R as its current
      subject, and R has a structural descendant X visible in
      the pane's rendered tree
    when: I click X's bullet
    then:
      - the pane's subject becomes X's canonical path via
        `LayoutOperation.Open` per R-Bramble-Subject-Path;
      - the pane's Article re-renders with X as the pageNode
        — X's content + supertags + children tree render
        rooted at the pane top;
      - the predecessor breadcrumb shows X's incoming-edge
        sources per F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav (the
        previously-visible "← Outline" link is replaced by
        this navigation primitive).

  req F-Zoom.subject-change-replaces-rendered-target:
    given: a pane's current subject is some specific
      Bramble.Node X (its canonical path per
      R-Bramble-Subject-Path)
    when: any other gesture changes the pane subject (e.g.
      `F-Bramble-Nav.today-opens-todays-day-node` clicking
      Today, `F-DAG.Phase3e` predecessor-nav, browser back,
      an `@`-mention commit per F-4, a link click in inline
      content)
    then:
      - the Article re-renders with the new subject as its
        pageNode;
      - X's view is replaced by the new subject's view —
        zoom state for X does not persist across the
        subject change because the subject is the zoom.
```

```mdl
feat F-Headline: zoomed headline preserves all content   # Increment Headline (current)
  desc: |
    The zoomed-view header (large bold H1 above the child tree) renders
    the full content of the zoomed Node — text segments AND inline
    refs alike. Refs render as link-blue spans (matching F-V3 inline
    ref styling) with the target's current label, kept live via the
    same `Obj.subscribe` pattern the inline RefNodeView uses.

    Current-behavior gap that prompted this spec:
    - The headline used to call `getDisplayLabel(zoomedNode)`, which
      filters Node.content down to text segments only and joins
      them. Refs were silently dropped, leaving visible gaps in the
      headline. Example: a bullet whose content is
      `"what is in [@A] and what [@B] now"` rendered as
      `"what is in  and what  now"` when zoomed in (note the double
      spaces where the refs were).

    Implementation: a new `Content` React component iterates
    `node.content` and renders each segment — text spans render as
    plain text; ref spans render via a `RefLabel` child that
    subscribes to the target via `useObject` and renders a styled
    span using `getDisplayLabel`. `Article` mounts
    `<Content block={zoomedNode} />` inside the H1.
  req F-Headline.1:
    given: a Node X whose content includes one or more inline refs to other Nodes
    when: I zoom into X
    then:
      - the headline (H1) renders X's text segments and ref segments in order
      - each ref renders as a link-blue span showing the target's
        current label (Obj.getLabel-resolved, falling back to the
        target's text content)
      - if a ref's target is later renamed, the headline updates
        within one tick (live, no reload)
      - the headline contains no missing-content gaps where refs used to be
  req F-Headline.empty:
    given: a Node X whose content is empty (no text, no refs)
    when: I zoom into X
    then: the headline renders the placeholder text "(empty)"
```

```mdl
feat F-Page-Header: root as editable header with parent breadcrumb   # Increment Page-Header (current)
  desc: |
    The article surface ALWAYS renders the current page node as an
    editable H1 header at the top of the page, with that node's
    children rendered as bullets below. There is no separate
    "unzoomed" view — the default page node is the outline's root,
    so the root Node IS the title node.

    F-Zoom's mechanic still applies: clicking a bullet makes that
    Node the page node, with its own children rendered below. But
    the header is no longer hidden when the page node is the root.

    The header is a real `Editor`, not a read-only display.
    Typing into it edits `pageNode.content`. F-V3 inline ref
    styling and F-V4 ref-mention all work the same as in body
    bullets. A `headlineMode` prop tells the editor to apply
    H1-large styling.

    Parent navigation (replaces F-Zoom's "← Outline" link):
    - When the page node is NOT the root, a "← {parent label}"
      breadcrumb renders above the H1. Clicking it makes the parent
      the new page node. This is recursive: click after click
      climbs up one level at a time until the page node is the
      root, where the breadcrumb is hidden.
    - The parent label is resolved via the same `Content`
      pipeline used for the headline (text + refs).

    `Graph.name` (the navigator label) is auto-synced from
    `root.content` via `useEffect` so the navigator always reflects
    the title the user sees in the header. No explicit name editing
    in the create dialog beyond the schema field default.

    Migration on first mount of an existing outline: if `root.content`
    is empty AND `outline.name` is non-empty, copy `name` into
    `root.content` (one-time) so existing outlines render their
    previous name in the new header position.
  req F-Page-Header.1:
    given: a Graph whose root Node is X
    when: the outline is opened
    then:
      - X's content is rendered as the H1 header at the top of the
        article surface (editable, with F-V3/F-V4 ref styling)
      - X's children render below the header recursively
      - no parent breadcrumb is shown (X is the root)
  req F-Page-Header.2:
    given: an outline currently displayed with page node X (root or child)
    when: I click the bullet of a child Y of X
    then:
      - Y replaces X as the page node (Y becomes the H1 header)
      - Y's children render below the header
      - the predecessor-navigation control
        (F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-list) appears at the top
        of the page reflecting Y's predecessor set, which
        includes X.
    Note: this req originally specified a single-parent
    `← {label of X}` breadcrumb. As of F-DAG.Phase3e that
    breadcrumb is SUPERSEDED by the predecessor-navigation
    control, which lists EVERY predecessor (Y can have more
    than one in a DAG). The legacy single-parent breadcrumb
    is no longer the spec'd UI.
  req F-Page-Header.3 [SUPERSEDED in F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-switch]:
    desc: |
      Originally: clicking the single-parent `← {label of P}`
      breadcrumb above the H1 swapped the page node to P
      (with the breadcrumb hiding when P was the root).

      Superseded because:
      - The legacy spec assumed every page node has at most
        one parent (a tree). F-DAG allows multi-predecessor
        Nodes, so a single `← parent` affordance can't surface
        the full set of "ways up".
      - F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-list provides the new
        affordance (a control listing every predecessor).
      - F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-switch defines the
        click-to-zoom behaviour that replaces the old single
        breadcrumb click.

      The historical implementation
      (`Article.findParent` walking `Node.children`)
      remains in code until the predecessor-nav control ships
      and the call-site can be migrated to `parentEdgesOf`.
  req F-Page-Header.4:
    given: the page node X has empty content
    when: the article is rendered
    then:
      - the H1 area renders the placeholder text "Untitled" in a
        muted (low-contrast) color, overlaid in the same baseline
        position as the editor's first character would sit.
      - the placeholder is purely visual: it is NOT stored in
        `X.content`, NOT selectable, NOT accessible to focus
        (pointer-events: none and aria-hidden) — keyboard/mouse
        events pass through to the editor underneath.
      - the placeholder remains visible WHEN THE H1 HAS FOCUS
        (per user clarification 2026-05-14: caret and overlay
        co-exist; the blinking caret IS the focus indicator,
        there is no other visual focus state such as an outline
        or ring around the H1). The caret paints at position 0
        of the empty paragraph; the placeholder "Untitled"
        appears at the same baseline, and the user sees the
        blinking caret either against / through the placeholder
        glyphs.
      - the moment the user types any character, the placeholder
        disappears (the H1 re-renders on the content mutation).
      - clearing the H1 back to empty restores the "Untitled"
        placeholder.
    Note: revised 2026-05-14. The prior version of this req
    explicitly forbade any placeholder text ("no '(empty)'
    placeholder text"). User feedback: an empty H1 with no
    visible marker reads as "the pane failed to load" rather
    than "this page has no title yet." The new placeholder is
    purely an affordance — typing replaces it — so the underlying
    "content stays in `X.content`" invariant from the original
    req is preserved.
  req F-Page-Header.7:
    given: any pane whose page node N has empty content
      (`hasContent(N) === false`) — fresh Bramble.Graph from the
      navigator, freshly-zoomed leaf in the current pane,
      Bramble.Node opened in a new pane via F-Open-Pane shift-click,
      or any other path that lands a content-empty Node as the
      page node
    when: the pane renders (initial mount OR a subsequent pageNode
      change via F-Zoom / F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-switch)
    then:
      - the H1 editor receives focus automatically; the native
        browser caret blinks at position 0 of the empty paragraph
      - per F-Page-Header.4, the "Untitled" placeholder is ALSO
        visible at the same position — the caret and the placeholder
        co-exist (the placeholder is `pointer-events: none` so the
        caret remains the active editing target). The blinking
        caret IS the focus indicator; no additional focus ring is
        drawn around the H1
      - typing immediately appears in the H1 (the caret is in
        position 0 of the paragraph; F-Caret.1 ensures the empty
        paragraph has min-width so the caret is paintable)
    given: any pane whose page node N has non-empty content
    when: the pane renders
    then:
      - the H1 editor does NOT receive autofocus — the user is
        likely arriving to read existing content, and a stolen
        focus would be disruptive. The user can click the H1 to
        edit the title at any time
    Note: revised 2026-05-14. The prior version restricted
    autofocus to the INITIAL mount of the Article only — this
    missed the "zoom into an empty sub-node" case (regular click
    on an empty bullet kept Article mounted but changed pageNode
    state, so autofocus did not re-fire and the empty pane had
    no visible cursor). The new rule keys off `hasContent(pageNode)`
    so every empty page autofocuses; every content-full page
    doesn't.
    Implementation: `Article` derives
    `autoFocusHeader = pageNode ? !hasContent(pageNode) : false`
    and forwards it to `PageHeader`. The Editor's mount effect
    depends on `block`, so re-evaluation fires on every pageNode
    change. No `useRef` initial-mount gate is used.
  req F-Page-Header.5:
    given: an outline whose root Node has empty content but `outline.name` is set
    when: the outline mounts for the first time after this feature ships
    then: `root.content` is populated with `outline.name` as a single text segment
      (one-time migration); after the migration `outline.name` continues to mirror
      `root.content`'s rendered text via auto-sync
  req F-Page-Header.6:
    given: the user edits `root.content` via the H1 editor (typing, paste, delete)
    when: the change commits to ECHO
    then: `outline.name` is updated to match the rendered text of `root.content`
      within one tick, so the navigator label stays in step
  req F-Page-Header.8:
    given: the cursor is in the H1 editor of page node P
    when: the user presses Enter
    then:
      - the H1 splits at the caret: P.content retains the BEFORE-cursor
        segments; a new Node Z is created whose `content` is the
        AFTER-cursor segments
      - Z is inserted as P's FIRST structural child (a new ChildEdge
        with `order` less than every existing child edge, i.e.
        `orderBetween(undefined, firstExistingEdge)`)
      - focus moves to Z; the caret lands at position 0 of Z (per
        F-Caret.1)
      - the F-Pending-Child.page-root row (which sits AFTER children)
        is not disturbed
    Note: this req revises the prior implicit F-Page-Header behaviour
    (the headlineMode editor swallowed Enter without splitting,
    per the now-obsolete F-Page-Header desc claim that "the header
    has no parent in the outline tree, so sibling-creation via
    Enter doesn't apply"). User clarification 2026-05-14: the H1
    behaves identically to a node-in-page; Enter on the H1
    creates a new child at position 0, mirroring how Enter on a
    body bullet creates a new sibling immediately below.
  req F-Page-Header.9:
    given: the cursor is in the H1 editor of page node P
    when: the user presses Shift+Enter
    then:
      - the keystroke is consumed as a no-op: P.content is
        unchanged; focus and caret stay in the H1; no Node
        is created; no pending row renders.
    Note: F-Shift-Enter.1 in body bullets means "render a
    pending row at the sibling slot below." For the H1, the
    page node is being viewed in a zoomed-in frame whose only
    visible sibling-flow is the H1 itself — there is no
    "sibling below" the page node visible in the current view
    (predecessor / successor navigation across the wider DAG
    is covered by F-DAG.Phase3e predecessor-nav, not by
    Shift+Enter). The H1 consumes Shift+Enter without effect,
    matching the F-Page-Header.10 (Cmd+Shift+Enter) no-op for
    the symmetric "sibling above" gesture.
  req F-Page-Header.10:
    given: the cursor is in the H1 editor of page node P
    when: the user presses Cmd+Shift+Enter (Mod+Shift+Enter)
    then:
      - the keystroke is consumed as a no-op: no Node is created;
        P.content is unchanged; focus and caret stay in the H1
    Note: F-Cmd-Shift-Enter.1 in body bullets means "create empty
    sibling ABOVE me." For the H1, "above" doesn't exist within the
    page-view scope (the predecessor-nav control covers cross-page
    upward movement; predecessors are NOT siblings). Rather than
    invent a special "create sibling of the page node" semantic,
    the H1 simply consumes Cmd+Shift+Enter without effect.
  req F-Page-Header.11:
    given: the cursor is in the H1 editor of page node P
    when: the user presses ArrowDown
    then:
      - the caret moves to the FIRST visible body bullet of the page
        (the next `[data-block-id]` element in DOM order — same
        F-Nav walk that body bullets use)
      - if the page has no body bullets and only the
        F-Pending-Child.page-root row, ArrowDown is a no-op (the
        pending-child row is not an editor target for arrow nav —
        it's promoted only by typing or click)
      - if the body's first bullet has its own children that are
        collapsed (state.expanded === false), the caret still lands
        on the first bullet (not its hidden descendants); collapsed
        children don't appear in the DOM walk per F-Nav
    given: the cursor is in the H1 editor of page node P
    when: the user presses ArrowUp
    then:
      - ArrowUp from the H1 is a no-op (nothing above the H1 within
        the page-view; the predecessor-nav control covers upward
        movement to a different page node)
  req F-Page-Header.12:
    given: the cursor is in the TOPMOST visible body bullet of the
      current page — i.e. the first row beneath the H1
    when: the user presses ArrowUp
    then:
      - the caret moves UP into the H1's editor (the previous
        `[data-block-id]` element in DOM order is the H1, since
        Article renders PageHeader before the Graph body)
      - the user is now editing the page node's content from the H1
    Note: this completes the symmetry of F-Page-Header.11 — Down
    from H1 reaches the first body bullet; Up from the first body
    bullet reaches the H1. Body-bullet ArrowUp uses the existing
    F-Nav `moveToAdjacentVisibleBlock` walk; no special-casing
    needed at the bullet end — it just works as soon as the H1's
    `[data-block-id]` is part of the DOM walk and the H1 honours
    `focusId === pageNode.id` as an autoFocus trigger.
```

```mdl
feat F-Scroll: article surface scrolls when content overflows   # Increment Scroll
  desc: |
    The article surface (the pane region rendered by
    `Article`) establishes its OWN vertical scroll region
    when the rendered page header + field group + outline tree +
    backlinks panel together exceed the pane's visible height.

    Before this increment the inner `<div className='p-4'>`
    wrapper inside `<Panel.Content>` had no overflow handling,
    while the outer `dx-container` (the panel chrome) sets
    `overflow: hidden`. Net effect: any content past the bottom
    of the visible region was silently CLIPPED — no scroll bar
    appeared and the user had no way to see or reach the rest.

    Fix: the inner article wrapper gets `h-full overflow-y-auto`
    so it fills the panel height and scrolls vertically as
    content overflows. The outer panel chrome stays
    `overflow: hidden` (its role is to scope the scroll region
    to the inner wrapper).
  req F-Scroll.1:
    given: the rendered article surface is TALLER than the pane's
      visible height (long outlines, tall typed-instance field
      groups, large backlinks panel, etc.)
    when: I view the page
    then:
      - a vertical scroll bar appears on the inner article
        wrapper.
      - I can scroll the wrapper to reveal content past the
        bottom of the visible area.
      - implementation: the wrapper carries `h-full overflow-y-auto`.
        The outer `Panel.Content` (`dx-container`) keeps
        `overflow: hidden` so the scroll region is the inner
        wrapper, not the pane chrome.
  req F-Scroll.2:
    given: the rendered article surface FITS within the pane's
      visible height
    when: I view the page
    then: no scroll bar is rendered (the `auto` track reserves no
      space when content height is less than or equal to the
      visible area).
```

```mdl
feat F-Open-Pane: shift-click bullet opens node in new pane   # Increment Open-Pane (current)
  desc: |
    Shift-clicking any bullet opens that bullet's Node in a NEW pane
    (sibling plank) to the right of the current pane. The new pane
    renders the Node as the page node per F-Page-Header — H1 header
    + child tree below + parent breadcrumb (within the pane's own
    subtree). Subsequent shift-clicks on bullets within the new pane
    open ANOTHER nested pane, recursively, as many levels deep as the
    user clicks.

    Implementation: Article provides an `OpenPaneContext` that
    invokes `LayoutOperation.Open` from `@dxos/app-toolkit` with the
    target Node id as `subject` and the current pane's `attendableId`
    as `pivotId`, so Composer's Deck plugin lands the new pane as a
    sibling to the current one. A second article surface is registered
    for `Bramble.Node` so the new pane has a renderer (the same
    `Article`, but with the Node as the pane's root rather than
    a `Graph.root`).

    The Bullet component accepts both `onClick` (zoom, regular
    click) and `onShiftClick` (open in pane).
  req F-Open-Pane.1:
    given: an outline displayed in pane P; the outline contains a Node X
      somewhere in its tree
    when: the user shift-clicks X's bullet
    then:
      - a new pane P' opens to the right of P (`pivotId` = P's
        attendableId)
      - P' renders X as the page node (H1 header) with X's children below
      - P remains visible (not replaced)
  req F-Open-Pane.recursive:
    given: pane P' is already open (rooted at Node X) and contains a child Node Y
    when: the user shift-clicks Y's bullet inside P'
    then: a further pane P'' opens to the right of P', rooted at Y. Both
      P and P' remain visible.
  req F-Open-Pane.regular-click:
    given: any bullet
    when: the user clicks (no shift) the bullet
    then: F-Zoom behaviour fires — the node becomes the page node of the
      CURRENT pane (no new pane opened).
  req F-Open-Pane.deck-disabled-fallback:
    given: Composer's deck-mode toggle is OFF (`settings.enableDeck === false`),
      i.e. the user is in single-pane mode
    when: the user shift-clicks any bullet
    then:
      - no new pane opens (Composer's `DeckContent` would immediately
        revert any multi-mode transition while deck is disabled)
      - the node becomes the page node of the CURRENT pane
        (F-Zoom-style fallback)
      - a toast is shown: title "Multi-pane disabled", description
        "Shift-click would open a new pane. Enable Deck mode in
        Settings to use this.", with an "Open Settings" action that
        invokes `SettingsOperation.Open({ plugin: 'org.dxos.plugin.deck' })`
        so the user lands directly on the Deck settings panel.
```

```mdl
feat F-Nav: Arrow-key navigation between bullets   # Increment Nav (current)
  desc: |
    The unmodified ArrowUp / ArrowDown keys move the cursor to the
    beginning of the previous / next *visible* bullet. "Visible" means
    the Node would be rendered given the current expand/collapse state
    of all ancestors — independent of scroll position. If the target is
    outside the viewport, the page scrolls just enough to reveal it.
    Single-line bullets don't need within-paragraph line navigation, so
    arrow keys always cross to the adjacent bullet.

    Implementation note: the navigation walks the rendered DOM
    (`[data-block-id]` elements in document order) rather than reaching
    into the ECHO tree. Because collapsed children aren't mounted, DOM
    order naturally matches the visible-tree order. Both arrow commands
    always return true to ProseMirror's keymap so the browser never
    scrolls the page as a side-effect of an edge-case no-op.

  req F-Nav.up:
    given: cursor is in Node X, and Node Y is the bullet immediately
      above X in the rendered tree (visible, regardless of scroll position)
    when: user presses ArrowUp
    then: caret moves to the start of Y; the page scrolls to bring Y
      into view if it was off-screen.
  req F-Nav.up.edge:
    given: cursor is in Node X, and there is no visible Node above X
    when: user presses ArrowUp
    then: caret does not move; the page does not scroll.
  req F-Nav.down:
    given: cursor is in Node X, and Node Y is the bullet immediately
      below X in the rendered tree (visible, regardless of scroll position)
    when: user presses ArrowDown
    then: caret moves to the start of Y; the page scrolls to bring Y
      into view if it was off-screen.
  req F-Nav.down.edge:
    given: cursor is in Node X, and there is no visible Node below X
    when: user presses ArrowDown
    then: caret does not move; the page does not scroll.
  req F-Nav.pending-child:
    given: cursor is in Node X, AND X is an expanded leaf (no real
      children) so F-Pending-Child.expand has rendered a pending-
      child placeholder editable directly below X's row in the
      indented child column
    when: user presses ArrowDown
    then:
      - the caret moves into the pending-child's editable area
        (the `.block-pending-child-editable` `<div contenteditable>`
        belonging to X). The DOM-walking F-Nav helper now treats
        `.block-pending-child-editable` as a focusable target
        alongside `[data-block-id]` elements, so the pending-child
        is reachable in document order from X.
      - the pending-child is focused via `editable.focus()` — NOT
        through `setFocusId`. No `Bramble.makeNode` call fires. No
        `db.add`. The pending-child remains a visual placeholder;
        navigating into it does NOT cause a real Node to exist.
      - the user can subsequently type a character to promote the
        pending-child to a real Node per F-Pending-Child.type, or
        press ArrowUp / ArrowDown to navigate further without
        leaving any persisted state behind.
    given: cursor is inside a pending-child editable
    when: the user presses ArrowUp or ArrowDown
    then:
      - the focus moves to the prev/next focusable element in DOM
        order (a real Node's editor or another pending-child),
        identical to F-Nav.up / F-Nav.down's walk but starting from
        the pending-child element instead of a `[data-block-id]`.
      - when MentionPicker is open inside the pending-child (the
        user typed `@`), ArrowUp/ArrowDown are intercepted by the
        picker's window-capture handler (F-4.7) and used for picker
        nav. The pending-child's own ArrowUp/Down handler only
        fires when no picker is intercepting.
    Note: F-Nav.pending-child closes a UX gap reported 2026-05-14:
    pressing ArrowDown from a leaf with a visible pending-child
    skipped past the placeholder to the next sibling, leaving the
    pending-child unreachable via keyboard. Implementation: the
    F-Nav helper queries both `[data-block-id]` AND
    `.block-pending-child-editable`, walks the merged list in
    document order, and routes focus through `setFocusId` (real
    Nodes) or `editable.focus()` (pending-child) accordingly.
    The pending-child receives focus but is NOT promoted — the
    promote path is gated on the editable's `input` event, which
    only fires when the user types a character.
```

```mdl
feat F-Caret: cursor visible after focus      # Increment Caret (current)
  desc: |
    Whenever a Node's editor receives focus — by mouse click, by being
    the new sibling created via Enter / Shift+Enter / Cmd+Shift+Enter,
    or by ArrowUp / ArrowDown navigation (F-Nav) — a visible caret
    renders inside the editor at a paintable position.

    Current-behavior gaps that prompted this spec:
    - Empty bullets render `<p><br/></p>`. The earlier Node
      wrapper (`[&_p]:inline-block`) made the empty `<p>` an
      inline-block element. The trailing break inside an inline-block
      `<p>` ends up rendering as TWO line-heights (one before the BR,
      one after) — and Safari paints a caret in EACH valid line
      position, producing two stacked blinking cursors. (Chromium
      paints only one but the rectangle is still 2-line tall.) Fix:
      keep `[&_.ProseMirror]:inline-block` on the wrapper but stop
      forcing `<p>` to inline-block. Inside the inline-block
      `.ProseMirror`, the `<p>` is plain block-level, the BR creates
      a single line, and the empty paragraph is exactly one line
      tall — one caret. The `caret-fix-plugin` continues to tag
      empty paragraphs with the `block-empty-paragraph` class so
      `block-editor.css` can apply `min-width: 1rem` (patterned on
      Tana's `.editable:empty { display: inline-block; min-width:
      1rem }` rule). The min-width gives the otherwise-0-wide empty
      paragraph somewhere for the native caret to paint.
    - F-Nav's focus helper called
      `range.selectNodeContents(editable); range.collapse(true)`,
      which put the DOM selection at `editable, 0` — BEFORE the
      `<p>`. That position has no caret rect even for non-empty
      paragraphs. Fix: F-Nav now routes focus through the
      `setFocusId` / `autoFocus` mechanism used by sibling-creation;
      the `Editor` then sets PM selection at
      `TextSelection.atStart(doc)` and calls `view.focus()`, so PM
      places the caret inside the first text node (or inside the
      empty paragraph at the start position the browser can paint).
    - Mouse-click on an empty bullet hit the same empty-paragraph
      issue as Enter family; it is fixed by the same layout change.
  req F-Caret.1:
    given: there is a Node X
    when: X's editor is selected for focus (by click, sibling creation,
      or ArrowUp/ArrowDown navigation)
    then:
      - a single visible cursor renders inside X's editor (the native
        browser caret in BOTH the text-content and the empty-paragraph
        cases — the empty case is enabled by the `min-width: 1rem` rule
        in `block-editor.css`)
      - the cursor is positioned as close as possible to where the
        selection occurred (click → click target; sibling creation →
        start of new bullet; arrow nav → start of target bullet)
      - if X has any text content, the cursor is positioned inside that
        content (DOM selection anchor is a Text node, not the editor
        DIV or paragraph element)
      - the empty-paragraph rectangle is exactly one line tall (no
        Safari double-cursor)
  req F-Caret.3:
    given: a pending-child row is focused; the user types a single
      character (the first character to enter that pending-child's
      editable area)
    when: the pending-child's `handleInput` fires `onPromote(typedText)`
      and a new `Bramble.Node` is created whose `content` is the typed
      character, then the new bullet's `Editor` receives autofocus via
      the `focusId === block.id` mechanism
    then:
      - the caret lands at the END of the new bullet's content, AFTER
        the typed character (not at position 0, which is BEFORE the
        typed character).
      - subsequent keystrokes append normally: typing `1234` into a
        pending-child produces a bullet whose content is exactly
        `"1234"` — the `1` from the pending-child promote, followed by
        `2`, `3`, `4` typed into the new bullet's editor.
    Note: implemented as the `autoFocusAtEnd` boolean prop on
    `Editor`, paired with a `focusAtEnd` boolean in `Article`'s focus
    state. The pending-child promote path (both the row-level one in
    `Node.tsx` and the page-root one in `Graph.tsx`) calls
    `setFocusIdAtEnd(newChild.id)` instead of `setFocusId(newChild.id)`
    so the new bullet's F-Caret useEffect uses
    `TextSelection.atEnd(doc)` instead of `TextSelection.atStart(doc)`.
    All other focus paths (Enter-split, F-Nav arrow nav, F-Page-Header.7
    empty-page autofocus) continue to use `atStart` because their
    semantics call for the caret at the new bullet's beginning. The
    pre-fix bug surfaced as the user typing `1234` and seeing `2341`
    appear instead — the `1` got promoted into a new bullet, the
    cursor landed at position 0 of `"1"`, and the `2`, `3`, `4` then
    inserted at position 0 each time, producing `2341`.
  req F-Caret.2:
    given: any Bramble ProseMirror editor — the H1 PageHeader's editor
      (per F-Page-Header), a body bullet's editor (per F-V1), the
      F-Pending-Child row's editable, or the headlineMode editor used
      for tagged-node titles
    when: the editor is focused (by any means: real mouse click,
      programmatic `view.focus()`, F-Nav arrow navigation,
      sibling-creation autofocus per F-Caret.1, F-Page-Header.7
      empty-page autofocus)
    then:
      - the blinking native browser caret is the ONLY visual focus
        indicator. There is NO outline, ring, border, halo, or
        background-color change drawn around the focused editor.
      - the rule applies regardless of how focus arrived: Chrome's
        default `:focus-visible` outline (a 1px outline in the OS
        accent color) is suppressed by the
        `.ProseMirror:focus, .ProseMirror:focus-visible { outline:
        none }` rule in `block-editor.css`.
      - the F-Page-Header.4 "Untitled" placeholder, when shown,
        remains visible alongside the blinking caret (the placeholder
        is `pointer-events: none` + `aria-hidden`; the caret paints at
        the editor's first-character position regardless of the
        placeholder overlay).
    Note: captured 2026-05-14 from user clarification. Prior to the
    `block-editor.css` rule, Chrome painted a 1-pixel orange/amber
    outline (`outline: rgb(229, 151, 0) auto 1px`) around every
    focused ProseMirror editor — the OS accent color happens to be
    amber on the developer's machine. Per the clarified visual:
    "the blinking cursor is the visual indicator for focus … there is
    no other visual indicator of focus (e.g. there is no orange
    bounding box)." The CSS suppression is plugin-scoped (lives in
    `block-editor.css`) so other Composer surfaces keep their
    focus rings; only Bramble's outliner editors are bare.
```

```mdl
feat F-V6 [DEPRECATED in F-DAG.Phase3a]: Reference-only Node
  desc: |
    DEPRECATED. This feature existed because the pre-DAG outline could
    only place a Node under ONE parent. To make a Node "appear in
    another place" the user had to create a separate wrapper Node
    whose entire `content` was a single ref segment pointing at the
    real Node. Such a wrapper was styled with a dashed outer ring
    and was non-interactive (no zoom) to signal "this is a stand-in,
    not original content."

    With F-DAG.Phase3a.add-existing-via-picker, the same use case is
    served by a structural Edge to an existing Node — the SAME
    Node appears in multiple places in the outline, with its NORMAL
    solid bullet and the multi-parent badge (F-DAG.Phase3e). No
    wrapper is created. No dashed ring.

    Consequences of the deprecation:
    - The `isReferenceOnlyBlock` detector, the `referenceOnly` flag
      on the Bullet component, and the dashed-ring rendering path
      are removed.
    - Reqs that depended on F-V6 (F-Zoom.reference-only,
      F-Open-Pane.ref-only, F-6.Phase3b.wrapper-not-reference-only)
      are likewise removed.
    - Inline ref segments INSIDE content (alongside text) are not
      affected — those are F-V3 mentions and continue to render
      with link-blue styling. F-V6 specifically governed Nodes
      whose content was ONLY a ref, which post-DAG is not a state
      reachable by any user action.
    - Legacy data: pre-DAG outlines may contain Nodes in the
      F-V6 state. After this deprecation those Nodes render
      with a normal bullet and show the target's label (via the
      ref nodeview already in F-V3). The visual "this is a
      reference" distinction is lost; the structural meaning is
      preserved. A subsequent migration could rewrite such
      Nodes as Edges if desired.
```

```mdl
feat F-V3: Inline ref styling                 # Increment V3 (current)
  desc: |
    Inline ref nodes render with link-style typography — distinct color
    (link blue), no underline by default — so users can scan a paragraph
    and see at a glance which spans are graph references vs. plain text.
  req F-V3.1: the rendered span for a ref node carries a class that applies link-blue color and no underline.
  req F-V3.2: hovering the ref shows a subtle affordance (cursor change or underline-on-hover) without changing layout.
```

```mdl
feat F-V4: Inline backlink count badge        # Increment V4 (current)
  desc: |
    Each Node that is referenced by other Nodes displays a small inline
    count badge to the right of its row. Counts are produced by the
    useBacklinks hook and propagated via React context to Node.

    Live-update guarantee: the count must refresh as soon as the ref node
    renders in the source Node — not after the user navigates away or
    presses Enter. Implementation note: a `db.query(...).subscribe` only
    fires on result-SET changes, not on field-level mutations to existing
    Nodes. The hook subscribes to each Node via `Obj.subscribe` and
    re-binds those subs whenever the Node set changes. This ensures
    that `Obj.update(sourceNode, m => m.content = [...with ref])`
    triggers a recompute synchronously, and the badge re-renders on the
    next paint frame.
  req F-V4.live:
    when: source Node X is mutated to include an inline ref to target Node Y (via @-mention picker, paste, programmatic update, or any other path)
    then:
      - within one paint frame after X's content is committed, Y's count badge increments by one
      - the user is NOT required to leave X, press Enter, navigate away, or reload
  req F-V4.placement:
    when: a Node has at least one incoming ref
    then: the count badge is rendered immediately to the right of the Node's text content (not pushed to the row's far edge); when text wraps to multiple lines, the badge sits at the end of the last line on which the text fits, rather than at a fixed right margin.
  desc: |
    Each Node that is referenced by other Nodes displays a small inline
    count badge after its content showing how many bullets (or other ECHO
    objects) point at it. Powered by db.query.referencedBy(); the badge
    updates live as refs are added or removed. Clicking the badge opens
    the linked-references panel from F-5 (when that lands).
  req F-V4.1:
    when: at least one inline ref points at a Node
    then: a small "[N]" badge renders inline after the bullet's text content.
  req F-V4.2: the badge count reflects live state (re-runs on referencedBy changes).
  req F-V4.3:
    when: no inline refs point at a Node
    then: no badge is rendered.
  note: |
    Visual reference: in Tana, the badge appears as a small bordered "1"
    next to the parent node. The same affordance reveals the backlinks
    list on click.
```

```mdl
feat F-4b: Live rename propagation   # Increment 4b (current)
  desc: |
    The RefNodeView subscribes to the resolved target via Obj.subscribe at
    mount and re-binds whenever its `dxn` attribute changes. Any field
    mutation on the target — including a rename — fires the subscription
    callback and refreshes the rendered label, with no local edit required.
  req F-4b.1:
    when: a target object's label changes (Obj.update m.name = ...) anywhere
    then: every visible inline ref pointing at that target re-renders with the new label
  req F-4b.2:
    when: an inline ref's dxn attribute changes (e.g., re-targeting in a future increment)
    then: the previous target's subscription is released and the new target's is bound
  req F-4b.3:
    when: the editor unmounts (block re-parented, outline closed)
    then: all ref subscriptions are released via NodeView.destroy
```

```mdl
feat F-5: Backlinks panel       # Increment 5 (current)
  desc: |
    A "Linked references" panel renders below the Graph in every Node
    Outline. It lists Nodes elsewhere in the database whose inline ref
    segments point at any Node in this outline's tree (or the outline
    itself). Self-references (a bullet inside the outline mentioning
    another bullet inside the same outline) are excluded — those are
    visible in the tree itself.
    Implementation note: ECHO's referencedBy() introspects typed Ref
    fields, but our refs live inside StructuredContent union segments
    which referencedBy doesn't traverse, so the panel performs a manual
    scan across all Nodes. A typed `Node.references` sidecar can replace
    the scan in a perf increment.
  req F-5.1: a Linked-references panel renders below the Graph on any Graph article surface.
  req F-5.2:
    when: at least one Node elsewhere has a content RefSpan whose target is in this outline's tree
    then: that source Node is listed with its label and the target's label.
  req F-5.3:
    when: no incoming refs exist
    then: the panel renders nothing (no empty header).
  req F-5.4: panel updates within one tick of the outline being mutated locally.
```

```mdl
feat F-6: Tags as ECHO-typed instances       # Increment 6 (in progress, Phase 2 current)
  desc: |
    Tana-style supertags, but leveraging DXOS ECHO types directly
    instead of inventing a parallel TagDef structure. Each "tag" IS
    an existing ECHO type from the database — applying `#Task` to a
    Node creates a new `Task` instance and links it to the Node via
    `Node.supertags`. Field values for a tag live ON the linked
    typed instance (so editing the Task's `title` edits the Task, not
    the Node).

    Phase split:

    - Phase 1 (current): typing `#` opens a picker showing every
      tag-ready ECHO type registered in the space (per
      `F-Supertag.types-shown` — every non-Relation, non-System
      schema from `db.schemaRegistry`, minus plugin-blocks's own
      scaffolding). Selecting a type creates a new instance via
      `Obj.make`, `db.add`s it, and appends `Ref(instance)` to
      `Node.supertags`. The applied tag renders as a chip inline
      at the end of the bullet text; the new instance's label is
      seeded from the node's text per `F-Supertag.title-sync`. NO
      field editing yet (that's Phase 2).

    - Phase 2 (current): when a tagged bullet is expanded, render
      a field-editor group between the bullet's content row and its
      children. The group lists the linked instance's schema-derived
      fields (text, number, enum, ref) with inline editors; edits
      flow into the linked instance via `Obj.update`, NOT into the
      Node itself.

      Enum-style fields (Schema.Literal unions or `singleSelect`
      PropertyMeta — e.g. `Task.priority`, `Task.status`) are
      MATERIALIZED as Nodes per the project's `R-No-Echo-Changes`
      rule: each declared literal value gets one per-space wrapper
      Node whose `tagOption: {typename, fieldName, literal}` marker
      pins its identity, and whose `content` is the user-visible
      (renameable) label. The typed instance still stores the
      schema-declared literal string (`task.priority === 'high'`);
      the FieldGroup renders that value as an inline pill whose label
      comes from the live option Node — so a rename of "high" → "Top"
      in the user's space propagates to every Task with that value
      without any schema edit.

      Arrays and nested-struct fields are rendered as
      count-or-placeholder for now and deferred to a later sub-phase.

    - Phase 3 (current — consolidated in `feat F-Supertag` below):
      supertag nodes are first-class outline entities under the
      Schema system node; chip-click navigates to the supertag
      node for the typename; a separate `↗` control on the chip
      opens the typed instance in its dedicated Composer surface
      (e.g. AgentArticle). The integrated supertag model — which
      ECHO types qualify, eager + live materialization of
      supertag nodes under Schema, per-(instance, supertag)
      uniqueness, and bi-directional node-content ↔ instance-label
      sync — is spec'd in `feat F-Supertag`. Sub-phases:

        - Phase 3b (current): query node — a child Node of the
          supertag node that lists every ECHO instance of the
          typename (wrapper Node or not) and lets the user
          click an entry to promote a wrapper Node into the
          per-space Library (parallels Tana's Library node).
          Find-or-create per `F-Supertag.uniqueness`.

    Implementation note: `Node.supertags` is already declared as
    `Ref<Obj.Unknown>[]` so no schema migration is needed for
    Phase 1. The placeholder `TagDef` ECHO type that was reserved
    for an alternative design is removed in Phase 1.
  req F-6.Phase1.trigger:
    given: I am editing a node
    when: I type the tag prefix character (`#`) anywhere in the paragraph
    then:
      - a tag picker opens adjacent to the cursor (anchored at the `#` position),
        flipping above the line if there isn't room below — same
        positioning rules as F-4's mention picker.
      - the picker shows one entry per tag-ready ECHO type
        registered in the space (every non-Relation, non-System
        schema from `db.schemaRegistry`, minus plugin-blocks's
        own scaffolding; see `F-Supertag.types-shown`), each
        labelled by the type's title annotation.
      - typing more chars after `#` filters the picker by
        case-insensitive substring on the type title.
      - pressing Escape closes the picker; the `#` and query remain as
        plain text.
      Note: the prior version of this req required `#` at start of
      paragraph or after whitespace (matching `@` for refs); user
      feedback called out that `#` should fire anywhere (Tana
      convention), so the surrounding-context check was dropped.
  req F-6.Phase1.escape:
    given: I am editing a node and have typed the tag prefix character (`#`)
    when: I press the Escape key
    then:
      - the `#` character remains in the node's text (the editor is
        not modified)
      - the tag picker closes
      - the cursor stays in the node, immediately after the `#`
      - subsequent keystrokes (typing more characters in the query
        position of the same `#`) do NOT re-open the picker — the
        picker stays dismissed for that specific `#` until the cursor
        moves before it or the `#` itself is deleted.
  req F-6.Phase1.keyboard-nav:
    given: the tag picker is open with one or more entries listed
    when: I press ArrowDown, ArrowUp, or Enter
    then:
      - ArrowDown advances the active item by one, wrapping from the
        last entry to the first.
      - ArrowUp moves the active item back by one, wrapping from the
        first entry to the last.
      - the active item renders with a visible highlight (distinct
        from the hover background) so the keyboard cursor is
        always findable.
      - moving the mouse over an entry sets the active item to that
        entry — keyboard and mouse share a single highlight.
      - if the active item is outside the picker's scroll viewport
        after a nav move, the list scrolls so the active item is
        in view.
      - Enter commits the active item (same path as a mouse click)
        and closes the picker; the editor does NOT split the bullet
        (Enter is consumed while the picker is open). Same applies
        when the active item is the first/only entry just after
        opening — the user can type `#ta<Enter>` to pick `#Task`
        without reaching for the mouse.
      - typing more characters after `#` re-filters the list; the
        active item resets to the first entry (so the highlight
        never points outside the now-smaller list).
      - when the picker filters down to zero entries, Enter is a
        no-op (the picker stays open showing "No matching tag
        types"); Escape still closes per `F-6.Phase1.escape`.
      - Shift+Enter and Cmd+Shift+Enter (and any meta-Enter chord)
        are N/A while the picker is open — the picker swallows
        them as no-ops (preventDefault + stopPropagation, picker
        stays open, no sibling created). Rationale (user
        clarification 2026-05-14): a sibling-creation gesture
        mid-`#`-query would silently insert a new bullet in the
        outline while the user's attention is on the picker,
        which is disorienting. The user must dismiss the picker
        (Escape, click outside, or commit a tag) before
        sibling-creation gestures take effect.
      - Cmd+ArrowUp / Cmd+ArrowDown (F-V2.8 / F-V2.9
        collapse/expand) are NOT intercepted — bare-arrow nav is
        for the picker's active item only, modified arrows fall
        through to the editor's keymap.
  req F-6.Phase1.apply:
    given: the tag picker is open with a selectable type
    when: user clicks an entry
    then:
      - the `#`-and-query range is removed from the bullet text
        (no inline marker — the chip is rendered separately).
      - a new instance of the selected type is created via `Obj.make`
        and added to the same database as the Node.
      - `Node.supertags` is updated to append `Ref(instance)`.
      - the new instance's label is seeded from the node's
        current text via the outliner-originated path of
        `F-Supertag.title-sync` (`Obj.setLabel(instance, nodeText)`
        immediately after `Obj.make`).
      - the picker closes; cursor returns to the bullet text.
  req F-6.Phase1.chip:
    given: a Node has one or more entries in `supertags`
    when: the Node renders
    then:
      - one tag chip renders for each supertag, inline at the end of
        the bullet's text content (after the editor, before the
        backlinks badge).
      - the chip displays the typename's title (e.g. "Task",
        "Person", "Organization") prefixed with `#`.
      - chip styling: link-blue background tint, rounded, small text.
        Different colour per typename (deterministic hash) optional.
  req F-6.Phase2.group:
    given: a Node has one or more entries in `supertags`
    when: the Node's row is expanded (state.expanded undefined or true)
    then:
      - one field group renders per supertag, inside the same indented
        column as the Node's children, BEFORE any child Node rows
        and before the pending-child row.
      - each group is headed by the supertag's typename title (small,
        amber, uppercase) so multiple tags on the same Node can be
        told apart visually.
      - field groups render only when the Node is expanded; they
        hide when the Node is collapsed.
  req F-6.Phase2.fields:
    given: a field group is rendering for a linked typed instance
    when: the group enumerates the schema's property signatures
    then:
      - each property surfaces as a labeled row (label : editor) with
        the label sourced from the property's title annotation (or
        the property name as fallback).
      - the auto-injected `id` property is hidden.
      - kinds rendered: `string` (text input), `number` (number
        input), enum (`Schema.Literal` union or `singleSelect`
        PropertyMeta — see F-6.Phase2.enum*), and `Ref` (read-only —
        displays the target's label via `getDisplayLabel`).
      - array and nested-struct properties show as a muted placeholder
        ("N items" / "—") and are not editable in Phase 2.
  req F-6.Phase2.persistence:
    given: a field editor commits a change
    when: the user blurs the input (text/number) or selects an
      option (enum)
    then:
      - the linked typed instance is mutated via `Obj.update` and the
        new value is written under the property name; clearing the
        value (empty string or "—" option) deletes the property so
        optional fields round-trip cleanly.
      - the Node is NOT modified; field values do not live on the
        Node.
      - the field group reads the live ECHO instance via `useObject`
        so concurrent mutations propagate without remount.
  req F-6.Phase2.coexist:
    given: a Node is a leaf (no children) AND has at least one
      supertag
    when: the Node is expanded
    then:
      - the supertag field group renders FIRST
      - the pending-child row renders BELOW it
      - typing into the pending-child row promotes a new child Node
        as in F-Pending-Child (the field group remains visible).
  req F-6.Phase2.enum.materialize:
    given: an enum-style field on a tagged instance (Schema.Literal
      union or `singleSelect` PropertyMeta) takes a value V
    when: the FieldGroup renders that field for the first time in the
      space
    then:
      - the plugin queries the space for a Node whose `tagOption`
        marker equals `(typename, fieldName, V)`.
      - if none exists, the plugin creates exactly one such Node,
        seeded with the schema-declared option title as its `content`
        and `tagOption: {typename, fieldName, literal: V}` as the
        stable identity, and `db.add`s it.
      - subsequent renders of any Task with the same value resolve to
        the SAME Node — there is never more than one option Node
        per (typename, fieldName, literal) tuple in a given space.
      - the schema is NOT edited, no ECHO change is required, and
        the typed instance still stores V as a string (per
        rule R-No-Echo-Changes).
  req F-6.Phase2.enum.pill:
    given: an enum field on a tagged instance has value V and the
      matching option Node exists in the space
    when: the FieldGroup renders that field
    then:
      - the value renders as an inline pill whose label is the option
        Node's `content` (NOT the schema-declared title).
      - if the user renames the option Node's content from "high" to
        "Top", every Task whose `priority === 'high'` reflects the
        new label live without remount (subscribes to the option
        Node via `useObject`).
      - the pill is clickable; click opens the enum picker (see
        F-6.Phase2.enum.picker).
  req F-6.Phase2.enum.picker:
    given: an enum field on a tagged instance is being edited
    when: the user clicks the pill (or the "—" placeholder when
      unset)
    then:
      - a dropdown picker opens listing every schema-declared literal
        for that field, plus a leading "—" row for clearing the
        value.
      - each row's displayed label is the matching option Node's
        `content` when materialized, else the schema-declared option
        title.
      - the currently-selected row is highlighted.
      - selecting a row commits the chosen literal (or undefined for
        "—") to the typed instance and closes the picker; clicking
        outside or pressing Escape dismisses without changing the
        value.
  req F-6.Phase1.create-instance:
    given: I am editing a node and select a tag from the picker
    when: the tag is applied
    then:
      - a new instance of the corresponding ECHO type is created
        via `Obj.make` (with `initialPropsForTag` filling any
        required fields) and added to the same database as the
        Node.
      - `Node.supertags` is updated to append `Ref(instance)`.
      - any subsequent edits to the rendered field group are
        persisted on the linked instance (Phase 2 wiring); the
        Node itself is not mutated for field-value writes.
    Note: this req formalises behaviour that's been in place since
    Phase 1 (`F-6.Phase1.apply`) and Phase 2 (the `.persistence`
    req) — captured here as a single user-visible scenario.
  req F-6.Phase3.tag-node.label:
    given: a chip is rendering for typename T and the matching tag
      Node exists in the space
    when: the chip renders
    then:
      - the chip's displayed label is the tag Node's `content`
        (NOT the schema-declared title), prefixed with `#`.
      - if the user renames the tag Node's content from "Task" to
        "Job", every chip with typename T reflects the new label
        live without remount (subscribes via `useObject`).
      - until the tag Node has materialized, the chip falls back to
        the schema-declared title so the chip never renders blank.
  req F-6.Phase3.tag-node.header:
    given: I am zoomed into a tag node (page node has `tagTypename`)
    when: the page renders
    then:
      - the H1 row shows a decorative amber-tinted `#` glyph on the
        left, followed by the tag Node's editable name.
      - the `#` glyph is purely visual: not part of the Node's
        `content`, not editable, not selectable.
      - editing the name (via the inline Editor) updates the
        tag Node's content; every `#`-prefixed chip referencing
        this typename reflects the new label live.
  req F-6.Phase3.system-node.readonly:
    given: I am zoomed into a system node (page node has
      `systemNode` set — e.g. Schema, Library)
    when: the page renders
    then:
      - the H1 displays the Node's content (e.g. "Schema") as
        plain non-editable text, styled muted to signal its
        special role.
      - no inline Editor is mounted for the header (so it
        can't be typed into).
      - the children list and bullet-level interactions are
        otherwise unchanged.
  req F-6.Phase3.regular-node.header-editable:
    given: I am zoomed into any non-system Node (regular bullet,
      tag node, option Node from Phase 2)
    when: I edit the H1
    then:
      - the Node's `content` is updated in place via the inline
        Editor (current F-Page-Header behaviour).
      - tag Nodes: the displayed name changes; chips elsewhere
        update live.
      - option Nodes (Phase 2): the displayed label changes;
        every typed instance whose literal matches reflects the
        new label live.
  req F-6.Phase3.picker.uses-renamed-label:
    given: in this space a tag has been renamed (e.g. the user
      changed the `#Task` tag Node's `content` from "Task" to
      "Job")
    when: I type `#` in any bullet and the TagPicker opens
    then:
      - each row in the picker displays the per-space tag Node's
        `content` as the label (e.g. "Job"), NOT the schema-declared
        title ("Task").
      - for typenames whose tag Node hasn't been materialized yet
        in this space, the picker falls back to the schema-declared
        title so the row still renders.
      - the in-picker substring filter matches against the displayed
        label (so typing "Jo" matches the row labelled "Job").
      - the underlying selection still creates a `Ref` to a new
        instance of the schema-declared typename — the picker only
        affects what the user sees, never the stored identity.
  req F-6.Phase3.tag-node.children-are-queries:
    given: a Node carries the `tagTypename` marker (it is a tag
      node)
    when: examining its outline children
    then:
      - the only Node kind allowed as a child of a tag node is a
        query node (a Node whose payload is "all instances of this
        typename"). Regular bullets, sub-tags, option Nodes, and
        free-form content are NOT permitted as children of a tag
        node.
      - Phase 3b materialises the query child at tag-Node
        creation time (see F-6.Phase3b.query-node.materialize);
        future increments will additionally reject Tab/Enter
        operations that would add a non-query child.
  req F-6.Phase3b.query-node.materialize:
    given: a tag Node is being created for typename T
    when: `createTagBlock` runs
    then:
      - a single child Node is also created carrying
        `queryRef: { typename: T }` (and no `content`).
      - the new query Node is added to the database and its `Ref`
        is appended to the tag Node's `children` array.
      - subsequent renames or use of the tag DO NOT create more
        query children; the one created at materialization stays.
  req F-6.Phase3b.query-node.render:
    given: a Node carries `queryRef.typename === T`
    when: the Node renderer encounters it
    then:
      - the standard bullet + content + children layout is REPLACED
        with `<QueryNodeView>`.
      - the query view lists every ECHO instance in the space whose
        typename equals T, regardless of whether each has a
        wrapping Node.
      - the list subscribes to both `Bramble.Node` query and the
        instance query so newly-created Nodes / instances refresh
        the rows without remount.
      - each row's label comes from the wrapper Node's
        `getDisplayLabel` when one exists, falling back to the raw
        instance's `getDisplayLabel`.
      - rows whose instance has NO wrapper Node render a dashed
        bullet glyph and a `no wrapper` hint badge.
  req F-6.Phase3b.promote:
    given: a query row whose instance has no wrapper Node
    when: the user clicks the row
    then:
      - the per-space Library Node is found-or-created
        (`systemNode: 'library'`, content: "Library"). Exactly one
        Library Node per space.
      - a fresh wrapper Node is created with:
        - `content`: a single ref segment pointing at the instance
          (so the bullet's display label tracks the instance's
          label live).
        - `supertags`: `[Ref(instance)]` (so the Phase-2 FieldGroup
          attaches and renders the typed fields).
      - the wrapper is `db.add`-ed and its `Ref` is appended to
        `Library.children`.
      - the current pane zooms to the new wrapper (cross-tree
        fallback covers the case where Library isn't in the
        current pane's outline tree).
      - the query view re-renders; the row's `no wrapper` hint
        disappears and the row now navigates to the wrapper on
        subsequent clicks.
  req F-6.Phase3b.promote.reuse:
    given: a query row whose instance ALREADY has a wrapper Node
      (e.g. the user previously promoted it, or the wrapper was
      created via the apply-tag flow on some other bullet)
    when: the user clicks the row
    then:
      - NO new wrapper is created.
      - the pane zooms to the existing wrapper (the one indexed
        in `buildWrapperIndex`).
      - exactly one wrapper Node exists per instance in the
        space; subsequent clicks remain idempotent.
```

```mdl
feat F-Supertag: typed-instance supertag model    # Increment Supertag (current)
  desc: |
    Consolidated specification of the supertag system. Earlier F-6
    Phase 1 / 2 / 3 reqs describe the picker, FieldGroup, and per-
    tag-node behaviours; this feat sits on top and captures the
    integrated model: which ECHO types are surfaced, how the per-
    typename supertag nodes materialise, the uniqueness invariant
    between a typed instance and its associated outline node, the
    title-sync between node text and instance label, and the
    chip's two navigation targets (the supertag node browse view
    vs. the typed instance's dedicated Composer surface).

    Terminology (canonical — supersedes the earlier "tag Node" /
    "wrapper Node" / "Node tagged with #X" vocabulary used in
    older F-6 prose):

    - **node** — an outline entity. What the rest of this spec
      calls a Node.
    - **node tagged with supertag** — a node whose `supertags`
      array carries a Ref to a typed ECHO instance. The Ref's
      typename IS the supertag.
    - **supertag node** — the singleton-per-(space, typename)
      Node carrying `tagTypename: <typename>` and a `queryRef`
      child. Hosts the browse view (every node tagged with the
      supertag, UNION every instance of the type that has no
      associated node) and owns the renameable label every chip
      with this typename displays.

    The five subsumed F-6 Phase 3 sub-reqs (`tag-node.materialize`,
    `tag-node.click`, `tag-node.shift-click`,
    `tag-node.schema-parent`, `all-echo-types`) have been removed;
    their behaviour is captured here.
  req F-Supertag.types-shown:
    given: I am editing a node in a space and open the `#` picker
    when: the picker mounts, OR the schemaRegistry changes while
      it's open
    then:
      - the picker lists every ECHO type registered with
        `db.schemaRegistry` at `location: ['database', 'runtime']`,
        MINUS Relations (`Type.relation`-flavoured schemas —
        `Edge` and friends, edges between entities rather
        than entities the user can tag a bullet with), MINUS
        plugin-blocks's own scaffolding (`Bramble.Node`,
        `Bramble.Graph`). The remainder is offered —
        no allowlist.
      - the list resolves via `useTagTypes(db)`, which holds a
        live subscription to
        `db.schemaRegistry.query({location: ['database', 'runtime']}).subscribe(...)`.
        Schemas registered in the space after the picker opens
        surface in the picker without remount; schemas removed
        from the registry drop out within one tick.
      - each entry's display label resolves through the per-space
        supertag node's renameable content (the supertag node
        exists for every qualifying type per
        `F-Supertag.eager-materialization`), falling back to the
        schema's `Title` annotation, then to the typename's
        last-segment capitalised (`org.dxos.type.person` →
        `Person`).
  req F-Supertag.eager-materialization:
    given: a Composer Space whose block-outliner article surface
      is mounted
    when: the surface mounts, AND any time the schemaRegistry
      emits a change thereafter
    then:
      - the plugin subscribes to
        `db.schemaRegistry.query({location: ['database', 'runtime']})`.
      - on each event (the initial fire plus subsequent change
        events), the plugin enumerates the types passing the
        `F-Supertag.types-shown` filter.
      - for each qualifying typename the plugin find-or-creates a
        supertag node via the existing `createTagBlock` helper
        (which also materialises the query-node child per
        `F-6.Phase3b.query-node.materialize`).
      - for each supertag node the plugin ensures a structural
        `Edge` from the per-space Schema system node (the
        existing `systemNode: 'schema'` Node, find-or-created
        on first use) to the supertag node. Existing edges are
        not duplicated.
      - operations are idempotent — re-runs against an unchanged
        registry produce zero writes.
      - schema removals leave the corresponding supertag nodes
        and their structural edges in place. Cleanup policy for
        orphaned supertag nodes is a deferred concern.
  req F-Supertag.uniqueness:
    given: an ECHO instance `X` of type `T` exists in a Composer
      Space
    when: any user gesture or migration touches a node's
      `supertags` array (the `#`-picker apply, promote-from-
      query, future drag-and-drop, etc.)
    then:
      - invariant: at most ONE node in the space has a
        `supertags` Ref targeting `X` whose typename is `T`. Any
        gesture that would create a second is normalised back
        to one before the write commits.
      - the `#`-picker apply path (`F-6.Phase1.apply`) creates a
        fresh instance each time it fires, so a new `X` is
        produced — the uniqueness invariant is preserved
        trivially on this path.
      - the promote-from-query path (`F-6.Phase3b.promote`)
        becomes find-or-create: before creating a new node, look
        the instance up via `buildWrapperIndex`; when a node
        already targets it, navigate to that node instead of
        creating a duplicate. `F-6.Phase3b.promote.reuse`
        already captured this for the post-creation case;
        F-Supertag.uniqueness extends the rule to every write
        path that adds a supertag Ref.
      - on outliner mount, a one-time-per-space normalisation
        sweep deduplicates any pre-existing violations: when two
        or more nodes share the same `(instance X, typename T)`
        pair, the canonical node is the one parented
        (transitively) under the Schema → supertag-node-for-T
        home; failing that, the lowest-`Node.id` node wins. The
        duplicate supertag Refs are dropped from the others.
  req F-Supertag.title-sync:
    given: a node tagged with supertag `#T` whose supertag target
      is instance `X`
    when: the node is created OR either side is edited after
      creation
    then:
      - initial seeding is path-dependent:
          a) **outliner-originated** — `#T` applied via the
             picker to a node whose content is "foo": the node's
             text seeds the instance's label.
             `Obj.setLabel(X, "foo")` is invoked immediately
             after `Obj.make`. This adds the seeding bullet to
             `F-6.Phase1.apply`.
          b) **externally-originated** — a node is being created
             for an instance that has no associated node yet
             (e.g. `F-6.Phase3b.promote` for an unwrapped
             instance, or a future "import-from-foreign-surface"
             gesture): the instance's label seeds the node's
             content as `[{kind: 'text', text: Obj.getLabel(X)}]`.
      - steady state is bi-directional:
          - editing the node's content writes through to
            `Obj.setLabel(X, newText)` within one tick.
          - editing `X`'s label elsewhere (a non-label FieldGroup
            write that updates the label field, a dedicated
            Composer surface like AgentArticle, an external
            mutation) propagates back to the node via
            `Obj.subscribe`: `node.content` becomes
            `[{kind: 'text', text: Obj.getLabel(X)}]`.
      - loop prevention uses the `externalSync` transaction
        meta-flag pattern from
        `F-DAG.Phase3e.multi-occurrence-edit-sync`: a write that
        originates as an external-sync reapplication doesn't
        re-fire the write-through to the instance.
      - when the schema has no useful `LabelAnnotation` (no
        candidate fields declared, or every candidate is the
        empty string), the sync degrades to no-op rather than
        throwing.
      - the SETTER side writes to the FIRST field listed in
        `LabelAnnotation`; subsequent fallback candidates are
        read-only by the GETTER side.
  req F-Supertag.title-hidden-in-fields:
    given: a FieldGroup is rendering the schema-derived fields of
      a typed instance linked to a node via supertag
    when: `listFields(schema)` computes the row list
    then:
      - any field whose name appears in the schema's
        `LabelAnnotation` is suppressed from the rendered rows.
        The node text in the row above the FieldGroup already
        shows that value per `F-Supertag.title-sync`; rendering
        it again would give the user two editable inputs for
        the same data.
      - non-label fields render as before.
      - when the schema declares no `LabelAnnotation`, no field
        is hidden (safe degradation).
  req F-Supertag.chip-gestures:
    given: a node tagged with supertag `#T` is rendering its
      TagChip
    when: I click the chip
    then:
      - **regular click**: the current pane zooms to the
        supertag node for `T` (the singleton parented under
        Schema). Mirrors Tana's "tag-page" navigation — the
        chip is the route TO browsing the tag.
      - **shift+click**: the supertag node opens in a NEW pane
        via F-Open-Pane semantics, with the deck-disabled
        fallback applying as for bullet shift-click.
      - this req subsumes the earlier
        `F-6.Phase3.tag-node.click` and
        `F-6.Phase3.tag-node.shift-click` reqs verbatim.
  req F-Supertag.open-instance-control:
    given: a node tagged with supertag `#T` whose supertag target
      is instance `X` is rendering its TagChip
    when: the row renders
    then:
      - a small icon control (e.g. an `↗` glyph) renders
        adjacent to the chip with an aria-label / tooltip like
        "Open <type-label>". The control is part of every chip;
        no per-typename gating in v1.
      - **regular click on the control**: the current pane opens
        `X` via `LayoutOperation.Open` with the qualified path
        derived from `getObjectPathFromObject(X)`. Composer's
        article-surface registry dispatches by typename:
        `org.dxos.type.agent` → AgentArticle,
        `org.dxos.type.document` → the markdown editor,
        `org.dxos.type.sketch` → the canvas, etc. When no
        article surface is registered for the typename,
        Composer's generic object viewer renders.
      - **shift+click on the control**: same `Open` call but
        with `mode: 'multi'` + the current pane as pivot, so
        the typed instance lands in a NEW pane to the right
        (analogous to F-Open-Pane for bullet shift-click). The
        deck-disabled fallback applies (toast + same-pane
        open).
      - the control is purely a navigation handle; it does NOT
        mutate the node, the instance, or any supertag state.
```

```mdl
feat F-Promote-Generalisation: supertag-node body splits into orphans + wrapped, conditional on type origin    # Increment Promote-Generalisation (not started)
  desc: |
    Refines the supertag-node body rendering captured today by
    `F-6.Phase3b.query-node.render` + `F-6.Phase3b.promote`,
    and operationalises the three-class supertag model captured
    in CONCEPTS.md §13.3 (Native / Bramble-defined ECHO /
    External ECHO).

    Net deltas vs current implementation:

    1. **Two visible queries for external ECHO types.** The
       supertag-node body splits into Query A (unpromoted
       instances — the orphan-promote surface) + Query B
       (wrapped Bramble.Nodes). Today the body renders ONE
       mixed query (per F-6.Phase3b.query-node.render — "every
       ECHO instance whose typename matches, including
       instances that don't have a wrapping Node yet").

    2. **Conditional rendering of Query A.** Query A renders
       ONLY for supertags whose backing is an external ECHO
       type. Bramble-defined ECHO types (`Bramble.Step`,
       `Bramble.Run`, `Bramble.Day`) and (future) native
       supertags render Query B only — no orphans exist for
       those classes, so the section is conceptually absent.

    3. **Promoted wrapper lands under today's Day-Node**
       (per `F-Today` "newly-created lands under Today"),
       not under the per-space Library Node. This supersedes
       the landing rule in `F-6.Phase3b.promote`. The
       drop-create path (F-PDF-Upload) keeps its drop-site
       hotspot landing — that's a different gesture.

    What stays unchanged (preserves existing implementation):

    - Schema-registry-driven discovery
      (`F-Supertag.types-shown`, `tag-types.ts:128-148`).
    - Eager + reactive supertag-node materialisation
      (`F-Supertag.eager-materialization`,
      `tag-supertags.ts:294-310`).
    - The `#` picker (M-Apply) exclusion list remains
      minimal — Relations + Bramble.Node + Bramble.Graph,
      per `F-Supertag.types-shown`.
    - Find-or-create wrapper discipline per
      `F-Supertag.uniqueness`.
    - The substrate invariant `R-Bramble-Surfaces-Wrap-Only`
      — mention surfaces Bramble.Nodes regardless of the
      two-query body shape; F-Promote-Generalisation does
      not touch MentionPicker.

    Supersession + R-Consolidation-Scrub: the implementation
    commit that ships F-Promote-Generalisation MUST scrub the
    superseded clauses — `F-6.Phase3b.query-node.render`'s
    single-query description + `F-6.Phase3b.promote`'s
    "appended to `Library.children`" landing rule + any
    `src/**` comments referencing those shapes — in the SAME
    commit per `R-Consolidation-Scrub`.

    Out of scope for F-Promote-Generalisation:
    - Native supertags (CONCEPTS §13.6) — separate feat.
    - Non-ECHO promote sources, URL/clipboard/agent-output
      (CONCEPTS §13.7) — separate feats.
    - Promote affordance refinements beyond click-on-row
      (CONCEPTS §13.9) — addressed if and when user
      research surfaces the need.
    - Wrapper-private fields lifecycle (CONCEPTS §13.9).
    - Multi-wrap (one ECHO instance with two Bramble.Node
      wrappers per CONCEPTS §13.9) — F-Supertag.uniqueness
      stays.
  req F-Promote-Generalisation.two-query-body:
    given: a Bramble outline mounted on a Composer space
    and: a supertag-node `S` for an external ECHO type `T`
      (where `T` passes the external-ECHO predicate per
      `F-Promote-Generalisation.external-echo-predicate`)
    when: the user navigates to `S` (via the Bramble-Nav
      "All Tags" landing, via clicking a `#T` chip
      elsewhere, or via opening `S` in a new pane)
    then:
      - the supertag-node body renders TWO visible query
        sections, in order:
        - Query A — orphan instances of `T` in the space
          (those NOT referenced by any Bramble.Node's
          `supertags` array). Each row carries a promote
          affordance per
          `F-Promote-Generalisation.promote-from-row`.
        - Query B — Bramble.Nodes in the space whose
          `supertags` array carries a Ref to an instance
          of `T`.
      - both queries are live: a new instance of `T`
        created anywhere in the space appears in Query A
        within one tick; a promote action on a Query A
        row moves the row to Query B reactively; deleting
        a wrapper (when the underlying instance survives,
        per current lifecycle policy) returns the row to
        Query A.
      - each query section renders a user-readable header
        (translation key TBD by implementation) and an
        empty-state hint when the section has zero rows
        (rather than collapsing the header).
  req F-Promote-Generalisation.conditional-orphan-query:
    given: a Bramble outline mounted on a Composer space
    and: a supertag-node `S` for a supertag that does NOT
      pass the external-ECHO predicate — i.e. either a
      Bramble-defined ECHO type (typename starts with
      `org.dxos.type.bramble.`) OR (future) a native
      supertag with no ECHO backing
    when: the user navigates to `S`
    then:
      - the supertag-node body renders ONLY Query B
        (Bramble.Nodes carrying the supertag).
      - Query A is NOT rendered — no section header, no
        empty-state hint. The conceptual absence of orphan
        instances is its UX-correct rendering: Bramble-
        defined types are wrapped at birth by Bramble's own
        flows; native supertags have no instances apart
        from the wrappers themselves.
  req F-Promote-Generalisation.promote-from-row:
    given: a Query A row on a supertag-node `S` for
      external ECHO type `T`, rendering an orphan instance
      `X` of `T`
    when: the user clicks the row (default promote
      affordance — alternative affordances per CONCEPTS
      §13.9 are deferred)
    then:
      - a wrapper Bramble.Node `W` is found-or-created per
        `F-Supertag.uniqueness`: if any Node already
        references `X` via `supertags`, the existing
        wrapper is reused; otherwise `W` is created with
        `supertags: [Ref(X)]` and `content` seeded from
        `Obj.getLabel(X)` (the externally-originated path
        in `F-Supertag.title-sync`).
      - `W` is attached as a successor of today's Day-Node
        per `F-Today`'s landing rule (find-or-create
        today's day-Node via `ensureDayNodeForDate`, then
        create a `'child'` `Edge` from the day-Node to `W`
        via `createEdge` per `R-Edges-First-Class`).
      - the Query A row reactively moves to Query B
        without remount; clicking the row again navigates
        to `W` (no second wrapper is created, per
        `F-Supertag.uniqueness`).
      - the current pane navigates to `W` so the user
        lands on the new wrapper for editing.
  req F-Promote-Generalisation.external-echo-predicate:
    given: any supertag `S` discovered by `collectTagTypes`
      per `F-Supertag.types-shown`, OR any (future) native
      supertag declaration
    when: the supertag-node renderer decides whether to
      include Query A
    then:
      - the renderer evaluates the external-ECHO predicate
        as a pure function of `S`:
        - TRUE iff `S` has an ECHO-type backing AND the
          backing's typename does NOT start with the
          Bramble namespace prefix
          (`org.dxos.type.bramble.`).
        - FALSE otherwise — native supertags fail the
          first conjunct (no ECHO backing); Bramble-
          defined ECHO types fail the second.
      - the predicate is side-effect-free and safe to
        evaluate every render; no I/O, no query, no
        registry walk beyond reading `S`'s typename and
        backing-kind which are already in hand at
        render time.
```

```mdl
feat F-DAG: parent/child as first-class edges     # Increment DAG
  desc: |
    Move the outliner's structural parent/child relationship off
    `Node.children: Ref<Node>[]` (an opaque adjacency list) and
    onto an explicit `Edge` relation entity. Multi-parent
    Nodes become possible, per-occurrence data (ordering, collapse
    state) attaches to the edge, and the same relation pattern
    generalises to arbitrary domain relationships (WorksAt,
    AssignedTo, …) without further plugin or ECHO changes.

    Migration is incremental — per `R-Edges-First-Class`, writers
    convert call-site by call-site and readers go through
    `useStructuralChildren`, which MERGES legacy `Node.children`
    entries with new Edges. `Node.children` is retained as a
    deprecated readable field until every writer is ported.

    Phases:

    - Phase 1: foundation only — `Edge` schema defined,
      registered, helpers shipped, Graph merges both
      representations. The F-6 Phase 3b `promoteToWrapper` flow
      under Library is migrated as a first proof; everything else
      still uses `Node.children`.
    - Phase 2 (current): `createTagBlock` migrates both of its
      writers — Schema → tag node and tag node → query child —
      to Edges. `Node` joins `Graph` in reading
      structural children via the merge hook so a tag node's
      query child still renders when the tag node appears as a
      nested Node (e.g. under Schema), not only when it's
      the page node.
    - Phase 3 (current, multi-step): main-outline writers convert
      to Edges:
        - 3a (current): the two "append exactly one child to an
          empty children list" writers — F-Pending-Child promote
          and Graph's legacy outline auto-seed — switch to
          `createEdge`. No sibling-order math needed.
        - 3b: `insertSiblingAfter` / `insertSiblingBefore`
          (Enter, Shift+Enter, Cmd+Shift+Enter). Needs sibling
          ordering — uses fractional `order` between adjacent
          existing siblings.
        - 3c: `handleIndent` / `handleDedent` (Tab / Shift+Tab).
          Adds and removes Edges to move a Node between
          parents; no new Node created.
        - 3d: lazy on-first-write migration of pre-existing
          `Node.children` entries to Edges so the merge
          stays consistent across edits to legacy outlines.
        - 3e: multi-predecessor UX — per-bullet predecessor-count
          badge (`predecessor-nav-list`'s smaller cousin on every
          bullet row); a predecessor-navigation control at the top
          of the page (`predecessor-nav-list` / `-switch` /
          `-open-pane`) that lists every predecessor of the page
          block and lets the user zoom to one (click) or open one
          in a new pane (shift-click); Cmd+Tab to LINK (creates a
          new predecessor edge alongside the existing one) versus
          Tab to MOVE (transfers the edge).
    - Phase 4 (current): collapse state moves off the Node and
      onto the edge. Reads go through `useEdgeExpanded(parent,
      block)`; writes go through `setEdgeExpanded`. Multi-parent
      Nodes (Phase 3e) get independent per-occurrence collapse —
      collapse a Node in one place and it stays open in the
      other.
    - Phase 5 (current): explicit edge `kind` (default
      'structural'); cycle prevention on writers so a Node can't
      be linked under one of its own descendants.
  req F-DAG.Phase1.schema:
    given: the plugin loads
    when: it registers its schema module
    then:
      - `Edge` (a `Type.relation` schema with `source: Node`,
        `target: Node`, `order: number`, optional `expanded` /
        `kind`) is included in the registered schemas alongside
        `Node` and `Graph`.
      - the relation typename is
        `org.dxos.type.bramble.edge`.
  req F-DAG.Phase1.helpers:
    given: code wants to read or write structural children
    when: it consults `components/Node/child-edges.ts`
    then:
      - `createEdge(db, parent, child, {order?, kind?})`
        creates a `Edge` via `Relation.make` (passing
        `Relation.Source` / `Relation.Target`), `db.add`s it, and
        defaults `order` to one past the current max among the
        parent's outgoing edges.
      - `getStructuralChildren(db, parent)` returns the merged list
        of structural children — `Node.children` entries first (in
        their array order), then Edges sorted by `order` —
        each as a plain `Ref` matching the legacy shape.
      - `useStructuralChildren(parent)` is the React-hook variant;
        subscribes to Edge queries so new edges trigger a
        re-render.
  req F-DAG.Phase1.promote-via-edge:
    given: the user clicks a wrapper-less query result (F-6
      Phase 3b.promote)
    when: `promoteToWrapper` runs
    then:
      - the wrapper Node is created and `db.add`-ed as before.
      - the wrapper's attachment to the per-space Library Node is
        recorded as a new `Edge` with `source: library`,
        `target: wrapper`, `order: nextOrderFor(library)` — NOT by
        appending to `library.children`.
      - `library.children` stays empty across the promote flow.
  req F-DAG.Phase1.tree-reads-via-merge:
    given: the Graph renders an outline rooted at any Node
    when: it enumerates that Node's structural children
    then:
      - it uses `useStructuralChildren(rootNode)`, which merges
        `Node.children` and incident Edges per
        F-DAG.Phase1.helpers.
      - for legacy outlines (no edges authored), the result is
        identical to the prior `snapshot.children` read.
      - for Library (post-promote), the result is the wrapper
        Nodes resolved from Edges; no regression to outline
        navigation in either case.
  req F-DAG.Phase2.tag-block-edges:
    given: `createTagBlock` is invoked for a new typename
    when: it materialises the tag node and its query child
    then:
      - the tag node is added to `Schema.children` via a NEW
        `Edge` with `source: schemaBlock`, `target: tagBlock`.
        `schemaNode.children` is NOT written to.
      - the auto-created query child is attached to the tag node
        via a `Edge` with `source: tagBlock`,
        `target: queryChild`. `tagNode.children` stays empty.
      - subsequent `createTagBlock` calls for different typenames
        add additional Edges off the same Schema Node; the
        Schema Node is still find-or-create singleton per space.
  req F-DAG.Phase5.structural-kind-default:
    given: a structural Edge is being created
    when: `createEdge(db, parent, child, options)` runs
    then:
      - if `options.kind` is omitted, the edge is created with
        `kind: 'structural'` (the constant
        `EDGE_KIND_STRUCTURAL` exported from `child-edges.ts`).
      - structural-children readers (`getStructuralChildren`,
        `childEdgesOf`, `parentEdgesOf`) filter to edges whose
        `kind` is undefined or `'structural'`. Edges with other
        kinds coexist in the schema but don't appear as outline
        children.
      - callers that want every edge regardless of kind pass
        `{ includeAllKinds: true }` to the reader.
  req F-DAG.Phase5.cycle-prevention:
    given: a structural Edge is being created (link, indent,
      promote, sibling-insert, …)
    when: `createEdge` is asked to attach `parent → child`
    then:
      - `wouldCreateCycle(db, parent, child)` runs first: it
        returns true when `parent === child` (self-loop) OR when
        `parent` is reachable by walking outgoing structural
        edges from `child`. The walk is bounded by a visited-set
        so any pre-existing cycle terminates safely.
      - if a cycle would be created, `createEdge` returns
        `undefined` without writing the edge.
      - non-structural edges (Phase-5-reserved kinds) are NOT
        cycle-checked — those are not part of the structural
        DAG.
  req F-DAG.Phase3a.add-existing-via-picker:
    given: my cursor is in a pending-child placeholder (the faint
      bullet on an expanded leaf)
    when: I type `@` and select an existing Node `X` from the
      mention picker
    then:
      - NO wrapper Node is created (no `Bramble.makeNode({content: [Ref(X)]})`).
      - a `Edge(parent, X)` is created via `createEdge`.
      - `X` now renders as a regular structural child of `parent`,
        with its NORMAL solid bullet.
      - the added node renders VISUALLY THE SAME as a node that
        was just typed inline — same bullet, same text colour,
        NO blue-link styling. The F-V3 blue ref treatment is for
        `@`-mentions inside a Node's TEXT, not for whole-node
        picks from the pending-child placeholder.
      - if `X` was already a child of another parent, it now has
        two structural parents; the multi-parent badge from
        F-DAG.Phase3e appears on both occurrences.
      - the pending-child placeholder unmounts because the leaf
        now has children.
      - cycle prevention from F-DAG.Phase5 applies — selecting
        `X` is silently rejected if `X` is an ancestor of
        `parent`.
    Note: this scenario is what distinguishes "add an existing
    node X" from "mention X in this Node's text". The latter
    happens when the user invokes `@` inside an existing
    Editor (typing into a Node's content): it inserts a Ref
    segment into the Node's `content` (an F-V3 inline mention,
    alongside text). The pending-child case is structurally
    different: the user is asking "what's the next bullet" not
    "what does this bullet say."
  req F-DAG.Phase4.per-occurrence-collapse:
    given: a Node rendering for `block` under `parent`
    when: it computes its collapse state OR toggles it
    then:
      - the read path uses `useEdgeExpanded(parent, block)`:
        - if the edge `parent → block` has `expanded` set, that
          value wins (per-occurrence semantics).
        - otherwise, the legacy `block.state.expanded` is read
          for backward compatibility.
        - default is `true` (expanded).
      - the write path uses `setEdgeExpanded(db, parent, block,
        value)`: persists on the edge if one exists, else falls
        back to `block.state.expanded` (defensive).
      - for a Node with multiple structural parents (Phase 3e
        link), each occurrence carries its own `expanded` on its
        own edge — collapsing the Node in one place does not
        affect the other rendering.
  req F-DAG.Phase3d.read-side-migration-backstop:
    given: a Node whose legacy `Node.children` array is
      non-empty (e.g. an outline created before Phase 3a / 3b)
    when: any `useStructuralChildren(thisBlock)` mounts in the
      current client session
    then:
      - a session-level lock (a `WeakMap<db, Set<parentId>>`) is
        consulted to ensure the migration runs at most once per
        parent, even when many Nodes mount concurrently and
        all subscribe to the same parent.
      - `ensureMigratedChildren(db, parent)` runs in a mount
        effect: legacy children are re-created as Edges with
        sequential orders, `parent.children` is cleared.
      - subsequent reads return purely from edges; the merge
        becomes a one-sided union.
      - outlines that were never edited after Phase 3a–3c land
        get drained passively just by being viewed.
  req F-DAG.Phase3e.multi-parent-badge:
    given: a Node has more than one structural parent — i.e. more
      than one `Edge` whose `target` is this Node
    when: the Node renders the bullet row
    then:
      - a small numeric badge appears immediately after the
        bullet showing the parent count.
      - the badge carries a tooltip ("Appears in N places").
      - the badge is suppressed when parent-edge-count <= 1.
      - the badge appears on EVERY occurrence of the Node (it
        renders inside Node, so each rendered instance
        shows it independently).
  req F-DAG.Phase3e.link-via-cmd-tab:
    given: the user holds Cmd (Mod) and presses Tab on a bullet
      whose previous sibling exists in the merged-children view
    when: the keymap fires `linkCommand`
    then:
      - both `parent` (current) and `prevSibling` (new) are
        migrated via `ensureMigratedChildren`.
      - a new Edge `prevSibling → block` is created and
        appended at the end of prevSibling's children.
      - the existing `parent → block` edge is PRESERVED — Node
        becomes a structural child of BOTH parents.
      - the operation is idempotent: if an edge from prevSibling
        to block already exists, Cmd+Tab is a no-op (no duplicate
        edges).
      - the multi-parent badge (F-DAG.Phase3e.multi-parent-badge)
        appears on the bullet row after the link is created.
  req F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-list:
    given: I am viewing a Node as the page node, AND the page
      block has at least one predecessor (a structural in-edge —
      i.e. at least one `Edge` whose `target` is the page
      block), AND a predecessor-navigation control is rendered at
      the top of the page for that page node
    when: I invoke the predecessor-navigation control
    then:
      - every predecessor of the page node appears as a
        selectable option in the control.
      - each option labels its predecessor using the same
        `Content` resolver the H1 header uses (text + refs),
        falling back to `(unnamed)` for predecessors with empty
        content.
      - the options are sorted alphabetically (case-insensitive)
        by display label, with `Node.id` as a deterministic
        tiebreaker so identically-labelled predecessors keep a
        stable order across re-renders. Unnamed predecessors
        (label resolves to `(unnamed)`) sort to the end of the
        list, then fall back to `Node.id` order among
        themselves.
      - non-structural edge kinds (Phase 5 reserved values) are
        EXCLUDED — only edges where `isStructuralEdge` returns true
        count as predecessors here. `parentEdgesOf(child)` is the
        authoritative reader.
      - the control replaces the single-parent `← {parent label}`
        breadcrumb from F-Page-Header.2 / .3 (which assumed at
        most one parent in the legacy tree model).
  req F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-inline:
    given: the page node has between 1 and 3 structural predecessors
      (inclusive) — i.e. `parentEdgesOf(pageNode).length <= 3`
    when: the predecessor-navigation control region renders
    then:
      - each predecessor renders as a SEPARATE inline control,
        side-by-side, in the same sort order as
        F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-list
      - each inline control has the visual form: `↑` (an up-arrow
        glyph), then a space, then the predecessor's display label
        resolved via the same `getDisplayLabel` pipeline the H1
        uses (text + refs; `(unnamed)` for empty content)
      - clicking an inline control (regular click) commits as
        F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-switch: the current pane's
        page node switches to that predecessor
      - shift-clicking an inline control commits as
        F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-open-pane: a new pane opens
        to the right rooted at that predecessor
      - the inline controls share the click-vs-shift-click
        distinction and the `(unnamed)` fallback with the dropdown
        variant — they are a rendering variant only, not a
        semantic divergence
    Note: user clarification 2026-05-14. The previous behaviour
    rendered a single "↑ N ▾" trigger that opened a dropdown for
    every N >= 1. For small predecessor sets (the common case in
    practice), the dropdown is over-machinery: an extra click and
    extra screen state to surface what could be presented inline.
    The ≤3 threshold is the bound at which inline controls
    visually crowd the page-top region — pick a deterministic cap
    so users always know whether to expect inline labels vs a
    dropdown.
  req F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-dropdown:
    given: the page node has 4 or more structural predecessors
      (`parentEdgesOf(pageNode).length > 3`)
    when: the predecessor-navigation control region renders
    then:
      - a SINGLE dropdown trigger renders. Visual form: `↑` (up-arrow
        glyph), a `▾` chevron indicating "click to open list".
        The trigger does NOT show the predecessor count number,
        and does NOT show any predecessor labels.
      - clicking the trigger opens a popup listing every
        predecessor in sort order (per
        F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-list)
      - clicking a menu item (regular click) commits as
        F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-switch
      - shift-clicking a menu item commits as
        F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-open-pane
      - clicking outside the popup dismisses it (same mousedown-
        deferred click-outside pattern the inline picker uses)
    Note: user clarification 2026-05-14 revised the prior single-
    universal "↑ N ▾" trigger. Above 3 predecessors, inline
    rendering becomes visually unwieldy — collapse to a dropdown
    so the page-top region stays compact. Below or equal to 3,
    F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-inline applies.
  req F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-switch:
    given: the predecessor-navigation control is open and listing
      the page node's predecessors
    when: I select one of the predecessor options with a regular
      click (no modifier keys held)
    then:
      - the current pane's page node switches to the selected
        predecessor.
      - the predecessor renders as the H1 header (per
        F-Page-Header) with its own successors below.
      - the predecessor-navigation control dismisses; on the new
        page node, the control reflects THAT page node's own
        predecessor set (which may be empty, one, or many).
      - no pane is opened or closed — the swap stays inside the
        current pane.
  req F-DAG.Phase3e.multi-occurrence-edit-sync:
    given: Node X is rendered in two or more places in the
      currently visible view — e.g. a multi-predecessor Node
      via `F-DAG.Phase3e.link-via-cmd-tab`, or any Node reachable
      from more than one structural edge — so two or more
      `Editor` instances are mounted against the same live
      `block` ref
    when: I type a character (or otherwise mutate `node.content`)
      inside ONE of those editor instances
    then:
      - every OTHER `Editor` instance bound to the same `block`
        re-syncs its ProseMirror doc from the new `node.content`
        within one ECHO subscription tick. The user sees their
        edit reflected in every visible rendering immediately
        — no manual refresh, no remount.
      - the editor the user typed into keeps its caret where the
        PM transaction left it; PM's own dispatch handles that.
      - destination editors that weren't focused don't acquire
        focus or scroll into view; they simply update content.
      - reactivity is rooted in `Obj.subscribe(block, …)`.
      - an idempotency guard (a content-equality check between
        the freshly computed PM doc and the editor's current doc
        via `Node.eq`) prevents the source editor's own write
        from being looped back as a redundant transaction.
    Note: this scenario was previously broken because the
    Editor mount effect's dependency array (`[block,
    makeRef, resolveRef]`) is stable across content changes —
    the editor only read `node.content` once at mount and never
    again. Multi-predecessor F-DAG outlines made the divergence
    immediately visible (typing in one occurrence left every
    other occurrence stale).
  req F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-open-pane:
    given: the predecessor-navigation control is open and listing
      the page node's predecessors
    when: I select one of the predecessor options with the Shift
      key held
    then:
      - a new pane opens to the right of the current pane (per
        F-Open-Pane), rendering the selected predecessor as the
        new pane's page node.
      - the current pane is unchanged — its page node AND the
        state of its predecessor-navigation control are both
        preserved.
      - the F-Open-Pane deck-disabled fallback applies when deck
        mode is off (a toast linking to Deck settings, plus an
        F-Zoom-style in-pane fallback), exactly as for bullet
        shift-click.
  req F-DAG.Phase3c.indent-dedent-as-edge-move:
    given: the user presses Tab or Shift+Tab on a bullet
    when: the indent / dedent handler fires
    then:
      - both endpoints (old parent and new parent) are migrated
        on first touch (`ensureMigratedChildren`).
      - indent: the edge `parent → block` is removed via
        `db.remove(edge)`; a new edge `prevSibling → block` is
        created and appended at the end (default
        `nextOrderFor(prevSibling)`).
      - dedent: the edge `parent → block` is removed; a new edge
        `grandparent → block` is created with `order` placed
        between parent's own edge in grandparent and parent's
        next sibling (via `orderBetween`).
      - if the expected parent edge in grandparent can't be
        found, dedent is a no-op (avoids orphan edges).
      - no `Node.children` array is written from either path.
  req F-Indent.auto-expand-parent:
    given: a bullet Y is being indented (Tab) under its previous
      sibling X, OR linked (Cmd+Tab, F-DAG.Phase3e.link-via-cmd-tab)
      under X, AND X is currently collapsed (`X.state.expanded` is
      false or undefined)
    when: the indent / link handler fires
    then:
      - the handler ALSO mutates X to set `X.state.expanded = true`
        in the same write batch as the edge creation. Without this,
        Y becomes invisible because X's collapsed-children column
        doesn't render the new edge.
      - Y remains focused (via `setFocusId(block.id)`) and its caret
        stays where it was — the auto-expand is silent from the
        user's perspective; they just see X open up.
    given: the user is editing X (which has no children yet, so X is
      a leaf with `state.expanded = false` per F-V2.12), creates a
      sibling Y below via Enter / Shift+Enter, places the caret in Y,
      then presses Tab to indent Y under X
    when: the handler runs
    then:
      - Y becomes a child of X (per
        F-DAG.Phase3c.indent-dedent-as-edge-move)
      - X expands; Y is rendered as the first child of X
      - the user's caret stays in Y; they can continue typing
        immediately
    Note: F-V2.12 makes every new Node collapsed by default, which
    is the right behaviour for the OTHER places new bullets appear
    (no auto-expansion of long lists), but it would break the
    indent flow without this auto-expand rule. Documented as a
    distinct req so future feature work (e.g. cmd+tab-link, drag-
    and-drop reparenting, paste-as-child) inherits the same
    "indent into a collapsed parent must expand it" guarantee.
  req F-DAG.Phase3b.sibling-insert-via-edge:
    given: the user presses Enter, Shift+Enter, or Cmd+Shift+Enter
      on a bullet whose parent may still hold pre-existing
      `Node.children` entries
    when: the insert handler fires
    then:
      - `ensureMigratedChildren(db, parent)` runs first: every
        legacy `Node.children` entry is converted to a
        `Edge` with sequential `order` (0, 1, 2, …) matching
        the merged view, and `parent.children` is cleared.
      - the new bullet is `db.add`-ed.
      - a new Edge is created with `order` placed BETWEEN
        the current bullet's edge and the appropriate neighbour
        (the next edge for `insertSiblingAfter`, the previous
        edge for `insertSiblingBefore`) via `orderBetween`.
        Edge-of-array inserts get `prev.order + 1` or
        `next.order - 1` so the resulting sort matches user
        intent.
      - after this write, the parent's `Node.children` is empty
        forever; every subsequent sibling-insert uses fractional
        orders without further migration.
  req F-DAG.Phase3a.append-via-edge:
    given: a writer needs to append exactly one new child to a
      Node that has no existing children (legacy or edges)
    when: the writer fires
    then:
      - the writer calls `db.add(newChild)` then
        `createEdge(db, parent, newChild)`.
      - parent's `Node.children` array is NOT touched.
      - the two call-sites covered by Phase 3a are
        `PendingChildRow.onPromote` (F-Pending-Child) and
        `Graph`'s legacy outline auto-seed migration.
      - the seed-decision in `Graph` consults
        `getStructuralChildren(rootNode)`, so an outline that
        already has either legacy children or Edges is left
        alone.
  req F-DAG.Phase2.blocknode-merge:
    given: Node renders a non-system Node whose children may
      come from `Node.children`, Edges, or both
    when: it computes `childRefs`
    then:
      - it uses `useStructuralChildren(block)`, the same merge hook
        Graph uses.
      - a tag node rendered as a nested Node (e.g. when the
        user expands a tag's bullet inside the Schema page) still
        shows its query child, sourced from the Edge.
      - legacy outline reads are unaffected — when no Edges
        fan out of `block`, the merged result is exactly the
        `snapshot.children` array, in the same order.
      - the hook is called BEFORE the `queryRef` early return so
        the rules-of-hooks order stays stable across all branches
        of Node rendering.
```

```mdl
feat F-One-Graph: a space has at most one Bramble    # Increment One-Graph
  desc: |
    A space carries zero or one Bramble. New spaces have none;
    the user creates the Bramble explicitly via the space's
    create-menu, and once a Bramble exists, any subsequent
    invocation of that same menu item is idempotent — no second
    Bramble is created.

    Why: Bramble's data model is space-wide (the per-space
    Schema Node, Library Node, supertag instances, tag-Nodes,
    and option-Nodes are all space-singletons). "Two Brambles"
    would be two roots into the same underlying graph — not two
    isolated outlines — so the multi-instance posture is a
    user-visible lie. Singleton makes the model honest.

    Greenfield per R-Greenfield-Stance: test spaces with
    multiple pre-existing Bramble instances are out of scope;
    user wipes and re-seeds.

  req F-One-Graph.singleton-per-space:
    given: any space in the user's deck
    when: the user observes the space's content
    then:
      - the space carries zero or one Bramble, never more.

  req F-One-Graph.bramble-created-via-space-menu:
    given: a space with no Bramble; the Bramble plugin is
      enabled
    when: the user opens the space's create-menu and selects
      the "Bramble" item
    then:
      - a single Bramble is created in the space;
      - the user is navigated to it.

  req F-One-Graph.create-action-is-idempotent:
    given: a space that already contains a Bramble; the
      Bramble plugin is enabled
    when: the user opens the space's create-menu and selects
      the "Bramble" item
    then:
      - no second Bramble is created;
      - the user is navigated to the existing Bramble (the
        same outcome they would get from any other navigation
        path to it).

  req F-One-Graph.bramble-not-listed-under-types:
    given: a space containing a Bramble; the user expands the
      generic per-typename navigator section (today shown as
      "Database" or similar)
    when: the user scans the listing
    then:
      - the Bramble does NOT appear there. It is reachable
        via the "Bramble" sidebar section (see F-Bramble-Nav),
        not via the per-typename listing.
```

```mdl
feat F-Bramble-Nav: per-space "Bramble" sidebar section    # Increment Bramble-Nav
  desc: |
    The space's sidebar carries a "Bramble" section that is
    present whenever the space contains a Bramble. The section
    is visible regardless of which Node, page, or Lens the user
    is currently viewing — it is part of the navigator chrome,
    not a per-page surface.

    The section's purpose: navigation entry-points into the
    Bramble. v1 contains two items; future increments add more
    (Recents, Capture, Pinned, Search, etc.).

  req F-Bramble-Nav.section-present-iff-bramble-exists:
    given: a space; the Bramble plugin is enabled
    when: the user opens the space and observes the sidebar
    then:
      - if the space contains a Bramble, the sidebar shows a
        section labeled "Bramble";
      - if the space contains no Bramble, no section is
        rendered.

  req F-Bramble-Nav.today-opens-todays-day-node:
    given: a space whose Bramble exists; the sidebar's
      "Bramble" section is visible
    when: the user activates the "Today" item inside the
      section
    then:
      - the active pane navigates to the Today alias per
        R-Bramble-Subject-Path; the resolver maps it to
        today's day-Node (the Node carrying the `#Day`
        supertag whose date is today's local-tz
        `YYYY-MM-DD`);
      - if no such Node exists yet, one is created (per
        `type Day`'s uniqueness invariant) and the pane
        navigates to it.

  req F-Bramble-Nav.today-resets-zoom:
    given: a pane displays any subject other than the Today
      alias — anything from a zoomed-in descendant of today's
      day-Node, to today's day-Node addressed via its
      canonical path (rather than the alias), to a completely
      unrelated subject (a supertag-node, a predecessor walk,
      an `@`-mention destination, etc.)
    when: the user activates the "Today" item in the left nav
    then:
      - the pane's subject becomes the Today alias via
        `LayoutOperation.Open` per
        R-Bramble-Subject-Path — independent of what the
        pane was displaying;
      - the resolver maps the alias to today's day-Node per
        F-Bramble-Nav.today-opens-todays-day-node;
      - the Article re-renders with the day-Node as its
        pageNode per F-Zoom.subject-change-replaces-rendered-
        target — any prior zoom state is naturally discarded
        because the subject is the zoom (per F-Zoom +
        R-Bramble-Subject-Path).
    Note: when the pane subject is ALREADY the Today alias,
    activating Today is the existing navtree no-op-while-
    current behavior (a scroll-into-view; the pane's view
    is unchanged because it's already on today).

  req F-Bramble-Nav.all-tags-opens-schema-node:
    given: a space whose Bramble exists
    when: the user activates the "All Tags" item inside the
      section
    then:
      - the active pane navigates to the per-space Schema
        Node — the Node under which every supertag class is
        materialised per F-Supertag.eager-materialization.

  req F-Bramble-Nav.section-expands-on-bramble-creation:
    given: a space with no Bramble
    and: the Bramble plugin is enabled
    when: a Bramble is created and the sidebar's "Bramble"
      section first appears (per
      F-Bramble-Nav.section-present-iff-bramble-exists)
    then:
      - the section is rendered EXPANDED on first appearance
        — Today, All Tags, and any other children are
        immediately visible without a separate click on the
        section's chevron.

  Subsequent collapse / expand of the section by the user is
  not affected by this req — the user can collapse the section
  after creation and that choice persists per the navigator's
  normal state behavior.
```

```mdl
feat F-No-Root: the Bramble is a marker; the DAG has no designated root    # Increment No-Root
  desc: |
    Bramble's data is a DAG (per F-DAG), not a tree. A DAG has no
    single "top" — any Node can have multiple parents; the
    structural top is an emergent set (Nodes with no incoming
    `'child'` predecessor edges), not a designated entity.

    The Bramble.Graph object is a marker on the space: it carries
    metadata (today: `name`) and proves the singleton-per-space
    Bramble exists (F-One-Graph). It does NOT designate a
    structural root and is NOT directly viewable. The user
    reaches the Bramble through its Nodes (today's Node, supertag
    pages, @-mention navigation, F-Zoom, the Bramble nav section
    per F-Bramble-Nav) — never through the Graph object itself.

    Why: the prior `Graph.root: Ref<Node>` field was a tree-shape
    artifact — the Bramble's contents framed as descendants of
    one designated Node. The DAG model has no use for it.
    Multi-parent is first-class (F-DAG.Phase3e); predecessor
    navigation is first-class (PredecessorNav); zoom addresses
    any Node (F-Zoom); the canonical landing is today's Node
    (per F-Bramble-Nav.today-opens-todays-day-node). Root has
    no remaining responsibility.

    Subsumes F-Today: the prior F-Today.mount-lands-on-todays-
    day-node req was a roundabout way to say "the user is
    navigated to today's Node when there is no specific Node in
    mind." Under F-No-Root that's expressed at the navigation
    layer — F-Bramble-Nav and F-No-Root.create-navigates-to-
    today together cover the user-visible behavior. The day-
    Node's "behaves like any other Node" invariant is implicit
    in `type Day` (a Node carrying #Day is structurally
    identical to any other Node — supertags don't special-case).

    Greenfield per R-Greenfield-Stance: test spaces with
    pre-No-Root Bramble.Graphs (carrying `root` Refs to Nodes
    that may or may not have been persisted) become invisible
    to the new render paths; user wipes + reseeds.

  req F-No-Root.graph-has-no-root-field:
    given: any Bramble.Graph in a space
    when: an observer inspects the Graph's data
    then:
      - the Bramble.Graph carries no field designating a root
        Node. The Graph's only observable fields are metadata
        (today: `name`).

  req F-No-Root.graph-not-directly-viewable:
    given: a Bramble.Graph exists in a space
    when: any path tries to open the Bramble.Graph as the
      subject of an article pane (deep-link URL, programmatic
      navigation, etc.)
    then:
      - no article surface renders the Bramble.Graph as its
        subject. The user does not encounter "the Bramble" as
        a viewable object — only Nodes are viewable.

  req F-No-Root.create-navigates-to-today:
    given: the user invokes the space's create-menu "Bramble"
      item (the F-One-Graph creation flow)
    when: the Bramble is created — OR located via the
      idempotent find-or-create path per
      F-One-Graph.create-action-is-idempotent
    then:
      - the user is navigated to today's Node (the Node
        carrying the `#Day` supertag for today's local-tz
        date, find-or-created per `type Day`'s uniqueness
        invariant);
      - NOT to the Bramble.Graph object itself.

  req F-No-Root.create-focuses-pane-cursor:
    given: a space with no Bramble
    and: the Bramble plugin is enabled
    when: a Bramble is created
    then:
      - the cursor is focused on the Node content for the
        Node currently zoomed into in the pane (e.g. today's
        Node by default per F-No-Root.create-navigates-to-
        today; the specific focused Node may change with
        future reqs).
```

