feat F-7: Tana Paste import     # Increment 7
  req F-7.1: Pasting a payload starting with %%tana%% into a Graph imports the structure as Nodes.
  req F-7.2: Supertags, fields, references, and dates are preserved using a stable-ID translation table.
```

```mdl
feat F-Step: #Step supertag — type marker for substrate work units    # Iteration 2a
  desc: |
    Registers `#Step` as a supertag class in plugin-bramble's
    type module. A Bramble.Node carrying this supertag is a
    Step per CONCEPTS.md §12.3: "a named piece of work,
    recursively composed" (substrate-principles, Key terms).

    The supertag's instance Schema starts empty — the marker
    alone is enough to start. Fields (formalization,
    description-conventions, etc.) get added as demand pulls
    them in (per Principle #1; see CONCEPTS.md §12.3 schema
    sketch for the field candidates).

    Out of scope for F-Step: the F-Run / F-Edge-Creation-Gesture
    sequence (2b/2c) that lets a tagged Step accumulate Runs;
    executor-availability edges (2e); any journal UX over Steps
    (2d).
  req F-Step.1: A `Step` ECHO Schema is exported as `Bramble.Step`
    from `plugin-bramble/src/types/Bramble.ts` with typename
    `org.dxos.type.bramble.step`, version `0.1.0`, and an empty
    Schema body (no fields).
  req F-Step.2: `BramblePlugin` registers `Bramble.Step` so the
    existing F-Supertag machinery picks it up:
      - `#` picker offers "Step" as a tag option
        (per T-Supertag-picker-lists-types)
      - selecting it creates a typed Step instance
        (per T-Supertag-picker-creates-fresh-instance)
      - chip renders per F-Supertag chip behaviors
```

```mdl
feat F-Run: #Run supertag + edge kinds for run-relations    # Iteration 2b
  desc: |
    Registers `#Run` as a supertag class and extends the
    `Edge.kind` literal union with two new values:
    `'is-run-of'` (Run → Step) and `'parent-run'`
    (child Run → parent Run). A Bramble.Node carrying `#Run`
    is a Run per CONCEPTS.md §12.4 — "an instance of a step
    being executed."

    The supertag's instance Schema starts empty, mirroring
    F-Step (per CONCEPTS.md §12.4: what fields a Run carries
    on day one is not yet decided and emerges from iteration).

    Out of scope for F-Run: any UX gesture for creating
    `'is-run-of'` / `'parent-run'` edges between two Nodes
    (deferred to F-Edge-Creation-Gesture, iteration 2c); any
    journal UX over Runs (2d). Edges of the new kinds can be
    created programmatically via `createEdge(db, source,
    target, { kind: 'is-run-of' })` once F-Run lands; this
    will be the substrate for the 2c UX work.
  req F-Run.1: A `Run` ECHO Schema is exported as `Bramble.Run`
    from `plugin-bramble/src/types/Bramble.ts` with typename
    `org.dxos.type.bramble.run`, version `0.1.0`, and an empty
    Schema body (no fields).
  req F-Run.2: `BramblePlugin` registers `Bramble.Run` so the
    existing F-Supertag machinery picks it up, identically to
    F-Step.2.
  req F-Run.3: `Bramble.Edge.kind`'s literal union is extended
    from `'child'` to `'child' | 'is-run-of' | 'parent-run'`.
    Existing `childEdgesOf` / `useStructuralChildren` continue
    to filter to `'child'`-kind edges so the outline read path
    is unaffected. `createEdge`'s `options.kind` type is
    widened to accept the new values; cycle detection remains
    `'child'`-only.
```

```mdl
feat F-New-Run-On-Step: "+ New Run" action on Step-tagged pages, recursive spawn    # Iteration 2c.1 (revised)
  desc: |
    First user gesture that creates a non-`child` edge. When the
    page Node carries the `#Step` supertag, a "+ New Run" button
    renders between PageHeader and the Graph body. Clicking it
    creates a *tree* of Run-Nodes: one parent Run for the Step,
    plus one child Run for every `#Step`-tagged structural
    descendant (recursive, full depth). The pane then zooms into
    the parent Run, which renders as the runbook-execution view
    (see F-Run-Execution-View).

    Per substrate-principles "Run, key term": "A run of a parent
    step may spawn child runs of its sub-steps; the parent-child
    relationship is recorded in the log." Per S2: "the substrate
    shows him the parent description, and within it, the
    `extract_amount` sub-step is visible. He fills in the amount
    as part of doing the parent." The recursive spawn is what
    makes that view possible.

    Stance (substrate-principles): the "Steve runs the step"
    flow (S1, S2). Pacing test (Principle #18): a one-click
    "do the work" affordance.

    Out of scope: a "Choose Step" affordance on `#Run` pages
    (F-Set-Step-On-Run, iteration 2c.4); any list of past Runs
    on a Step page (F-Step-Runs-List, iteration 2d). Pinning
    the Run to a snapshot of the Step it was executed against
    is handled by F-Versioning (the `'is-run-of'` edge created
    here auto-pins per `F-Versioning.auto-pin-on-create`).
  req F-New-Run-On-Step.1: When the page Node carries a supertag
    instance of typename `org.dxos.type.bramble.step`, the
    Article surface renders a "+ New Run" button between
    PageHeader and the Graph body. On Nodes without `#Step` the
    button is absent.
  req F-New-Run-On-Step.2:
    when: user clicks "+ New Run" on a Step-tagged page
    then:
      - a parent Bramble.Node is created and tagged `#Run` per the
        F-Supertag application path
      - an Edge with kind `'is-run-of'` is created from the new
        parent Run-Node (source) to the Step-Node (target)
      - for every `#Step`-tagged structural child of the Step,
        a child Run-Node is created recursively (the same shape:
        new Node + `#Run` supertag + `'is-run-of'` edge to the
        sub-Step), linked to its parent Run-Node via an Edge of
        kind `'parent-run'`
      - recursion continues to full depth — a `#Step`'s sub-sub-
        steps also get Run-Nodes, etc.
      - the current pane zooms into the parent Run-Node
      - the parent Run-Node renders as the F-Run-Execution-View
        (structured runbook walkthrough), not as a blank outline
```

```mdl
feat F-Run-Execution-View: structured runbook walkthrough for a Run-Node    # Iteration 2c.2
  desc: |
    Renders a Run-Node as a guided walkthrough of its Step's
    narrative. Replaces the Graph (outline) body on pages whose
    page Node carries `#Run`.

    For each Run-Node, the view shows:
      - The corresponding Step's content as a read-only "runbook
        prompt" (sourced by following the `'is-run-of'` edge from
        Run → Step and reading `Step.content`).
      - An editable bound to the Run-Node's body content — this is
        where the user types their fill-in for this sub-Step.
      - The Run's child Run-Nodes (located by following incoming
        `'parent-run'` edges where this Run is the target), rendered
        recursively, indented one level per depth.

    Per substrate-principles S1: "the substrate shows him the
    description and a text box." S2: "the substrate shows him the
    parent description, and within it, the `extract_amount` sub-
    step is visible. He fills in the amount as part of doing the
    parent." The recursive child rendering matches Q1's "recursive
    as far as needed" answer.

    Versioning (Q2 — resolved by F-Versioning):
    F-Run-Execution-View resolves the Step's narrative via
    `resolveEdgeTarget('is-run-of'-edge)`. The `'is-run-of'`
    edge is auto-pinned at Run-creation per
    `F-Versioning.auto-pin-on-create`, so the rendered Step
    narrative reflects the moment the Run was created — not
    subsequent live edits to the Step. Pre-F-Versioning Runs
    (whose edges have no `targetVersion`) keep rendering the
    LIVE Step per `F-Versioning.unpinned-edges-render-live`
    (greenfield).
  req F-Run-Execution-View.1:
    when: the Article renders a page Node carrying `#Run`
    then:
      - the Graph (outline) body is NOT rendered for this page
      - in its place, F-Run-Execution-View renders rooted at the
        page Run-Node
  req F-Run-Execution-View.2: For each Run-Node rendered, the
    view shows: (a) the Step's content as read-only narrative
    text (the "runbook prompt"), (b) an editable bound to the
    Run-Node's body content (the user's fill-in), (c) zero or
    more child Run-Nodes, rendered recursively with one level of
    indentation per depth.
  req F-Run-Execution-View.3: Child Run-Nodes are located by
    querying incoming Edges of `kind: 'parent-run'` where the
    current Run-Node is the target. Order among siblings follows
    the Edge `order` field. (No `useChildRunsOf` migration is
    needed because `'parent-run'` edges were introduced in 2b.)
```

```mdl
feat F-Run-Nav: page-top navigation for Run-Nodes via outgoing edges    # Iteration 2c.2 follow-up
  desc: |
    Closes a gap exposed during F-Run-Execution-View verification:
    a Run page has no UI back-navigation because every navigation
    primitive in Bramble today (`PredecessorNav`, chip-click,
    bullet-zoom) walks `'child'` edges, and Runs have no `'child'`
    predecessors. Their navigable edges are OUTGOING — to the
    Step (`'is-run-of'`) and to the parent Run (`'parent-run'`).

    `F-Run-Nav` renders a small inline row above the Run
    Execution View showing:
      - "← Step: {label}" — always (every Run has exactly one
        `'is-run-of'` edge)
      - "↑ Parent run: {label}" — only when the Run is nested
        (top-level Runs have no `'parent-run'` edge)

    Click switches the pane to the target (zoom). Shift-click
    opens it in a new pane. Mirrors PredecessorNav semantics for
    consistency.

    Why a new component rather than extending PredecessorNav?
    PredecessorNav reads INCOMING `'child'` edges; Run-Nav reads
    OUTGOING non-`'child'` edges. Different reverse direction and
    different edge-kind filter. A dedicated component is clearer
    than overloading PredecessorNav, especially given future
    substrate edges (`'available-executor'`, `'executed-by'`,
    `'in-context'`) will likely each want their own surface.
  req F-Run-Nav.1: When the page Node carries `#Run`, an inline
    nav row renders between the supertag chip row and the
    F-Run-Execution-View, surfacing the Run's outgoing
    `'is-run-of'` and `'parent-run'` edges as click-to-navigate
    controls.
  req F-Run-Nav.2: The Step link ("← Step: {label}") is rendered
    iff the Run has an `'is-run-of'` edge whose target exists.
    The parent-run link ("↑ Parent run: {label}") is rendered
    iff the Run has an outgoing `'parent-run'` edge whose target
    exists.
  req F-Run-Nav.3: Click on a link calls the same
    `handleSelectPredecessor` Article uses for PredecessorNav
    (zoom in current pane). Shift-click calls the same
    `handleOpenPane` (open target in new pane). Mouse-down
    handling rather than click to avoid editor-blur races.
```

```mdl
feat F-Step-Runs-List: list of Runs on a `#Step` page    # Iteration 2d
  desc: |
    Surfaces every Run-Node that has an `'is-run-of'` edge
    pointing at the current Step. Renders between the
    "+ New Run" button and the outline body. Click a row to
    zoom into that Run's F-Run-Execution-View; shift-click to
    open it in a new pane.

    Per substrate-principles Principle #17 ("the substrate must
    be useful as a journal alone"): this is the journal payoff.
    Steve reviews his past Runs of `reconcile_line` here. The
    list is the entry point for journal-mode review of past
    work.

    Order: chronological (ULID Node.id sort = creation-time sort).
    Row label: the Run's display label if non-empty; otherwise a
    fallback "Run N" indexed by position in the list. (Parent
    Run-Nodes typically have no body content because the user
    fills in their child Runs; the index fallback keeps rows
    scannable.)

    Out of scope: filtering (by date, by outcome, by executor) —
    deferred until usage signals demand. Aggregated summary view
    (e.g. "30 runs, script agreed with Steve 28 times") — same.
  req F-Step-Runs-List.1: When the page Node carries `#Step` and
    has one or more `'is-run-of'` edges pointing at it (i.e. one
    or more Runs reference this Step), the Article surface
    renders a Runs list between the StepActions row and the
    Graph outline body.
  req F-Step-Runs-List.2: When the page Node carries `#Step` but
    has zero Runs, the list renders nothing (no empty-state
    panel — "+ New Run" sits above as the call-to-action).
  req F-Step-Runs-List.3: Click on a row calls
    `handleSelectPredecessor` (zoom in current pane).
    Shift-click calls `handleOpenPane`. Mouse-down handling
    rather than click for editor-blur consistency with the
    rest of Bramble's navigation controls.
```

```mdl
feat F-Run-Lens: first instantiation of the Lens primitive (CONCEPTS.md §9)    # Iteration 2c.5
  desc: |
    The Run Lens is the FIRST CONCRETE instantiation of the Lens
    primitive that CONCEPTS.md §9 introduced and §11 named as
    "first-class — unified language is one option, not the
    default." A Lens (per Cabrera's POV-2 / substrate-principles
    Principle #11) is a named perspective on the substrate that
    maps its own vocabulary, property schemas, and visibility
    rules onto shared underlying identities.

    The Run Lens, applied to a Run-Node, transforms the view
    into a guided runbook walkthrough:
      - Step prompts render read-only (the schema as seen by the
        executor)
      - Each Run-Node has a read/write response area that is a
        full Bramble outline rooted at the Run-Node — the user
        can add bullets, refs, marks, and (when 2f lands) PDF
        attachments
      - A wizard surface controls progression: Start / Next /
        Previous / Stop / Done

    Activation: per-pane in-session state (not persisted to
    ECHO yet). "+ New Run" on a Step page creates the Run-Node
    tree AND activates the Lens. Stop / Done deactivate. On a
    `#Run` page with the Lens deactivated, a "Resume in Run
    Lens" button restores it (resets wizard state).

    Q2 (Versioning) — resolved by F-Versioning. The Run Lens
    renders Step prompts via the Run's auto-pinned
    `'is-run-of'` edge (per `F-Versioning.auto-pin-on-create`),
    so an edit to the live Step from outside the Lens doesn't
    retroactively change what past Runs display. The Lens's
    own read-only-inside treatment prevents accidental in-
    Lens edits to the Step; F-Versioning prevents external
    drift.

    Connection to CONCEPTS.md: §9 talked about Lens-as-generative-
    P at length (observer substitution, ontology inheritance,
    generative response, attribution, composition). The Run Lens
    exercises a subset:
      - Observer substitution: the user is viewing the graph
        through the "actively running" perspective
      - Visibility rules: Steps shown read-only; responses
        editable; non-#Step children invisible to wizard
      - Composition: not yet (no Lens-on-Lens composition until
        a second Lens exists)
    Attribution and generative response are deferred to
    subsequent Lenses (Cabrera-P style content generation, etc.).
  req F-Run-Lens.1: Article carries per-pane state `runLensActive:
    boolean`. Default false. Set true when "+ New Run" fires
    (via the StepActions `onCreateRun` callback). Set false on
    Stop / Done. Restored to true on Resume.
  req F-Run-Lens.2: When the page Node carries `#Run` AND
    `runLensActive` is true, Article renders `<RunLensShell>`
    around the RunExecutionView. The shell provides:
      - a top banner ("Run Lens · {Step label}") with the
        sky-tinted visual treatment that signals "you are in
        the Lens"
      - the wizard control row (Start / Next / Previous / Stop
        / Done)
  req F-Run-Lens.3: Wizard controls:
      - "Start" is visible before the user has clicked it; on
        click, the wizard begins with active focus on the
        first Run-Node (depth-first traversal of the tree).
      - "Next" advances the active focus to the subsequent
        Run-Node; disabled at the last Run.
      - "Previous" retreats; disabled at the first Run.
      - "Stop" deactivates the Lens; the Run-Node tree is
        preserved as-is (partial responses are fine).
      - "Done" only enabled when every Run-Node in the tree
        has at least one structural child with non-empty text
        content. On click: writes an ISO timestamp to the
        Run supertag instance's `completed` field, then
        deactivates the Lens.
  req F-Run-Lens.4: The active Run-Node section is visually
    distinguished by a sky-tinted ring around the corresponding
    `[data-run-view]` element. The wizard scrolls the active
    section into view on Start / Next / Previous.
  req F-Run-Lens.5: Each Run-Node's response area inside the
    Lens is a full Bramble outline (the existing `<Graph>`)
    rooted at the Run-Node. The user gets the standard outline
    editing affordances (Enter/Tab/Shift+Tab, refs, marks,
    future attachments).
  req F-Run-Lens.6: The Run supertag schema (Bramble.Run) gains
    a `completed?: string` optional field. Set by F-Run-Lens.3's
    Done action. Absence means "in progress or stopped without
    completing."
```

```mdl
feat F-Supertag-Chips-On-Page-Header: supertag chips render alongside the H1    # Iteration 2c.1 follow-up
  desc: |
    When a Bramble Node is opened as the page node and carries one
