```

```mdl
feat F-PDF-Upload: drop a PDF onto a page to become a Node   # Increment 2f
  desc: |
    Drag-and-drop a PDF file anywhere over a Bramble page (the
    article surface, a specific bullet row, or the pending-child
    placeholder) to create a Bramble bullet that represents the
    uploaded file. The bullet renders with a PDF icon + a
    user-renameable label; clicking the chip opens the file
    inline via Composer's standard navigate-to-object pathway
    (which routes through plugin-wnfs's `FileContainer`).

    The bullet IS a Bramble.Node wrapping a Wnfs.File ECHO
    instance (typename `org.dxos.type.file`, contributed by
    plugin-wnfs). The wrap uses the existing F-Supertag pattern
    — the Bramble.Node's `supertags` array carries a Ref to the
    Wnfs.File. The Wnfs.File holds the canonical file identity
    (name, mime type, content-addressed `cid`); the Bramble.Node
    carries the user-facing editable label as its `content`.

    Storage routes through `AppCapabilities.FileUploader` — the
    capability that plugin-wnfs contributes today. plugin-bramble
    does NOT roll its own file storage. The capability's WNFS
    implementation creates and `db.add`s the Wnfs.File ECHO
    instance automatically as part of the upload (via
    `WnfsOperation.Create` + `SpaceOperation.AddObject`);
    Bramble locates the just-created instance by `cid` and wraps
    it in a Bramble.Node. If no FileUploader is registered, the
    drop target shows a "file upload unavailable" hint instead
    of accepting the drop. This keeps R-No-Echo-Changes
    satisfied (file storage and the file ECHO type both stay in
    the WNFS plugin).

    Same-PDF-twice deduplicates via the content-addressed `cid`
    (WNFS storage is content-addressed: same bytes → same cid by
    construction; no Bramble-side hashing required). If a
    Wnfs.File with the same `cid` is already in the space AND
    is already wrapped by a Bramble.Node via supertag, neither a
    new Wnfs.File nor a new Bramble.Node is created. Instead a
    structural edge (`createEdge` per F-DAG) is added from the
    drop-site parent to the existing Bramble.Node, so the
    existing bullet appears as a structural successor at the
    drop point. Re-dropping the same file under different drop
    sites surfaces the same Node in multiple places via the
    F-DAG multi-parent badge (per F-DAG.Phase3e).

    Drop site resolution:
      - When the drop coordinates land over a rendered bullet
        row, that bullet's Node becomes the new wrapper-Node's
        structural predecessor.
      - When the drop coordinates land over the pending-child
        placeholder of an expanded parent, the new wrapper-
        Node lands as the LAST structural child of that
        parent (matching the placeholder's Enter-key semantics).
      - Otherwise the page node (the pane's current `pageNode`)
        is used.

    Switch note (2026-05-16): F-PDF-Upload originally inlined
    file identity onto Bramble.Node via an `attachment` struct
    (`{ kind, url, name, mimeType, sha256 }`). That model
    duplicated plugin-wnfs's canonical Wnfs.File for every
    drop. The current spec switches to the Wnfs.File model via
    the F-Supertag wrap pattern (above). Greenfield per
    R-Greenfield-Stance — pre-switch PDFs in test spaces are
    invisible to the new flow; user wipes + re-seeds.

  req F-PDF-Upload.drop-creates-node:
    given: a Bramble page is open; a PDF file is dragged from
      the OS over the page; no Wnfs.File with that file's
      content-addressed `cid` exists in the space yet
    when: the user releases the drag (`drop`) over the article
      surface
    then:
      - the file is uploaded via the registered
        `AppCapabilities.FileUploader` capability, which creates
        a Wnfs.File ECHO instance W (typename
        `org.dxos.type.file`) and adds W to the space;
      - a Bramble.Node B is created with empty `content` and
        with W as its sole supertag (`B.supertags = [Ref(W)]`);
      - B is added under the drop-site parent (resolved per
        F-PDF-Upload's drop-site rules above) via `createEdge`
        per F-DAG, so B appears as a fresh bullet at that
        location.

  req F-PDF-Upload.drop-seeds-content-with-filename:
    given: a PDF file is being dropped onto a Bramble page
    when: the wrapping Node is created (per
      F-PDF-Upload.drop-creates-node)
    then:
      - the Node's content is a copy of the dropped file's
        name (the Bramble bullet displays the filename as its
        editable text);
      - the user can edit the content like any other Bramble
        bullet (Tana-style rename) — the rename does not
        affect the underlying file association.

  req F-PDF-Upload.dedup-reuses-existing-node:
    given: a PDF file is being dropped onto a Bramble page
    and: the PDF is already present in the Bramble — there is
      an existing Node X wrapping the corresponding Wnfs.File
      (whose `cid` matches the dropped file's bytes)
    when: the drop completes
    then:
      - Node X is placed at the drop site (rendered as a
        structural successor at the drop point);
      - a new `'child'`-kind edge is created with the
        drop-site parent as source and Node X as destination;
      - no new Wnfs.File is created (the existing one is
        reused);
      - no new Bramble.Node is created (X is reused);
      - if the drop-site parent already had a `'child'` edge
        to X, the edge addition is a no-op (`createEdge` is
        idempotent — re-dropping the same file at the same
        drop site doesn't pile up duplicates).

  req F-PDF-Upload.chip-rendering:
    given: a Bramble.Node N whose supertags include a Wnfs.File
      W whose mime type is a PDF (`'application/pdf'`) or whose
      file name ends in `.pdf`
    when: N is rendered as a bullet (either as a structural
      child OR as a zoomed page node)
    then:
      - the bullet's content is the user-renameable text (per
        F-PDF-Upload.drop-seeds-content-with-filename);
      - the #File supertag chip appears after the bullet
        content (standard F-Supertag chip rendering);
      - a small PDF icon (the `ph--file-pdf--regular` glyph)
        appears AFTER the #File chip as a visual indicator
        only — it is NOT interactive (no click action, no
        hover affordance, no navigation);
      - the user opens the PDF file itself via the #File
        supertag's normal navigation affordances (chip click
        → tag Node, OpenInstanceControl → the specific
        Wnfs.File) — not via the PDF icon;
      - the bullet remains zoomable per F-Zoom — its bullet
        dot is unchanged.

  req F-PDF-Upload.no-uploader-graceful:
    given: an environment where no plugin contributes
      `AppCapabilities.FileUploader` (e.g. plugin-wnfs is not
      enabled in this Composer build)
    when: a PDF is dropped on a Bramble page
    then:
      - no Wnfs.File is created, no Bramble.Node is created,
        no upload is attempted;
      - the drop overlay surfaces a "file upload unavailable"
        affordance (a hint text or toast) acknowledging the
        gesture without silently dropping the file.

  req F-PDF-Upload.drop-target-per-row:
    given: a Bramble page is open with one or more bullets
      rendered; the user is dragging a PDF file over the
      article surface
    when: the drag cursor passes over a specific rendered
      bullet row OR over the pending-child placeholder at the
      foot of an expanded parent
    then:
      - the drop-target highlight snaps to the bullet (or
        placeholder) under the cursor — not to the whole
        article surface — so the user can see and target a
        specific structural parent;
      - on `drop` over a bullet, the resulting wrapper-Node
        lands as a child of that bullet's Node (per the
        existing F-PDF-Upload.drop-creates-node /
        F-PDF-Upload.dedup-reuses-existing-node wiring);
      - on `drop` over the pending-child placeholder, the
        resulting wrapper-Node lands as the LAST child of the
        parent the placeholder belongs to (matching the
        placeholder's Enter-key semantics);
      - when the cursor is over neither a bullet nor the
        placeholder (header, gap, backlinks panel, etc.), no
        per-row highlight renders and the drop falls back to
        the page node per the existing rule.

```

```mdl
feat F-Versioning: pinned-target edges preserve the target's snapshot at pin time
  desc: |
    Some Bramble.Edge kinds carry a contract that their target
    should be observed as it was at the moment the edge was
    created, not as it is now. The canonical case (today):
    F-Run's `'is-run-of'` edge — when a Run is created against
    a Step, editing the Step later shouldn't retroactively
    change what that Run displays. The executor saw the Step
    as it was; the Run records that bound moment.

    F-Versioning generalises this via per-edge pinning. An Edge
    of a "pinning" kind captures the target's current version
    (an encoded Automerge heads vector from `Obj.version` /
    `Obj.encodeVersion`) into a `targetVersion` field at edge-
    creation time. At render time, a resolver returns either
    the LIVE target (when `targetVersion` is unset — the legacy
    case) or the TIME-TRAVELED snapshot (when set, via
    `Obj.checkoutVersion`). The user always navigates the live
    Node; the snapshot is only the visible content the resolver
    chooses to render under a pinned edge.

    Mechanism — what we build:
      - `Bramble.Edge` gains optional `targetVersion?: string`
        (encoded Automerge heads).
      - A closed set of pinning `Edge.kind` values (today:
        `'is-run-of'`; new kinds join the set by deliberate
        spec change).
      - `createEdge` auto-captures the heads when the kind is
        in the set — call sites don't choose; the kind's
        contract does.
      - `resolveEdgeTarget(edge)` returns live OR snapshot per
        the field.

    Substrate alignment (substrate-principles §10 — identity is
    mutable and consensual; the substrate retains its history):
      - The pin survives any structural change to the target —
        delete, retire, merge, split — because the substrate
        is non-destructive. Targets get RETIRED, not
        destructively removed; their history stays
        reconstructable via `checkoutVersion`. The resolver
        renders the pinned snapshot regardless of the live
        target's deleted state.
      - When a future "genuine privacy deletion" operation
        lands (separate from normal flows) and removes a
        target entirely, the resolver falls back to a
        `[removed]` placeholder. v1 doesn't implement privacy-
        deletion; the fallback fires only if `checkoutVersion`
        itself fails on a target.
      - Future merge / split (substrate §10) don't relocate
        pins: a pinned edge to a pre-merge / pre-split
        identity continues rendering the pre-event snapshot.
        Re-pinning is an explicit user action, not automatic.

    Use of existing ECHO capabilities:
      - `Obj.version(entity)` reads the current Automerge
        heads.
      - `Obj.encodeVersion(v)` / `Obj.decodeVersion(str)`
        serialise heads to a string suitable for a
        Bramble.Edge field.
      - `checkoutVersion(target, heads)` (from `@dxos/echo-db`)
        time-travels to the target at the heads vector.
        Returns raw data today; Bramble wraps in a local
        hydration helper that exposes the fields the renderer
        reads (`content`, `kind`, etc.) without setting up
        live subscriptions (the snapshot is frozen — nothing
        to subscribe to). Local helper now; promote upstream
        later as the shape stabilises.

    Greenfield migration: pre-versioning `'is-run-of'` edges in
    test spaces have no `targetVersion`. They keep rendering
    the LIVE Step (today's behavior). New edges pin.
    R-Greenfield-Stance — no backfill code.

    Out of scope for v1: a browseable history-of-Node timeline
    UX (the `getEditHistory` walk is available but rendering
    it is a separate increment); explicit re-pinning gestures;
    merge / split operations (separate substrate-principles
    work).

  req F-Versioning.pinning-edge-kinds-set:
    given: a code module that needs to know whether an
      `Edge.kind` value triggers pinning
    when: it consults the canonical set
    then:
      - the set is a single named constant in code (today:
        `PINNING_EDGE_KINDS`) whose authoritative content for
        v1 is exactly `{'is-run-of'}`;
      - new kinds join the set by deliberate spec change (a
        new feat / req entry that names the kind and reasons
        about why pinning applies to it).

  req F-Versioning.auto-pin-on-create:
    given: `createEdge(db, parent, child, { kind })` is called
      with a `kind` value that belongs to `PINNING_EDGE_KINDS`
    when: the edge is created
    then:
      - `targetVersion = Obj.encodeVersion(Obj.version(child))`
        is captured and stored on the Edge as part of the
        same create transaction;
      - call sites do not pass or check a separate "pin"
        argument — the kind's contract is the only signal.

  req F-Versioning.unpinned-edges-render-live:
    given: a Bramble.Edge whose `targetVersion` is unset (the
      kind is not in `PINNING_EDGE_KINDS`, OR the edge
      predates F-Versioning)
    when: `resolveEdgeTarget(edge)` is called
    then:
      - the LIVE target object is returned;
      - reactive subscriptions to the target continue to
        propagate per F-4b live-rename semantics (unchanged
        from today).

  req F-Versioning.pinned-edges-render-snapshot:
    given: a Bramble.Edge whose `targetVersion` is set
    when: `resolveEdgeTarget(edge)` is called
    then:
      - the resolver returns the target's state at the pinned
        heads (via `checkoutVersion(target, decoded)`),
        wrapped by a local hydration helper that exposes the
        fields the renderer reads;
      - the rendered fields reflect the moment the edge was
        created, not the current live state;
      - subsequent edits to the LIVE target produce new heads
        but do not change what this pinned edge displays.

  req F-Versioning.pin-survives-deletion:
    given: a pinned Bramble.Edge points at a target whose live
      state has been marked deleted (via `db.remove`,
      retirement, etc. — non-destructive per
      substrate-principles §10)
    when: `resolveEdgeTarget(edge)` is called
    then:
      - the resolver returns the pinned snapshot — the
        target's Automerge history is still reachable, and
        `checkoutVersion` reads from it regardless of the
        live object's deleted mark;
      - if `checkoutVersion` itself fails (target has been
        removed by an explicit privacy-deletion operation,
        out of v1 scope), the resolver returns a sentinel
        that the renderer surfaces as a `[removed]`
        placeholder.
```

```mdl
feat F-Drag-Drop: drag a Node by its bullet to move it within the Bramble    # Increment Drag-Drop (not started)
  desc: |
    Direct-manipulation reordering: the user click-drags a
    Node X by its bullet, sees a rendering of X (plus its
    visible subtree) following the cursor, sees a visual
    indicator (a dropspot) over the slot the drop would
    commit to, and commits the move by releasing the cursor.
    The move rewires X's incoming `'child'` Edge from its
    old position to the new position per R-Edges-First-Class
    and substrate-principles §10 (non-destructive identity —
    X's identity is preserved across the move; its history
    remains reachable via `Obj.checkoutVersion`).

    Cycle prevention: dropspots inside X's own subtree are
    not eligible during the drag — the user is never
    visually invited toward a slot that would make X a
    descendant of itself. The same cycle predicate
    `createEdge` enforces per F-DAG.Phase5.

    Interaction with R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet:
    every pending row has a dropspot that, when committed,
    routes X through the pending row's `onAddExisting(X)`
    path — promoting the pending row's slot by attaching X
    structurally to the pending row's parent at the slot
    per R-Edges-First-Class.

  req F-Drag-Drop.click-drag-starts-drag:
    given: I click to drag on the bullet for an existing
      Node X
    when: I am dragging Node X
    then:
      - a rendering of X — including the expand/collapse
        indicator, the bullet, X's content, and X's visible
        subtree — follows the cursor;
      - X in its original location on the page is rendered
        dimmed.

  req F-Drag-Drop.dragging-shows-dropspot-indicator:
    given: I am dragging some Node X
    when: my cursor goes over a dropspot for X (per
      F-Drag-Drop.dropspot-placement +
      F-Drag-Drop.dropspot-rendering)
    then:
      - a visual indicator for the dropspot underneath the
        cursor is rendered per
        F-Drag-Drop.dropspot-rendering.

  req F-Drag-Drop.drop-changes-edges:
    given: I am dragging some Node X
    when: I release the cursor over a dropspot
    then:
      - the incoming `'child'` Edge from X's old parent to
        X at the dragged-from occurrence is removed;
      - a new `'child'` Edge from the dropspot's parent to
        X is created at the dropspot's slot, with an
        `order` landing X at the correct position via
        `orderBetween` per R-Edges-First-Class;
      - X's identity is preserved (the same Bramble.Node)
        — per substrate-principles §10, the move is non-
        destructive; X's history remains reachable via
        `Obj.version` / `Obj.checkoutVersion`.

  req F-Drag-Drop.dropspot-placement:
    given: a Bramble pane displays a tree of Nodes
    and: the user is dragging some Node X (per
      F-Drag-Drop.click-drag-starts-drag)
    when: the renderer evaluates the visible tree for
      dropspots
    then:
      - every visible Node N has two between-rows
        dropspots — one immediately above N and one
        immediately below N in N's parent's children list
        — each at N's structural depth;
      - each between-rows dropspot's left edge aligns with
        N's bullet column (including N's chevron / expand-
        collapse control); its right edge is the right
        edge of the pane's body area;
      - every visible pending row R (per
        R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet — leaf-row-end,
        page-root, or sibling-slot pending rows alike) has
        a pending-row dropspot at R's slot, sized to
        surround R's bullet column through the right edge
        of the pane's body area;
      - when N is the last visible descendant in an
        ancestor A's expanded subtree, N's below-dropspot
        and A's below-dropspot coexist vertically — each
        at its own structural indent (N's deeper than
        A's), so both are reachable by horizontal cursor
        position;
      - each between-rows dropspot semantically represents
        a sibling slot — the above-N dropspot is the
        position immediately before N in N's parent's
        children list, and the below-N dropspot is the
        position immediately after N;
      - each pending-row dropspot semantically represents
        the pending row's slot — dropping into it routes
        through the pending row's `onAddExisting(X)` path
        per R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet (the pending
        row's slot is filled by attaching X via a
        structural `'child'` Edge per R-Edges-First-Class);
      - the above-X and below-X dropspots (whose slots
        correspond to dropping X immediately before /
        after itself in X's current parent's children
        list) are NOT in the dropspot set during the drag
        — they are no-ops;
      - any dropspot that lives within X's own subtree
        (whose slot would make X a descendant of itself,
        creating a cycle) is NOT in the dropspot set
        during the drag — the same cycle-prevention
        predicate `createEdge` enforces per F-DAG.Phase5;
      - X's original location is rendered dimmed (per
        F-Drag-Drop.click-drag-starts-drag); the dimmed
        region contains no eligible dropspots, matching
        the visual cue that those slots are not valid
        drop targets.

  req F-Drag-Drop.dropspot-rendering:
    given: a Bramble pane displays one or more dropspots
      per F-Drag-Drop.dropspot-placement
    and: the user is dragging some Node X
    when: the cursor moves over the visible tree
    then:
      - at any moment, at most one dropspot is "active" —
        the dropspot the user would commit X to if they
        released the cursor at the current position;
      - the active dropspot is determined by the cursor's
        position:
        - vertically: the cursor's y-coordinate selects
          the vertical band between two visible rows (or
          the band over a pending row); dropspots whose
          flanking rows bracket this band are candidates;
        - horizontally: among candidates that coexist at
          the same vertical band (per
          F-Drag-Drop.dropspot-placement's coexistence
          clause), the dropspot whose structural indent
          is closest to the cursor's x-coordinate is the
          active one;
      - the active dropspot renders visually:
        - a between-rows dropspot renders as a blue
          horizontal line spanning from the dropspot's
          left edge to the right edge of the pane's body
          area;
        - a pending-row dropspot renders as a box: dark-
          blue outline, light-blue fill; the pending
          row's faint bullet and editable area remain
          visible inside the box;
      - dropspots that are NOT the active one do not
        render visually — the user sees at most one
        dropspot indicator at any time;
      - when the cursor is not over any dropspot's region
        (e.g., over the H1, over a chip, over the side
        rail, over empty space at the bottom of the page
        beyond the page-root pending row), no dropspot is
        active and no indicator renders.
```

## Acceptance

```mdl
test T-1: Increment 1 — scaffold renders
  given: composer-app is running with isLabs enabled
  when: user creates a Bramble graph from the navigator
  then:
    - Graph + empty root Node appear in ECHO
    - Article surface renders the placeholder
    - no console errors
```

```mdl
test T-2: Increment 2 — editable bullet round-trips
  given: composer-app is running, a Bramble graph has been created
  when: user types "hello world" into the editor and reloads the page
  then:
    - Node.content === [{ kind: 'text', text: 'hello world' }]
    - on reload, the editor mounts with "hello world" visible
    - no console errors attributable to plugin-blocks
```

```mdl
test T-3: Increment 3 — Enter creates a new sibling
  given: a Bramble graph with a single bullet "first"
  when: user places cursor at end of "first" and presses Enter, then types "second"
  then:
    - rootNode.children has two Node refs in order
    - first Node.content === [{ kind: 'text', text: 'first' }]
    - second Node.content === [{ kind: 'text', text: 'second' }]
    - on reload, both bullets render with original text
```

```mdl
test T-4: Increment 3 — DXN copy
  given: a Bramble graph with a bullet, navigator.clipboard available
  when: user right-clicks the bullet
  then: Obj.getDXN(node) is written to the clipboard
```

```mdl
test T-5: Increment 3b — Tab indents
  given: an outline with two top-level bullets [A, B]
  when: cursor is in B and user presses Tab
  then:
    - rootNode.children === [A] (B removed from top level)
    - A.children === [B] (B is now A's child)
    - B.children unchanged (B keeps its own subtree)
    - B's editor has focus after re-mount
```

```mdl
test T-6: Increment 3b — Shift+Tab dedents
  given: an outline where A.children === [B]
  when: cursor is in B and user presses Shift+Tab
  then:
    - A.children === [] (B removed from A)
    - rootNode.children === [A, B] (B is now sibling of A)
    - B's editor has focus after re-mount
```

```mdl
test T-7: Increment 4 — @-mention inserts a ref
  given: an outline with at least one other ECHO object available in the space
  when: user types "hello @" then types a query that matches an object's label, then clicks the object
  then:
    - the @-and-query range is replaced by an inline ref node
    - Node.content contains a RefSpan segment with target pointing at the chosen object
    - the rendered text shows the target's current label
    - on reload the ref persists with the same DXN
```

```mdl
test T-9: Increment V2 — collapse / expand parent
  given: an outline where bullet A has one child bullet B
  when: user clicks A's bullet marker
  then:
    - A's bullet renders in the collapsed (hollow) style with a "1" badge
    - B is no longer visible in the tree
    - A.state.expanded === false in ECHO
  when: user clicks A's bullet marker again
  then:
    - A's bullet renders in the open (filled) style
    - B is visible again
    - A.state.expanded === true (or the field is reset)
```

```mdl
test T-V4-live: Adding a ref increments the target's count without leaving the source
  given: an outline with a Node X (currently being edited) and another Node Y; Y currently has zero or N references
  when: user inserts an inline ref to Y inside X — e.g. types @ in X, picks Y from the picker
  then:
    - within one paint frame after the ref node renders inside X, Y's count badge displays previous_count + 1
    - the user does not need to press Enter, leave X, navigate away, or reload for the badge to appear
```

```mdl
test T-10: Increment 5 — backlinks panel lists external refs
  given: outline X has bullet B; outline Y has a bullet whose content @-mentions B
  when: user opens outline X
  then: the "Linked references" panel below X's tree lists the source bullet from Y, with label and target label
  when: outline X has no incoming refs
  then: no panel renders
```

```mdl
test T-8: Increment 4b — rename propagates without local edit
  given: an outline that contains an inline ref to a target object T with label "alpha"
  when: T is renamed to "beta" by another panel or peer (no local edit in the outline)
  then:
    - within one tick, the rendered label in the outline updates from "alpha" to "beta"
    - no manual reload or focus-change is required
```

```mdl
test T-Cmd-Shift-Enter-Pending-Row-Above: Cmd+Shift+Enter renders a pending row at the sibling slot above the editing Node
  given: a Bramble with a single real `Bramble.Node` X carrying
    non-empty content under a parent P
  and: I am editing X
  when: I press Cmd+Shift+Enter (Mod+Shift+Enter)
  then:
    - a pending row Y renders at the sibling slot immediately
      before X under P per R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet;
    - the rendered children of P are, in order: pending row Y,
      then X;
    - focus is on Y; the caret renders at Y's editable area
      per F-Pending-Child.cursor-alignment;
    - X's content remains unchanged.
```

```mdl
test T-Caret: focus paths produce a single visible caret
  given: an outline with two top-level bullets X (with text) and Y (empty)
  when: X's or Y's editor receives focus via any path (click, sibling
    creation, ArrowUp/ArrowDown nav)
  then:
    - the editor is the active element (document.activeElement is the
      .ProseMirror DIV)
    - exactly ONE caret is visible (Safari and Chromium both)
    - for X (non-empty): the selection anchor is a Text node and the
      caret sits inside the text content
    - for Y (empty): the paragraph carries the
      `block-empty-paragraph` class added by `caret-fix-plugin` and
      has `min-width: 1rem` so the native browser caret has somewhere
      to paint; the rendered rectangle is exactly one line tall
```

```mdl
test T-Headline: zoomed headline renders refs alongside text
  given: a Node X whose content is the segments
    [text "what is in ", ref→A, text " and what ", ref→B, text " now"]
    where A's label is "alpha" and B's label is "beta"
  when: I zoom into X
  then:
    - the H1 contains text content "what is in alpha and what beta now"
    - the H1 contains 2 child elements with class `block-ref` (the ref spans)
    - each `block-ref` span is link-blue and shows the target's label
  when: A is renamed from "alpha" to "ALPHA"
  then: within one tick, the H1 reflects "what is in ALPHA and what beta now"
    without any reload or focus change
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-picker-lists-types: # picker offers every registered ECHO type as an option
  given: a space whose plugins have registered one or more typed
    ECHO schemas (e.g. Task, Person, Document)
  when: I am editing a bullet and I type `#`
  then:
    - a picker opens and lists every registered ECHO type as a
      selectable `#`-prefixed entry. Each entry's label is the
      type's display title (e.g. `#Task`, `#Person`,
      `#Document`).
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-picker-excludes-relations: # picker does not offer relation-typed schemas
  given: a space whose plugins have registered "relation" types —
    ECHO types whose role is to describe an edge between two
    other entities rather than to be an entity in their own right
  when: I open the `#` picker
  then:
    - relation types do NOT appear as picker entries. A bullet
      can BE a Task or a Person; a bullet cannot BE an edge
      between two other things.
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-picker-excludes-self-types: # picker does not offer the Bramble graphr's own types
  given: a space in which the user has at least one block
    outline (so the outliner's own ECHO types are registered
    alongside the rest)
  when: I open the `#` picker
  then:
    - I do NOT see `#Node-outline` or `#Node` in the picker.
      Tagging a bullet with the outliner's own scaffolding
      would let the bullet "be" a copy of itself or its
      container — a recursive arrangement that doesn't make
      sense to the user.
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-picker-sorted: # picker entries are alphabetical, case-insensitive
  given: a space with types whose display titles are "Zebra",
    "apple", and "Banana"
  when: I open the `#` picker without filtering
  then: the entries appear in the order "apple", "Banana",
    "Zebra".
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-schema-shows-all-types: every type has a supertag node under Schema
  given: a space whose plugins have registered typed ECHO
    schemas (filtered per F-Supertag.types-shown)
  when: I open the Bramble graphr and zoom into the Schema
    system node
  then: for every registered type, there is a corresponding
    supertag node among Schema's children. Each supertag node's
    display label is the type's title (e.g. `Task`, `Person`,
    `Document`); zooming into one lists every instance of that
    type in the space.
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-mat-idempotent: supertag node set matches the allowed-type set, idempotently
  given: a space with a current set T of allowed ECHO types
    (the same exclusion rule that F-Supertag.types-shown
    defines for the picker)
  when: the system establishes supertag nodes for these types,
    one or more times, in any order
  then:
    - for every type in T there is exactly one supertag node
      in the space.
    - the supertag node for a given type carries a stable
      identity across successive establishments.
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-mat-live: newly registered types get a supertag node live
  given: I am viewing the Bramble graphr; some plugin
    registers a new typed ECHO schema (that passes
    F-Supertag.types-shown's filter) after the outliner has
    mounted
  when: the schema registration commits
  then: within one tick the new type has a supertag node
    among Schema's children, and I see the new entry as a
    child of the Schema node.
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-picker-creates-fresh-instance: applying #T creates a new typed instance
  given: a bullet whose content is "Buy milk" and zero
    supertags
  when: I open the `#` picker on this bullet and select T
  then:
    - a new instance of type T is created in ECHO (verifiable
      via a query for type T's instances in the space — one
      more instance exists than before).
    - the "Buy milk" node is associated with the new instance
      (its supertags array now references this instance).
    - the new instance's label is "Buy milk" (seeded per
      T-Supertag-title-seed-outliner).
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-promote-reuses-existing: clicking an already-associated instance navigates to its existing node
  given: an instance X of type T; a node N in the outline is
    already associated with X via `#T`; X's row appears in
    the `#T` supertag node's query view
  when: I click X's row in the query view
  then:
    - the outliner navigates to N.
    - the association set for (X, `#T`) remains {N}
      (F-Supertag.uniqueness is preserved on this path).
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-uniq-sweep: normalisation collapses duplicate supertag associations
  given: a space in inconsistent state — two distinct nodes
    N1 and N2 both have `supertags` referencing the SAME
    instance X of type T (left over from some earlier
    broken flow)
  when: the system runs the uniqueness-normalisation sweep
    for this space (one or more times, in any order)
  then:
    - the association set for (X, `#T`) becomes {canonical}
      — exactly one node, selected by lower `Node.id` per
      F-Supertag.uniqueness's tie-break.
    - any other supertag-Refs on N1 and N2 (for types
      other than T or instances other than X) are
      preserved.
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-title-seed-outliner: bullet text seeds the new instance's title
  given: a bullet whose content is "Buy milk" and zero
    supertags; type T (e.g. `#Task`) is registered
  when: I open the `#` picker on this bullet and select T
  then:
    - the bullet still reads "Buy milk".
    - the new type-T instance attached to this bullet (per
      T-Supertag-picker-creates-fresh-instance) has its label
      set to "Buy milk".
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-title-seed-from-instance: clicking an existing instance creates a node carrying its label
  given: an instance X of type T with label "Quarterly
    Review"; no node in the outline is currently associated
    with X via `#T`
  when: I click X's row in the `#T` supertag node's query
    view
  then:
    - a new node appears (parented per F-6.Phase3b.promote).
    - the new node's bullet text reads "Quarterly Review"
      (seeded from X's label per F-Supertag.title-sync's
      externally-originated path).
    - the new node's supertag references X.
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-title-write: typing in the bullet renames the typed instance
  given: a bullet N tagged with supertag `#T` (e.g. `#Task`);
    N's linked instance has label "old"
  when: I focus the bullet, select all, and type "new"
  then:
    - the bullet reads "new".
    - N's linked instance has label "new" within one tick
      (per F-Supertag.title-sync's steady-state write path).
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-title-read: renaming a typed instance updates the bullet targeting it
  given: a bullet N tagged with supertag `#T` (e.g. `#Task`)
    whose linked instance has label "old"; the outliner is
    mounted and N is visible
  when: another surface renames the linked instance from
    "old" to "new" (e.g. a write via Composer's article
    view, a navtree rename, an automation)
  then:
    - within one tick the bullet N reads "new" (per
      F-Supertag.title-sync's steady-state read path).
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-title-no-label-noop: types without a designated label still permit typing
  given: a bullet tagged with `#T` where T's schema doesn't
    designate any field as its label (no canonical "title"
    or "name" field for the sync to write into)
  when: I type any text into the bullet
  then:
    - the bullet's content updates to reflect the typed text,
      same as for any other bullet.
    - the typed instance's fields remain at their prior
      values — the title-sync write path has no label-field
      to target on this schema, so it leaves the instance
      alone (per F-Supertag.title-sync's degradation
      behaviour).
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-title-hidden-in-fields: FieldGroup shows the typed instance's non-label fields
  given: a bullet N tagged with supertag `#T` (e.g. `#Task`,
    whose schema designates `title` as its label field); N
    is expanded
  when: the FieldGroup renders below the bullet
  then:
    - the FieldGroup renders exactly one row per non-label
      field declared by T's current schema (per
      F-Supertag.title-hidden-in-fields). Examples of such
      fields for Task at time of writing: Priority, Status,
      Assigned, Estimate, Description, Project — the exact
      set tracks whatever non-label fields T's schema
      currently declares.
```

```mdl
test T-Supertag-chip-click-zoom: clicking the chip navigates the current pane to the supertag node
  given: a bullet N tagged with supertag `#T` (e.g. `#Task`)
