```mdl
test T-Supertag-browse-reactivity: query row label updates live as the instance is renamed
  given: I am zoomed into the `#T` supertag node (e.g.
    `#Task`); its query view lists one row for an instance
    X of type T with label "alpha"
  when: another surface renames X from "alpha" to "beta"
    (via rename in Composer, an automation, etc.)
  then:
    - within one tick the query row's label reads "beta"
      (per F-Supertag.title-sync's read path applied to the
      query-row label resolver).
```

```mdl
test T-Promote-Two-Query-External: an external-typename supertag-node renders both orphan + wrapped queries
  given: a Bramble outline mounted in a space; an external
    ECHO type `T` (e.g. `org.dxos.type.task`) with at
    least one orphan instance `X` (no wrapping Bramble.Node)
    and at least one wrapped instance `Y` (some Bramble.Node
    references `Y` via `supertags`)
  when: the user navigates to the supertag-node for `T`
  then:
    - the body renders two distinct query sections, in
      order: Query A (orphans) then Query B (wrapped);
    - Query A's row set contains `X` (and any other orphan
      instances of `T`); Query B's row set contains the
      Bramble.Node wrapping `Y` (and any other wrappers);
    - both sections render a user-readable header;
    - no row appears in both sections — the partition is
      exact.
```

```mdl
test T-Promote-One-Query-Bramble-Owned: a Bramble-defined supertag-node renders only the wrapped query
  given: a Bramble outline mounted in a space; a Bramble-
    defined ECHO type whose typename starts with
    `org.dxos.type.bramble.` (e.g. `Bramble.Step` or
    `Bramble.Run`); at least one Bramble.Node carrying the
    supertag
  when: the user navigates to the supertag-node for that
    type
  then:
    - the body renders Query B (wrapped Bramble.Nodes) only;
    - no Query A section is rendered — no header, no empty-
      state hint, no orphan rows;
    - the same rule applies when zero wrapped instances
      exist (Query B renders its empty-state hint; Query A
      remains absent).
```

```mdl
test T-Promote-Row-Click-Creates-Wrapper-Under-Today: clicking an orphan row wraps the instance + attaches the wrapper to Today
  given: a Bramble outline mounted in a space; an external
    ECHO type `T` with an orphan instance `X` displayed in
    Query A of the supertag-node for `T`; today's Day-Node
    exists (or is find-or-created on first reference per
    F-Today)
  when: the user clicks the Query A row showing `X`
  then:
    - a wrapper Bramble.Node `W` is created (or
      find-or-create-reused, per F-Supertag.uniqueness)
      with `supertags: [Ref(X)]` and `content` seeded from
      `Obj.getLabel(X)`;
    - an `Edge` of `kind: 'child'` is created from today's
      Day-Node to `W` via `createEdge` (per
      R-Edges-First-Class);
    - the Query A row reactively moves to Query B without
      remount;
    - the current pane navigates to `W`.
```

```mdl
test T-Promote-Row-Click-Reuses-Existing-Wrapper: clicking an orphan-shaped row that already has a wrapper navigates instead of duplicating
  given: a Bramble outline mounted in a space; an external
    ECHO type `T` with instance `X`; a wrapper `W`
    already exists for `X` (perhaps created concurrently
    by another pane, or by a prior promote / drop-create
    gesture not yet reflected in this pane's render); the
    user sees a Query A row for `X` due to a stale render
  when: the user clicks the Query A row showing `X`
  then:
    - NO new wrapper is created (`F-Supertag.uniqueness`
      enforces at-most-one wrapper per (instance,
      typename));
    - the current pane navigates to the existing `W`;
    - the supertag-node body re-derives and the stale
      Query A row vanishes (now appearing in Query B).
```

```mdl
test T-Create-Filter-And-Pick: typing into the create-object dialog narrows the type list; click commits
  given: I have opened the create-object dialog ("+") in a space
    that has the Bramble plugin enabled
  when: I type a substring of a type name (e.g. "bra" for Bramble)
    into the type search input
  then:
    - the rendered option list narrows to entries whose label
      fuzzy-matches the typed substring; the cursor
      (aria-selected) lands on the highest-scored entry so that
      pressing Enter would commit it
    - the typed substring persists across parent re-renders —
      the filter remains in effect for the duration of the
      user's typing session
    - clicking a remaining entry commits the selection and
      creates an instance of that type; the dialog closes and
      the new object opens in the pane
    - hovering with the mouse highlights the hovered item with
      a clearly visible selection state
  desc: |
    Reported 2026-05-13 as: "the selection of menu items is not
    working — i can't select Bramble and what I do select isn't
    clear". Diagnosis split: the "can't select" half traced to a
    different setup (port 5173 / a separate composer-app
    instance, not this worktree) and is out of scope here.
    What's reproducible against THIS worktree's preview build is
    a narrower pair of upstream defects:

    1. Filter is inert. Typing into the type-search input does
       not narrow the option list — every registered ECHO type
       remains visible and the cursor stays on whichever entry
       auto-selected first (alphabetically, typically "Agent").
       Targeted selection of Bramble (or any other entry) is
       possible by scrolling and clicking; the filter is a
       UX-affordance regression, not a blocker.

       Root cause: `useSearchListResults` in
       @dxos/react-ui-search auto-resets its internal query
       state whenever the `items` prop arrives with a fresh
       array identity. `CreateObjectDialog` rebuilds `options`
       each render (its deps include `manager.capabilities`
       lookups and a `viewTypenames` Set that recomputes on
       schema-registry runs), so any parent re-render between
       the keystroke and the 200 ms `onSearch` debounce wipes
       the user's query.

    2. Mouse hover does not visibly highlight items. The
       `Picker.Item` ships a low-contrast `:hover` background
       (a 97%-light grey on a near-white dialog body) AND keeps
       the keyboard cursor (`aria-selected`, ~89% grey)
       decoupled from the mouse cursor — so users see neither
       a prominent hover-highlight nor a single moving cursor
       that tracks the mouse.

    Both root causes live UPSTREAM of plugin-bramble —
    `useSearchListResults` (in @dxos/react-ui-search) for (1)
    and `Picker.Item` (in @dxos/react-ui-list) for (2). They
    affect every consumer of those primitives, not Bramble
    specifically. Fixes are deliberately OUT OF SCOPE for this
    plugin and are flagged in `## Remaining` for a future
    upstream coordination.

    Captured here so the Bramble create-flow UX expectations
    are documented end-to-end; the test will pass once the
    upstream fixes land.
```

```mdl
test T-Add-Existing-Via-At-Picker-Edges-Not-Duplicates: @-picker selection of an existing Node adds an edge, never creates a new Node
  given: a Bramble outline at the page root with three sibling
    Nodes — `happy`, `111`, and `222` — all collapsed (per F-V2.12)
  when: the user expands `111` (chevron click) and types `@happy`
    into the row-level pending-child of `111`, then commits the
    selection of the `happy` entry by either:
      (a) clicking the entry with the mouse (mousedown), OR
      (b) pressing Enter while the entry is the picker's active item
        (per F-4.7 keyboard nav)
  then:
    - a single `Bramble.Edge` is created with source=`111` and
      target=the EXISTING `happy` Node (no `Bramble.makeNode` call;
      no new Node)
    - the `happy` Node now has two structural predecessors
      (the page root and `111`) and renders with the multi-parent
      badge "2" everywhere it appears in the outline (per
      F-DAG.Phase3e.multi-parent-badge)
    - the pending-child of `111` is cleared and the page-root
      pending-child below `111`'s subtree continues to render per
      F-Pending-Child.page-root
  when: the user expands `222` and repeats the gesture (`@happy` +
    commit)
  then:
    - a second edge is created with source=`222` and target=the
      same existing `happy` Node — STILL no new Node is created
    - the badge on every `happy` instance now reads "3" (root, 111,
      222 all point at the same target)
  when: the user zooms into any `happy` bullet
  then:
    - the predecessor-nav control shows three predecessors: the
      page root (label `(unnamed)`), `111`, and `222` —
      rendered inline per F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-inline
      (count ≤ 3)
  desc: |
    Bug reported 2026-05-14:
    > "has created a new node for each instance I added @happy …
    >  so that when I zoom into happy that is a child of 111, i see
    >  the only incoming node as '111' … also when I open the @
    >  menu I see multiple 'happy' nodes in the menu items"

    Root cause (verified empirically on the worktree): the
    MentionPicker had no keyboard navigation. The intended commit
    path was a mouse click on the picker entry, which routes
    through `onSelect` → `onAddExisting` → `createEdge` (correct:
    no new Node). But users habitually press Enter after typing the
    query, and pressing Enter inside a plain `<div contenteditable>`
    (the pending-child's editable) inserted a newline into the
    text instead of committing. The picker's substring filter then
    failed to match (because the text included the newline);
    typically users backspaced back to a state where the
    pending-child's `handleInput` saw text WITHOUT a leading `@`
    and routed through `onPromote` — which DOES create a new Node.

    Reproduction with mouse-click on `worktree@2026-05-14`: works
    correctly — `@happy` + click → edge added, multi-parent badge
    increments, no duplicate Nodes. The bug manifests only on the
    Enter-without-click path.

    Fix: F-4.7 adds keyboard nav to MentionPicker (Arrow + Enter
    via window-capture, mirroring F-6.Phase1.keyboard-nav for the
    `#` picker), so pressing Enter while the picker is open
    commits the active item through the same `onSelect` path the
    mouse-click flow uses. The fall-through to native
    contenteditable Enter handling is now closed. F-4.6 already
    swallows modifier-Enter chords so they don't accidentally
    create siblings.
```

```mdl
test T-Sibling-Above-Lands-Between: Cmd+Shift+Enter on Y renders a pending row at the slot between X and Y
  given: a fresh Bramble at the page root with exactly two real
    sibling Nodes in document order — X with content "XXX" and
    Y with content "YYY"; both are direct children of the page
    node and have no children of their own
  when: the cursor is in Y's editor and the user presses
    Cmd+Shift+Enter (Mod+Shift+Enter)
  then:
    - a pending row Z renders at the sibling slot between X and
      Y under the page node per F-Cmd-Shift-Enter.1 +
      R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet;
    - the page body now renders three rows in this exact order:
      ● XXX, ● (pending Z), ● YYY — immediately followed by the
      F-Pending-Child.page-root row at the end;
    - focus is on Z; Z's editable area shows a visible caret
      per F-Caret.1;
    - X.content === "XXX" and Y.content === "YYY" are
      unchanged.
  desc: |
    Reported 2026-05-14 to clarify the multi-sibling placement
    of Cmd+Shift+Enter. Rewritten 2026-05-17 under
    R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet — the row at the slot
    between X and Y is a pending row (render-only) until first
    meaningful input promotes it to a real Bramble.Node at the
    slot per F-Pending-Child.sibling-slot.

    Caveat for legacy Brambles: when a parent's children are
    still mixed between `Node.children` (legacy array) and
    `Bramble.Edge` relations during the F-DAG transition,
    `getStructuralChildren` renders all legacy children FIRST
    and edges AFTER (see edges.ts comment, F-DAG Phase 3d).
    `insertSiblingBefore` calls `ensureMigratedChildren` to
    port the parent's legacy children to edges before placing
    the new edge, but in a polluted test space the migration
    can leave the new edge sorted after pre-existing legacy
    entries. The test is therefore stated against a fresh
    Bramble; the legacy-mixed case is a transitional concern
    documented separately.
```

```mdl
test T-Sibling-Below-Lands-Between: Shift+Enter on X renders a pending row at the slot between X and Y
  given: a fresh Bramble at the page root with exactly two real
    sibling Nodes in document order — X with content "XXX" and
    Y with content "YYY"; both are direct children of the page
    node
  when: the cursor is in X's editor and the user presses
    Shift+Enter
  then:
    - a pending row Z renders at the sibling slot between X and
      Y under the page node per F-Shift-Enter.1 +
      R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet;
    - the page body now renders three rows in this exact order:
      ● XXX, ● (pending Z), ● YYY — immediately followed by the
      F-Pending-Child.page-root row at the end;
    - focus is on Z; Z's editable area shows a visible caret
      per F-Caret.1;
    - X.content === "XXX" and Y.content === "YYY" are
      unchanged.
  desc: |
    Reported 2026-05-14 alongside T-Sibling-Above-Lands-Between.
    Rewritten 2026-05-17 under R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet
    — the slot between X and Y carries a pending row (render-
    only) until first meaningful input promotes it to a real
    Bramble.Node at the slot per F-Pending-Child.sibling-slot.

    The same legacy-vs-edges caveat from
    T-Sibling-Above-Lands-Between applies.
```

```mdl
test T-Pending-Row-Mention-Commit-Attaches-At-Slot: committing an @-mention target on a pending row creates a structural Edge from the parent to the target at the row's slot
  given: a Bramble with parent `P` whose children are real
    sibling Nodes `X` (content "alpha") and `Y` (content
    "beta"), in that order
  and: a pending row `Z` rendered at the sibling slot between
    `X` and `Y` (e.g. created by pressing Shift+Enter on `X`
    per F-Shift-Enter.1)
  and: a real `Bramble.Node` `T` exists elsewhere in the
    space carrying meaningful state (e.g. wrapping a Wnfs.File
    PDF per F-PDF-Upload, or any other Bramble.Node)
  and: focus is on `Z` with the caret in the editable area
  when: I type `@` followed by a substring matching `T`'s
    resolved display label, and commit the mention target `T`
    from the picker (per F-4 + R-Bramble-Surfaces-Wrap-Only)
  then:
    - a structural `'child'` Edge is created from `P` to `T`
      at the slot between `X` and `Y` via `createEdge` per
      F-Pending-Child.sibling-slot + R-Edges-First-Class,
      preserving the X / T / Y order under `P`;
    - the pending row's render is replaced by `T` at the
      slot;
    - `T`'s content, supertags, edges, and fields remain as
      they were (the mention attaches `T` structurally);
    - focus moves to `T`'s rendered position at the slot.
```

```mdl
test T-Backspace-To-Empty-Retires-Node: backspacing through the last meaningful state of a Node retires it via incoming-edge removal; the renderer projects a pending row at the vacated slot
  given: a Bramble with parent `P` whose children are real
    sibling Nodes `X` (content "a") and `Y` (content "beta"),
    in that order
  and: `X` has empty `supertags`, empty `children`, no
    outgoing Edges, empty `fields` (`X` carries meaningful
    state on the `content` axis only)
  and: I am editing `X` with the caret positioned at the end
    of its content
  when: I press Backspace until `X`'s content is empty
  then:
    - the incoming `'child'` Edge from `P` to `X` is removed
      via the Edge-deletion path R-Edges-First-Class governs
      per F-Retire-Empty-Node.1;
    - a pending row renders at the sibling slot between `P`'s
      first child and `Y` (where `X` was rendered) per
      R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet;
    - focus moves to the pending row; the caret renders at
      the editable area per F-Pending-Child.cursor-alignment;
    - `X` remains in ECHO with full history; `Obj.version(X)`
      and prior versions retrievable via `Obj.checkoutVersion`
      per F-Versioning's substrate alignment.
```

```mdl
test T-New-Sibling-Focus-And-Caret: Shift+Enter / Cmd+Shift+Enter render a pending row at the appropriate sibling slot with focus and a visible caret
  given: a Bramble contains a real `Bramble.Node` X that is
    being edited (the cursor is inside X's ProseMirror editor)
  when: the user presses Shift+Enter
  then:
    - a pending row Y renders at the sibling slot immediately
      after X under X's parent per F-Shift-Enter.1 +
      R-Pending-Row-Is-The-Empty-Bullet;
    - focus shifts from X to Y: Y's editable area's
      `autoFocus={focusId === Y's pending-row id}` flips true on
      the same render, so the editable area's mount effect calls
      `view.focus()` and ProseMirror sets the selection to
      `TextSelection.atStart`;
    - Y's editable area renders a single visible caret per
      F-Caret.1 (the `min-width: 1rem` rule in
      `block-editor.css` gives the empty paragraph somewhere to
      paint).
  when: the user presses Cmd+Shift+Enter instead (Mod+Shift+Enter)
  then:
    - same outcome but Y renders at the sibling slot immediately
      before X under X's parent per F-Cmd-Shift-Enter.1.
  when: X has a trailing `#` so the hash picker is visibly OPEN
    at the time of keypress
  then:
    - Shift+Enter / Cmd+Shift+Enter are N/A (per user
      clarification 2026-05-14 and the modifier clause in
      F-6.Phase1.keyboard-nav): the picker swallows them as
      no-ops; the picker remains open with the active item
      unchanged. The user dismisses the picker (Escape / click
      outside / commit a tag), THEN the sibling-creation
      gestures take effect.
  desc: |
    Reported 2026-05-14 as a basic sibling-creation scenario.
    Existing reqs F-Shift-Enter.1, F-Cmd-Shift-Enter.1, and
    F-Caret.1 already cover the bare case end-to-end; this test
    captures the combined behavior plus the user's clarification
    that sibling-creation gestures are N/A while a picker is
    open. Two-step bug discovery on the same day: (1) earlier in
    the day, `TagPicker`'s keydown handler matched
    `event.key === 'Enter'` without checking modifiers, so
    Shift+Enter / Cmd+Enter / etc. all committed the picker's
    active item — pressing Shift+Enter on `alpha #` silently
    applied `#AccessToken`. (2) An intermediate fix was made to
    let modified-Enter chords fall through to the editor's
    keymap so siblings COULD be created mid-picker; the user
    clarified this was the wrong direction — the correct UX is
    to N/A those chords while the picker is open. Final fix:
    in `TagPicker`'s keydown handler, swallow Enter with
    `shiftKey || metaKey` (preventDefault + stopPropagation, no
    commit); commit only on bare Enter; bare ArrowUp/Down still
    nav, Cmd+ArrowUp/Down still pass through.
```

```mdl
test T-Page-Last-Node-Has-Pending-Child-Below: a page with bullets shows a pending-child after the last one
  given: a Bramble outline whose page node has 1 or more real
    children rendered as bullets (e.g. a top-level outline with
    a few notes, OR a sub-node that has been zoomed into and
    already has at least one child)
  when: the pane renders
  then:
    - a faint pending-child row is present immediately below the
      last real child, at the same indent level (the page body's
      indented column)
    - typing into the pending-child promotes a new Node and
      appends it as the LAST child of the page node (the new
      Node becomes the new "last bullet" and the pending-child
      slides down beneath it on the next render — so there is
      always a pending-child at the end of the list)
    - the existing in-list bullets are unaffected (no re-order,
      no focus disruption); only the pending-child position
      shifts to below the new last child
  desc: |
    Reported 2026-05-14 with a Tana-style reference screenshot
    showing `● this is the last node on a page` followed by a
    faint `○` underneath. Current behavior on this worktree:
    after my F-Pending-Child.page-root landed earlier today,
    the page-root pending-child only appeared when childRefs was
    EMPTY (`childRefs.length === 0`). A page with bullets had
    no visible "add another bullet" affordance — the user had
    to press Enter on the last bullet to extend the list. The
    fix flips `showPageRootPendingChild` to always true in
    `Graph.tsx`, so the pending-child renders after `.map`-ed
    children and before the page's bottom edge.
```

```mdl
test T-Zoom-Empty-Node-New-Pane-Untitled-And-Autofocus: shift-click an empty node → new pane with cursor in header, Untitled placeholder, and pending child as only body content
  given: a Bramble outline contains a Node X with no content
    (X.content is empty / has only an empty text segment) and
    no children
  when: the user shift-clicks X's bullet (F-Open-Pane.1) so a
    new pane P' opens rooted at X
  then:
    - the H1 area of P' displays the placeholder "Untitled" in
      a muted color (per F-Page-Header.4 revised); the placeholder
      is not in X.content (verifiable: `X.content` remains empty)
    - the H1 editor has FOCUS — the caret is inside the H1, in
      front of/behind the "Untitled" placeholder, so the user
      can immediately start typing (per F-Page-Header.7)
    - the body of P' contains exactly ONE row: a pending-child
      placeholder (the faint ○ in the same indented column real
      children would occupy), per F-Pending-Child.page-root
    - typing any character into the H1 replaces the "Untitled"
      placeholder with the typed text; X.content is updated;
      the page-root pending-child remains in the body
  desc: |
    Reported 2026-05-14 with a screenshot showing an "Untitled"
    H1 in grey and a single faint bullet beneath it. Current
    behavior on this worktree: zooming into an empty node (via
    same-pane click; deck mode state not exhaustively verified
    on the smoke build) renders the H1 as a completely blank
    line and does NOT auto-focus the editor — the user sees a
    confusingly empty pane with no obvious where-to-click.
    The fix combines three pieces: (a) revising F-Page-Header.4
    to render a muted "Untitled" placeholder, (b) adding
    F-Page-Header.7 to autofocus the H1 editor on initial
    Article mount, and (c) ensuring F-Pending-Child.page-root
    fires (already in place from earlier today).
```

```mdl
test T-Zoom-Leaf-Shows-Pending-Child-In-Pane: zooming into a leaf surfaces a pending child at the top of the pane
  given: a Bramble outline contains a Node L that has no children
    (e.g. a freshly-created leaf per F-V2.12)
  when: the user shift-clicks L's bullet (F-Open-Pane.1) so a new
    pane P' opens rooted at L
  then:
    - P' renders L as the page node (H1 from `getDisplayLabel(L)`)
    - immediately below the H1 (and below any page-level field
      groups for tagged L), a faint pending-child row appears in
      the indented column where real children would render
    - the pending-child row's editable area accepts focus and
      typing exactly like the row-level pending-child of
      F-Pending-Child.type — first keystroke creates a real child
      Node (collapsed, per F-V2.12), appends it to L's children via
      a ChildEdge, and transfers focus to the new child
  when: the user clicks L's bullet (no shift) instead — F-Zoom in
    the current pane
  then:
    - the same affordance appears in the current pane's body
      (the rule is page-rendering-conditioned, not pane-conditioned)
  desc: |
    Reported 2026-05-14 alongside the F-V2.12 "new nodes are
    collapsed" change. Current behavior on this worktree (port 5188
    smoke): zooming into a leaf renders a completely blank pane
    body — the H1 shows the (empty) label, the predecessor-nav
    control shows ↑1 ▾, and the rest of the page is white space.
    There is no obvious affordance for the user to start typing.
    The fix wires a pending-child row at the top of `<Graph>`'s
    body whenever `rootBlock` has no structural children, gated
    only on `childRefs.length === 0` (not on
    `rootBlock.state.expanded`), so it appears even when the
    page-root Node was created collapsed.
```

```mdl
test T-New-Node-Collapsed: a newly created Node renders collapsed
  given: a Bramble outline with at least one bullet B containing some
    text; the bullet has no children of its own
  when: the cursor is at the end of B and the user presses Enter to
    create a new sibling C
  then:
    - C is created with `state.expanded === false` (explicitly, not
      undefined) — verifiable via `Obj.getMeta` or by rendering: C
      shows no pending-child placeholder row beneath it and, if
      tagged, no field group
    - clicking C's Expand control (chevron) toggles state.expanded to
      true; the pending-child placeholder appears as in
      F-Pending-Child.expand
  when: the user presses `#`, picks a type, applies the tag to C
  then:
    - the supertag chip renders inline on C (F-6.Phase1.chip)
    - the field group does NOT render automatically — C remains
      collapsed; the user must expand C before the field group
      surfaces (per F-V2.12 and F-6.Phase2.group's "render only when
      expanded" clause)
  desc: |
    Reported 2026-05-14 as: "given: a new node is created, when: it
    is rendered, then: the node is collapsed."
    Current behavior on this worktree (port 5188 smoke): every newly
    created Node is rendered as if `state.expanded` were true —
    `Bramble.makeNode({ content })` leaves `state` undefined, F-V2.4
    treats undefined as expanded, and so each fresh Node immediately
    shows a faint pending-child dot beneath it (and, for tagged
    Nodes, the entire FieldGroup). Visually confirmed in preview:
    the initial graph seed and an `alpha`-with-#Trigger Node both
    rendered with their pending-child placeholders and field group
    visible without any user action.
    The fix sets `state: { expanded: false }` explicitly at the four
    user-facing creation sites: the initial seed in `Graph.tsx`,
    `insertSiblingAfter` and `insertSiblingBefore` in `Node.tsx`,
    and the pending-child promote handler in `Node.tsx`. System
    Nodes (library, schema, tag wrappers, query nodes) are
    deliberately not changed.
```

```mdl
test T-Tag-Picker-Keyboard-Nav: arrow keys + Enter pick a tag without a mouse
  given: a Bramble outline with at least three registered tag-eligible
    ECHO types (e.g. Task, Person, Organization) and a Node being
    edited
  when: I type `#` to open the tag picker
  then:
    - the picker renders with the first entry highlighted (the
      keyboard cursor lands somewhere visible, not on nothing)
  when: I press ArrowDown
  then:
    - the second entry becomes the active item; the first loses its
      highlight; only one entry is highlighted at a time
  when: I press ArrowDown until I wrap past the last entry
  then:
    - the highlight wraps to the first entry (no off-by-one where the
      list "runs off the bottom" and shows nothing highlighted)
  when: I press ArrowUp from the first entry
  then:
    - the highlight wraps to the last entry
  when: I press Enter with an entry active
  then:
    - the active entry is applied (same outcome as
      `F-6.Phase1.apply`): a typed instance is created, the
      `#`-and-query range is removed from the bullet, `Node.supertags`
      gets a new ref, and the picker closes
    - the editor does NOT split the bullet (Enter while picker is
      open is consumed; pressing Enter in the same bullet immediately
      after the picker closes splits normally)
  when: I type `#t` (filtering to fewer matches) then ArrowDown then Enter
  then:
    - the highlight starts on the first filtered match (it was reset
      when the list narrowed), advances to the second, and Enter
      commits the second filtered match — not whichever entry was
      active before the filter narrowed
  when: I hover the mouse over a different entry while the picker is open
  then:
    - hovering sets the active item to the hovered entry (single
      shared highlight); pressing Enter after the hover commits the
      hovered entry
  desc: |
    Quality-of-life polish that makes the picker fully usable from
    the keyboard. Before this change the picker comments explicitly
    deferred keyboard nav to a polish increment ("keyboard
    navigation can land in a polish increment"); Enter while the
    tag picker was open also split the bullet because the editor's
    `enterCommand` only checked the mention plugin state, not the
    hash plugin state. Both gaps are closed here.
```

```mdl
