## Remaining

Captured here so the next session can resume without re-reading every commit.

### How to resume the smoke test

The user is remote and verifies via screenshots. To re-run the dev server on
a stable port for an attached preview:

1. Add a temporary entry to `.claude/launch.json` (do NOT commit):

   ```json
   {
     "name": "composer-app-smoke",
     "runtimeExecutable": "pnpm",
     "runtimeArgs": ["--filter", "@dxos/composer-app", "exec", "vite", "dev", "--port", "5188", "--strictPort"],
     "port": 5188
   }
   ```

2. From the worktree (`.claude/worktrees/agitated-nash-bd0b0f`), start it via the
   preview tool: `preview_start` with that entry name. Wait for "ready in".
3. `preview_screenshot` after each interaction; share screenshots with user.
4. When the verification is signed off, REVERT `.claude/launch.json` (the smoke
   entry must not be committed — `launch.json` is a tracked file).

Worktree path:  `.claude/worktrees/agitated-nash-bd0b0f`
Branch:         `claude/agitated-nash-bd0b0f`

### Future increments (not started)

- F-6 Phase 3 — clicking a supertag chip opens the linked instance in
  a popover (or new pane via F-Open-Pane). Array and nested-struct
  field editing inside the Phase 2 group (currently rendered as
  muted placeholders).
- F-7 Tana Paste import — parse a payload starting with `%%tana%%` into
  Nodes + TagDefs, preserving structure, supertags, fields, refs, and
  dates via a stable-id translation table. Schema is already a faithful
  import target; what's missing is the parser.

### Pieces flagged but not yet spec'd

- Concurrent-mount race in the per-space system-block
  helpers (`findOrCreateSchemaBlock`,
  `findOrCreateLibraryBlock`). Both currently lack the
  per-(db, key) lock that `ensureAllSupertagNodes` /
  `useTagBlock` already use. When two Article panes
  mount on the same tick (or React Strict Mode double-
  invokes one mount effect), both helpers can return
  before either's `db.add` is observable in the
  schemaRegistry's query cache — both check, both see
  nothing, both create. Result: duplicate `systemNode:
  'schema'` Nodes or `systemNode: 'library'` Nodes per
  space. Fix: thread the same `acquireLock(db, systemNode)`
  pattern through both helpers so concurrent calls
  serialise. No backwards-compat / cleanup required — the
  user's intent is to fix the race so greenfield instances
  never accumulate duplicates; existing duplicates in test
  spaces are historical noise and can be discarded with
  those spaces.
- Component blocks for the rest of the F-Supertag UI:
  `OpenInstanceControl` (the small icon button rendered
  adjacent to each TagChip; activating it opens the typed
  instance's Composer article surface per
  F-Supertag.open-instance-control), `SupertagNode` (the
  supertag node as a page node — H1 header sourced from the
  node's renameable content, with a query-child body listing
  every instance of the type), and `PredecessorNav` (the
  page-top control introduced by F-DAG.Phase3e.predecessor-nav-*
  that lists every predecessor of the current page node).
  `TagChip` is already in `## Components`; the others are
  referenced from tests and reqs but never had their own
  component block authored.
- `add object label` translation key shows raw on hover for the create-Node-Outline affordance.
- BacklinksPanel reactivity to a *source* Node's label rename — counts and refs update reactively, but the panel's "source label" column hasn't been verified for live rename propagation. Likely already works via Obj.subscribe.
- Editor's Storybook story (referenced as standard practice but not yet authored).
- **Upstream `@dxos/react-ui-search` + `@dxos/react-ui-list` defects
  affecting the create-object dialog when launching Bramble** (see
  `test T-Create-Filter-And-Pick` desc for the full diagnosis):
  - `useSearchListResults` resets its query when `items` arrives with
    a new array identity → typing in the type-search input is inert.
  - `Picker.Item` ships a low-contrast `:hover` background AND keeps
    mouse hover decoupled from the keyboard cursor → users see no
    visible highlight under the mouse.
  - Fixes belong in `react-ui-search` and `react-ui-list` (each
    affects every consumer of those primitives, not just Bramble);
    out of scope for this plugin. Workaround for users today: scroll
    + click directly.
- **Per-Node supertag-type uniqueness (write-time enforcement).**
  Flagged 2026-05-15 during F-New-Run-On-Step verification: nothing
  currently prevents a single Node from carrying multiple supertag
  Refs whose instances share the same typename (e.g. `[#Step, #Step]`
  on one Node). Substrate-principles "Steps nest" via children, not
  via multiple typings on one Node — same logic applies to every
  supertag class (a Node is one Run, not two; one Person, not two).

  Existing `F-Supertag.uniqueness` enforces the *complementary*
  invariant — one Node per (instance, typename) — but not this one
  (one (typename) per Node). `T-Supertag-uniq-sweep` already
  collapses duplicates at mount via normalisation, so the corrupted
  state shown in the F-Step screenshots (two `#Step` chips on one
  bullet) self-heals on next mount. But write-time prevention is
  still missing.

  Future fix: a small guard in the F-Supertag apply path
  (`Editor.handleSelectTag`) and in any other supertag-write helper
  (`createRunOfStep`, future `applySupertag`) that rejects (or
  silently drops) an apply when a Ref to an instance of the same
  typename already exists in `node.supertags`. Then promote
  `R-Supertag-Type-Uniqueness` to a real rule citing both
  invariants (instance-uniqueness AND typename-uniqueness).
- **R-Consolidation-Scrub one-time audit sweep.** The rule
  itself is operative for all future commits (see `## Rules`).
  The audit covers existing leftovers from prior
  consolidations on this branch: `F-V6`,
  `F-6.Phase3.tag-node.materialize`,
  `F-6.Phase3.tag-node.click`,
  `F-6.Phase3.tag-node.shift-click`,
  `F-6.Phase3.tag-node.schema-parent`,
  `F-6.Phase3.all-echo-types`, the `tag block` → `supertag
  node` vocabulary rename. Sweep PLUGIN.mdl, CONCEPTS.md,
  `src/**`, README, and the agent memory file; remove every
  in-scope mention of the retired names. The rule body's
  Concrete examples cite two real-world confabulations that
  the in-scope shadows generated; the audit removes the
  shadow-sources.
- **Bramble sidebar section still collapsed on first creation
  (flagged 2026-05-16, fix attempt landed but didn't take).**
  Spec'd as `F-Bramble-Nav.section-expands-on-bramble-creation`
  (+ acceptance test). Initial implementation:
  BramblePlugin.createObject explicitly calls
  `NavTreeCapabilities.State.setItem(['root', spaceId,
  'org.dxos.plugin.bramble.section'], 'open', true)` after
  find-or-create + ensureDayNodeForDate. Smoke test post-commit
  `5082df3cf7` confirmed the section is STILL collapsed on first
  appearance. Diagnosis (next session): the path the navtree
  uses for the section is probably different from `['root',
  spaceId, '<section-id>']` — could be wrapped (e.g. nested
  under a space-tree-root id), could include linked-segment
  prefixes, OR the setItem fires before the section's node
  exists in the navtree graph so the open-state isn't read at
  render. To debug: log `getItem(...)` for paths that include
  the section id (probably observable via the existing
  NavTreeCapabilities.State atoms) to find the actual path the
  navtree uses, then setItem that path. Also consider invoking
  `LayoutOperation.Expose` with a subject derived from the
  navtree's path-to-section rather than mutating state directly
  — Expose handles the "expand path" walk for us.
- **Hide Bramble.Node from the generic per-typename navigator
  listing (flagged 2026-05-16, deferred).** Surfaced during the
  F-No-Root smoke test: the just-created today-Node appeared
  under "Database / org.dxos.type.bramble.node / All" labeled
  "New item" — a confusing double-listing alongside the proper
  Bramble nav section (the today-Node is the same object
  reached via Today). Bramble.Graph and Day are already
  hidden via SystemTypeAnnotation; the same treatment for
  Bramble.Node would resolve this. Out of scope question:
  whether to also hide Step / Run / other Bramble-defined
  typed-instance types — those are user-facing entities
  reachable via supertag pages, so the secondary per-typename
  listing might still be useful. Not addressing now; user
  asked to defer.
- **Scrub "page" / "pageNode" vocabulary from spec + code
  (flagged 2026-05-16).** "Page" is a rendering metaphor, not a
  substrate noun — a pane shows a Node; the Node is the
  substrate. Reify-as-entity is the failure mode (e.g.
  `pageNode`, "the page Node", "page-block"). Sweep PLUGIN.mdl
  feats (F-Page-Header, F-Pending-Child, F-Caret, F-Headline,
  several `## Acceptance` tests reference "page Node"), then
  source comments / identifiers. Rename to "focused Node" /
  "Node in focus." F-Page-Header's NAME is fine — it's a UI
  element (an H1 row); only entity-flavoured uses need
  scrubbing. Surfaced 2026-05-16 during F-No-Root design,
  alongside the more sweeping "Bramble is just a marker, the
  DAG has no designated root" reframe.
- **Reconcile DXOS capabilities × what Bramble has built ×
  what substrate-principles describes (flagged 2026-05-16).**
  Three-way audit:

  1. Survey the public surface of `@dxos/echo`,
     `@dxos/echo-db`, `@dxos/app-toolkit`, and adjacent core
     packages — what primitives exist that Bramble could be
     using but is currently rebuilding? (F-PDF-Upload's
     attachment-vs-Wnfs.File duplication and F-Versioning's
     COW-sidecar-vs-Automerge-history duplication were both
     instances of "Bramble didn't know the upstream capability
     existed.")
  2. Map substrate-principles doc's demands (Steps, Runs,
     Contexts, Artifacts, Refinement, Executor, Engagement,
     Translator, Demand, Provenance, merge / split / non-
     destructive deletion) against what Bramble has shipped vs
     what's spec'd vs what's missing entirely.
  3. Cross the two maps. For each substrate-principles demand:
     does an existing DXOS capability already provide the
     mechanism? If yes, the Bramble feat should compose it
     (not rebuild it). If no, it's a real Bramble-layer build.

  Why: avoid the recurring duplication anti-pattern. A
  finding pass before any new substrate-vocabulary feat saves
  rewriting it later. Output shape probably a section in
  CONCEPTS.md (parallel to §12.6 "Implications for next spec
  increments") with one row per substrate demand mapping it
  to ECHO capability + Bramble status + gap.

